22680:## 2026-09-25 �?" GXEMUL [087a90]: `#549` fbctrlportkill; what its review files
22681:
22682:Evidence: `regress/artifacts/fbctrlportkill_20260924/` -- the panels, `opus5_*.md` / `opus549_measured/` (the width x
22683:offset walk; F2's prototype and two-machine run), `fable5_*.md`, kill tables `table1/`, `table2/` and `table3/`.
22684:
22685:### `fbctrlcmdkill` (HIGH, device) �?" dev_fbctrl.c: any command other than 0-2 ends the process, including the documented FILL and COPY
22686:
22687:`fbctrl_command()`'s `default:` calls `exit(1)` for every command value other than NOP (0), SET_RESOLUTION (1) and
22688:GET_RESOLUTION (2) -- reached by a guest DATA write while the COMMAND port (0) is selected.  `dev_fb.h` DOCUMENTS
22689:`DEV_FBCTRL_COMMAND_FILL` (3) and `COPY` (4); the source marks them `TODO`.  Reachable on every test machine
22690:(testmips, testarm, testm88k, testppc, testriscv, testsh).  **#549 made one path to it more direct:** the power-on
22691:selector is 0, and #549 keeps the previous selector on a refused write, so "refused selector, then a DATA write of an
22692:unknown command" now lands here (the Opus seat's walk, 4 cells).  Design question for its round: decline + latched
22693:announce for unknown commands, and decline or implement FILL/COPY (implementing is a feature; the owner's scope is fixes).
22694:The detector's session C already drives COMMAND (GET_RESOLUTION); "survives command N" is a new oracle.  The DATA path's
22695:width handling (a narrowed read or write of a port cell) belongs to this round too: #549's contract discloses it.
22696:
22697:### `fbctrloffkill` (MED, device) �?" dev_fbctrl.c: any access at an offset other than 0x00/0x10 ends the process
22698:
22699:`DEVICE_ACCESS(fbctrl)`'s `default:` calls `exit(1)` for every offset in the 0x20-byte window other than PORT (0x00)
22700:and DATA (0x10) -- e.g. a 1-byte access to +0x03.  The Opus seat's walk measured 52 such cells on the fix build.  An
22701:unmapped-offset policy (decline + announce, or a bus error where the CPU delivers one) is the shape every device in the
22702:tree faces, so its round should grep the tree's precedent first.
22703:
22704:### `fbctrltwoinst` (LOW, detector) �?" #549's per-device latch is untested across two fbctrl instances; the route exists and was measured
22705:
22706:#549's detector runs one `-E testmips` machine, so one fbctrl: a process-global latch (`M_static`) and an announce
22707:conditioned on `serial_nr == 1` (`T_D9c_serial1`) both score 73/73 there (kill table 3, disclosed in the contract).
22708:**A second instance IS reachable without adding a device**: `gxemul -V @cfg` with two `machine(type("testmips") ...)`
