1982:> switch, the visible shape invited a per-case-return idiom, and four independent readers
1983:> produced the same wrong line. **When every reader makes the same mistake, suspect the brief.**
1984:>
1985:> Corollary, measured the same day and worth its own sentence: **a handler's RETURN VALUE is
1986:> itself a contract** (`return 1` = handled). What a `0` does to the caller was checked by
1987:> nobody in that entire exchange -- not the three seats, not the adjudicator, not me. An edit
1988:> that changes a return path owes that check.
1989:
1990:> ### *** THE THREE-MEMBERED OPTION SET -- adopted 2026-08-25, after a round chose between two
1991:> ### options while the third was already shipping ONE FILE UP ITS OWN CALL PATH. ***
1992:>
1993:> `#463` de-fatalised a device and framed the choice as *keep `fatal()` unlatched* versus
1994:> *delete it*. It deleted, and recorded honestly that this cost the unconditional diagnostic.
1995:> **But `src/cpus/memory_rw.c:495-523` -- in the file that DISPATCHES to that very device --
1996:> already bounds a guest-drivable diagnostic to 8 emissions via `n_trunc_msgs`**, reasoning that
1997:> *"probing at the end of a device's mapped range is common and benign."* Same phenomenon, and
1998:> the fork's own answer was BOUND THE COUNT, KEEP THE MESSAGE.
1999:>
2000:> Worse: **this carrier already said so.** *"A guest-drivable `fatal()` needs a latch and not a
2001:> verbosity flag"* was standing rule before that round, and the two-membered framing never
2002:> consulted it.
2003:>
2004:> > *** THE SET HAS SIX MEMBERS, NOT THREE.  Grown twice, and BOTH TIMES BY A SEAT THAT WENT
2005:> > LOOKING IN THE TREE RATHER THAN REASONING -- which is the instruction below working. ***
2006:> >
2007:> > **1 DELETE  A�  2 DECLINE (silent)  A�  3 DECLINE + BOUNDED (LATCHED) ANNOUNCE  A�
2008:> > 4 ACCEPT  A�  5 FAULT  A�  6 BACKED ACCEPT  A�  7 IMPLEMENT**
2009:>
2010:> > ### *** GROWN A THIRD TIME, 2026-09-06, AND AGAIN BY A SEAT THAT WENT LOOKING IN THE TREE. ***
2011:> >
2012:> > * **7 IMPLEMENT** -- write the code.  **All six members above presume the access is a REFUSAL DECISION**,
2013:> >   and that presumption fails for an UNIMPLEMENTED PATH.  `dev_lca.c`'s `lca_interrupt_assert` and
2014:> >   `lca_interrupt_deassert` are bare `fatal("...TODO"); exit(1)` with **no guard at all**: there is no value
2015:> >   being refused and nothing to decline, accept or fault.  In the codex seat's words, *"declining an
2016:> >   interrupt leaves the machine incomplete."*  The tell that member 7 is the right one is a site with NO
2017:> >   PREDICATE -- a whole path stubbed out, rather than a guard rejecting a value.
2018:> >
2019:> > *** AND THE SAME PASS FOUND THAT **MEMBER 5 IS NOT UNIVERSALLY AVAILABLE**, which no earlier statement of
2020:> > this set said. ***  FAULT works because `memory_rw.c` dispatches an exception on a failed device access --
2021:> > **for MIPS and M88K.  There is no Alpha equivalent.**  So `dev_unreadable.c`, which `machine_sgi.c`
2022:> > installs ten times, does not transfer to an Alpha machine at all, and a round reaching for FAULT there owes
2023:> > Alpha exception delivery first.
2024:> >
2025:> > > **BEFORE CHOOSING A MEMBER, CHECK IT IS AVAILABLE ON THIS CPU.**  The set is a menu of dispositions, not
2026:> > > a menu of things every machine can do -- and the option-set rule's own advice (grep the fork for the
2027:> > > idiom) finds the idiom without telling you whether the machinery under it exists on your target.  That is
2028:> > > the warrant-transfer rule one level down: a precedent real elsewhere in the tree is not thereby
2810:> **THE RULE IS UNCHANGED AND THE EXEMPLAR IS WEAKER.** Rung still measures reachability-proof
2811:> strength and not harm; `armbdt` still corrupts guest data and still outranks `b120r`. What
2812:> changes is that the contrast is severity-versus-*small* severity rather than
2813:> severity-versus-nothing -- so this pair no longer makes the point as sharply as it reads, and
2814:> a future round wanting a clean illustration should find a better one rather than lean on this.
2815:>
2816:> *This is the anchor rule's lesson recurring: a rule minted from ONE instance carries that
2817:> instance's shape as if it were the class, and **a freshly minted rule's exemplar is a
2818:> hypothesis**. The instruction survived contact; the example did not.*
2819:**a witness makes an item ELIGIBLE; SEVERITY and gate blind-spots order the queue.** Rung
2820:is a floor on admissibility, never a priority. Strongest witness today �?" boot > probe >
2821:construction **"No witness of either kind" is a CHECKABLE claim and must be checked, not
2822:asserted.**
2823:
2824:> #### *** THE EXEMPLAR THAT USED TO SIT HERE WAS WRONG, corrected 2026-08-26. The RULE is
2825:> #### untouched -- it is one of the most useful lines in this file -- but `dfreq` is not an
2826:> #### instance of it. ***
2827:>
2828:> This clause read *"dfreq's was measured false by building the witness in one session."*
2829:> Checked against the row and the probe: **`dfreq`'s `witness` field is `None`.** Its closure
2830:> names *"the MIPS compare write ... converts the distance to a deadline into a frequency"*,
2831:> i.e. MIPS CP0 Compare -- while the row mentions ARM Footbridge four times and MIPS CP0 not
2832:> once. **The probe that was actually built (`footbridge_sites_probe.py:111`) execs `-E cats`,
2833:> an ARM/SA110 machine that executes no MIPS instruction and cannot reach CP0 Compare.** It
2834:> returned entirely CORRECT values, and its own cited line has since gone stale
2835:> (`dev_footbridge.c:548` is `console_putchar`; the timer mask is at `:640`).
2836:>
2837:> So a witness was built and it witnesses a DIFFERENT PATH from the one the row's closure
2838:> defines. The row is internally conflated, which is the finding -- not the rule's failure.
2839:> Found by the codex seat answering `dfreq`'s held stage; every element re-verified here
2840:> before the edit.
2841:>
2842:> ### *** AND THIS IS THE THIRD EXEMPLAR IN THIS FILE TO FAIL IN A SINGLE DAY WHILE ITS RULE
2843:> ### HELD. That ratio is itself a finding about this file. ***
2844:>
2845:> The anchor rule's `mask9 rt` ("anchored to a cell, not to a neighbour by distance" -- it is
2846:> anchored by distance); `b120r`'s "MEASURED symptom of NIL" (NIL for the pair compared, and
2847:> `told-0` flips control flow); and now `dfreq`. **Three rules, three intact instructions,
2848:> three broken examples.**
2849:>
2850:> The reason is structural rather than careless. **A rule is a claim about a CLASS and ages
2851:> well; an exemplar is a frozen claim about ONE INSTANCE, and instances move** -- code is
2852:> edited, rows are re-scoped, line numbers drift, and a row can be silently conflated with a
2853:> second defect. Nothing re-checks an example once it is written down, because it reads as
2854:> illustration rather than as an assertion.
2855:>
2856:> **So: an exemplar in this file is a DATED MEASUREMENT wearing the clothes of an
2857:> illustration.** When a round leans on one, re-derive it rather than cite it -- and if it has
2858:> decayed, correct it in place and keep the rule. All three today were caught that way, and in
2859:> all three the instruction survived contact while the example did not.
2860:
2861:**Round scope (the reopening rule):** a witness makes a site ELIGIBLE, never mandatory. A round
2862:takes ONE site; a second joins only if it is witnessed, in the SAME device and SAME semantic class
2863:under the third doctrine arm (so one fix design covers both), and covered by the SAME detector
2864:with no new oracle. **More witnesses create more candidate ROUNDS, never bigger rounds.**
2865:
2866:### *** TWO CORRECTIONS TO THIS LADDER, 2026-08-26, from `reprowitness`'s held stage. The
2867:### ladder failed its OWN exemplar, and its own checker had already written down the second gap. ***
2868:
2869:**(a) CLAUSE (ii) IS SCOPED TO ACCESS WITNESSES, RUNGS 3�?"4 �?" it was written as rung-2�?"4 and is
2870:UNSATISFIABLE AT RUNG 2.** Rung 2 admits machine construction for init-time defects and names the
2871:macppc heap OOB (#23). A construction-time OOB **performs no address decode and returns no device
2872:value**, so it cannot carry a control of the form "a known value returns through the same decode".
2873:The ladder's own exemplar could not satisfy the ladder's own clause, and had not been able to
2874:since the day both were written.
2875:
2876:> **A RUNG-2 WITNESS OWES CONSTRUCTION-SPECIFIC CONTROLS INSTEAD, and they are the same idea
2877:> aimed at a different channel:** a LIVENESS control that the construction under test actually
2878:> ran (the gate's own object count, or the machine type appearing in its list), and an
2879:> ATTRIBUTION control that the observed failure came from THIS construction rather than from the
2880:> harness or a neighbour (rebuild it alone; show the symptom follows the machine type). Same
2881:> two-control discipline; different observable, because there is no decode to route through.
