C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\fablequeue8n_20261009\regress_harness\fx_regresshold\ledger.json:1:{"project": "GXemul 0.7.0 hardened fork", "flagship": "fable5", "updated": "2026-10-02", "language": "ASD-STE100 unless a technical name is necessary (owner directive 2026-08-15)", "battery": {"verdict": "REGRESS_PASS", "coverage": "16/16", "tally": "passed 16  failed 0  skipped 0", "head": "7580e2f", "when": "2026-08-15 03:00-03:43, unattended", "evidence": "_scratchpad/nightly/verdict_20260815_030002.txt", "note": "Full battery: Sundays 18:00 (moved from Saturday 03:00 so it runs BEFORE the Sunday-night regression batch). Next: 2026-08-16 18:00. Small unit tests and targeted gates run at any time."}, "seats": [{"id": "fable5", "name": "Fable 5", "ver": "max", "cls": "FR", "note": "FLAGSHIP. This seat is the main loop and the adjudicator. It is the session model (from the 2026-08-15 login). The seat is serviceable: after the login, the echo-test sent back the two nonces. Before the login, six tests failed because of the quota. The login made the seat serviceable. The date did not. The Kimi seat came back the same way.", "model": "claude-fable-5"}, {"id": "opus5", "name": "Opus 5", "ver": "1M", "cls": "FR", "note": "The measure seat. It compiles the code and does the tests. More than 10 times, its measurements showed that the main loop was not correct. It has the same model family as the main loop before the login. Thus its approvals of that loop's work show the s badge.", "model": "claude-opus-5"}, {"id": "codex", "name": "Codex 5.6-SOL", "ver": "xhigh", "cls": "FR", "note": "A CLI seat that can read the repository. When its session is read-only, the seat says so. Then its report is a READING, not a measurement.", "model": "gpt-5.6-sol"}, {"id": "agy", "name": "agy 3.7", "ver": "high", "cls": "FR", "note": "The model gemini-3.7-flash-high. It reads files through --add-dir.", "model": "gemini-3.7-flash-high"}, {"id": "grok", "name": "Grok 4.6", "ver": "xhigh", "cls": "FR", "note": "This seat uses --prompt-file. Thus the 32 KB argument limit does not apply to it.", "model": "grok-4.6"}, {"id": "kimi", "name": "Kimi 3", "ver": "k3", "cls": "FR+M", "note": "This seat can read files and can do tests with its own probes. On 2026-08-15 it did the adjudication alone. The FABLE/KIMI directive permits this.", "model": "kimi-code/k3"}, {"id": "glm", "name": "glm-5.2", "ver": "cloud", "cls": "PK", "note": "A packet-fed seat. It gave the honest report: 'no files read; every line citation is the prompt's, re-stated'.", "model": "glm-5.2:cloud"}, {"id": "deepseek", "name": "deepseek-v4-pro", "ver": "cloud", "cls": "PK", "note": "A packet-fed seat. If the brief says 'read the source', the seat stops. That was a defect in the brief. For days, the record showed it incorrectly as a seat failure.", "model": "deepseek-v4-pro:cloud"}, {"id": "minimax", "name": "minimax-m3", "ver": "cloud", "cls": "PK", "note": "A packet-fed seat. The record showed it as unserviceable for days. When a brief contained all the necessary facts, the seat gave a 12 KB answer.", "model": "minimax-m3:cloud"}], "phase_labels": {"assess": "Assessment (initial problem)", "research": "Research (fix proposals)", "review": "Final review (flagship adjudication)", "regress": "Regression testing"}, "state_semantics": {"_ADOPTED": "*** RATIFIED BY OWNER DECISION 2026-08-25, WITH BOTH AMENDMENTS. *** No longer pending. Amendment 1 (the missing `open` definition) is applied above. Amendment 2 (model the main-loop hat on `closure.by`, with roster validation) is ratified in the same decision and is tracked as `closurebyroster` -- ratified, not deferred. The retroactive-meaning / forward-only-enforcement split below is ratified as written.\n\n  *** THE ROW NAMED HERE, `closurebyroster`, HAS NEVER EXISTED IN THIS LEDGER. Found 2026-08-29 by following the cross-reference. ***  The sentence above ends \"an amendment ratified in one place and tracked in another is how an obligation goes quiet\" -- and it was attached to an obligation that had gone quiet, for exactly that reason.  THE REAL CARRIER IS `ledgerclosureby`, whose title is \"closure.by holds the WRONG KIND of value on two rows, and a role distinction THE ROSTER DOES NOT MODEL on ten more\" -- i.e. it already covers both halves of this amendment (the main-loop hat and roster validation).  What it did NOT carry was any record that it is discharging an OWNER-RATIFIED amendment rather than an ordinary records nit, which is the difference between an obligation and a tidy-up.  No new row was created: inventing `closurebyroster` would have split one obligation across two rows, which is the defect this sentence warns about, committed a second time to fix its first instance.", "_WHY": "The ledger's schema had NO WRITTEN SEMANTICS. That single absence is the cause behind six mis-counts in one session, two file-reading seats reading OPPOSITE conventions into one closure string, and five seats agreeing on only 4 of 11 rows they were asked to state-check. A field every round ranks and closes from was never defined.", "_THE_QUESTION_state_ANSWERS": "Is anything still owed on this row, and who acts next?", "_TENSE": "*** `state` IS PRESENT-TENSE; `closure.note` IS PAST-TENSE. *** The note is an EVENT LOG of what happened to a STAGE ('CLOSE (assess)' closes the assess stage). `state` asserts what the ROW still owes. **They are independent BY DESIGN** -- 23 rows share the string `CLOSE (assess)` across three states, and that is the ledger working correctly, not a defect: rows that experienced the same stage event legitimately differ in what else they owe. NEITHER can be derived from the other, and both attempts to do so have been measured wrong.", "closed": "Nothing outstanding; the claim was discharged BY WORK. Mechanical companions, era-scoped: commits, closure.by, and witness if closed on/after 2026-08-19.", "dropped": "Nothing outstanding; the claim was retired WITHOUT work, with the reason. Confusing this with `closed` is the es438 class -- both mean 'nothing owed', and misstating WHY is what misleads a future reader.", "held": "Something outstanding, waiting on a NAMED party or event, and carrying the QUESTION it is held for (standing rule). `open`/`held` WITH commits is normal here.", "in work": "Something outstanding, actively claimed.", "_NOT_DERIVABLE_FROM": "closure.note, entries, or stage coverage. Establishing a row's state requires a SUBSTANCE READ -- commits, gates, holds, filed questions -- which is what `asicbadaddr` was closed from and is the template.", "open": "Something outstanding, UNCLAIMED -- the complement of `in work`'s 'actively claimed'. No named party is required (that is `held`) and no work is under way. *** THIS DEFINITION WAS MISSING FROM THE BLOCK UNTIL 2026-08-25, AND `open` IS THE SECOND-LARGEST STATE IN THE LEDGER (46 of 216 rows). *** It appeared only inside `held`'s prose. Found by the fable5 adjudicator when asked to ratify the block -- it was told plainly that ratification was not wanted and to check the premises, and the gap is what that instruction bought. A checker built on the block as written would have misclassified a fifth of the ledger while reporting full coverage, which is this project's own 'a detector built from the wrong census reports full coverage' class wearing schema clothes.", "_RETROACTIVE_OR_FORWARD_ONLY": "*** THE FIRST DRAFT SAID FORWARD-ONLY FLAT, AND THAT WAS HALF RIGHT IN THE LOAD-BEARING HALF. *** The split is by LAYER, and the block's own _TENSE ruling decides it: **the MEANING binds retroactively; only MECHANICAL CHECKS are forward-only by date.** `state` is PRESENT-TENSE, and a present-tense field has no era -- a false state is false NOW. 'Pre-adoption rows are read charitably' would preserve the exact defect this block exists to end (the es438 reader reads 'not done' off shipped, witnessed work TODAY) and would keep the ledger permanently bilingual. Forward-only enforcement follows the boilerplate-check precedent: a rule that retro-fails the archive gets disabled, and a disabled rule protects nothing. *** THE DISHONESTY WORRY DISSOLVES ONCE THE LAYERS ARE SEPARATED: `state` is a LIVE FIELD, NOT HISTORY. *** Correcting it rewrites no history -- `entries`, notes and `commits` are untouched. The migration is the warranted in-place repair already used twice on 2026-08-25: correct the state cell and attach a warrant note naming the row's OWN evidence. NO BULK REWRITE -- the round's central finding stands (state is NOT derivable; five seats agreed on only 4 of 11 rows), so every remaining row gets a substance read.", "_AMENDMENT_2": "RATIFIED 2026-08-25 in the same owner decision, tracked as `closurebyroster`: model the main-loop hat on `closure.by` plus roster validation. Recorded here rather than only in that row so ratification's SCOPE is legible from the block it ratified -- an amendment ratified in one place and tracked in another is how an obligation goes quiet.\n\n  *** THE ROW NAMED HERE, `closurebyroster`, HAS NEVER EXISTED IN THIS LEDGER. Found 2026-08-29 by following the cross-reference. ***  The sentence above ends \"an amendment ratified in one place and tracked in another is how an obligation goes quiet\" -- and it was attached to an obligation that had gone quiet, for exactly that reason.  THE REAL CARRIER IS `ledgerclosureby`, whose title is \"closure.by holds the WRONG KIND of value on two rows, and a role distinction THE ROSTER DOES NOT MODEL on ten more\" -- i.e. it already covers both halves of this amendment (the main-loop hat and roster validation).  What it did NOT carry was any record that it is discharging an OWNER-RATIFIED amendment rather than an ordinary records nit, which is the difference between an obligation and a tidy-up.  No new row was created: inventing `closurebyroster` would have split one obligation across two rows, which is the defect this sentence warns about, committed a second time to fix its first instance."}, "rows": [{"id": "fx_regheld", "title": "sh4sqwrite  The SQ WRITE half is never judged -- so an SQ page can raise no initial-page-write and no write-side protection fault, while the PREF still raises the read-side set", "state": "closed", "commits": "abc1234", "hold": "", "witness": "none yet -- GUEST-REACHABLE and witnessable at rung 3 (a real guest store through real decode, observable in EXPEVT), but a witness needs a build, which the machine embargo forbids.", "closure": {"by": "", "badge": "", "note": ""}, "entries": [{"seat": "fable5", "phase": "regress", "date": "2026-09-19", "note": "[HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5] the regress review is owed", "ev": "", "d": "HELD"}], "state_note": "*** SCOPE CORRECTION 2026-08-27: THE DEFECT IS THE **WRITE HALF ONLY**, and saying the window 'bypasses the MMU ENTIRELY' reintroduces wording this row's title was already corrected away from. ***\n\n**Why the unqualified form is wrong:** the round's own `sq miss evt` detector observes a **read-type TLB miss (`0x040`)** on the fixed build, so the read side is NOT unrouted. `memory_sh.c:290-301` carries the corrected scope.\n\n> **THE CORRECT STATEMENT: no initial-page-write exception and no write-side protection check on stores into the SQ window.** The read/prefetch path translates; the store path does not. That asymmetry IS the defect, and it is narrower and more defensible than 'bypasses the MMU'.\n\n**AND THE SECTION CITATIONS ARE BOTH RIGHT, FOR DIFFERENT THINGS -- reconciled here so the next reader does not have to choose:**\n  * **section 3.6.7** *defines* the Initial Page Write exception and its code: *\"occurs when the D bit is 0 even though a UTLB entry contains address translation information matching the virtual address to which a data access (write) is made... Sets exception code H'080 in EXPEVT.\"* (`sh7750_hardware.txt:9918-9928`)\n  * **section 4.7.4, 'SQ Protection'** *applies* it to the store queues: *\"Write type exception judgment is performed for writes to the SQs... a TLB miss exception, protection violation exception, or initial page write exception is generated.\"* (`:11742-11748`)\n**The code cites 4.7.4; I cited 3.6.7. Cite BOTH -- 4.7.4 is the one that makes it apply to this window, and 3.6.7 is the one that says what the exception is.**\n\n**Ranking, from today's adjudication: 7th of 8.** Latent -- no known guest depends on SQ write-protection. *It is a fidelity gap, not a live corruption, and my earlier 'most severe row in the batch' was severity within that batch, not across the queue.*\n\n*** INDEPENDENTLY RE-VERIFIED 2026-08-27, and the step that could have KILLED it -- whether SH-4 exempts the store queue from translation -- goes the OTHER WAY. The manual refutes the exemption. (Already documented in-source and filed; today's work is re-verification.) ***\n\n**THE BYPASS IS UNCONDITIONAL.** `memory_sh.c:353-357`:\n```c\n/*  Store queue region:  */\nif (vaddr >= 0xe0000000 && vaddr < 0xe4000000) {\n        /*  Note/TODO: Take SH4_MMUCR_SQMD into account.  */\n        *return_paddr = vaddr;\n        return 2;\n}\n```\n**No `MMUCR.AT` test, no UTLB walk, no PR/D check**, and it returns 2 (read/write). `cpu_sh.c:106` is the only assignment of `translate_v2p`, and the sole SH consumer of `0xe0000000` in `src/cpus/` is `pref_rn` -- **no store instruction special-cases the window**, so every `mov.l Rm,@Rn` into it takes this branch. The preceding arm (`:339-350`) matches neither, so the path is identical with AT=1 and AT=0.\n\n*** THE MANUAL SETTLES IT AGAINST THE EXEMPTION, from three directions: ***\n  * **section 4.7.4, SQ Protection** (`sh7750_hardware.txt:11742-11748`): *\"When MMU is on -- Operation is in accordance with the address translation information recorded in the UTLB, and MMUCR.SQMD. **Write type exception judgment is performed for writes to the SQs** ... a TLB miss exception, protection violation exception, or **initial page write exception** is generated.\"*\n  * **section 4.7.3** (`:11691-11696`): *\"the ASID, V, SZ, SH, PR, and D bits have the same meaning as for normal address translation.\"*\n  * **QACR is explicitly the MMU-OFF-only mechanism** (`:11719-11723`, `:10473`), so it is NOT an alternative translation path under AT=1.\n\n**AND PRIVILEGE DOES NOT RESCUE IT.** The Data TLB Protection Violation table (`:12632-12640`) gives `PR=00` as **\"Only read access possible\"** in PRIVILEGED mode, with EXPEVT `0x0A0` read / `0x0C0` write at `:12650`.\n\n*** THE ASYMMETRY IS THE CLEANEST STATEMENT OF THE DEFECT: THE READ SIDE IS TRANSLATED AND THE WRITE SIDE IS NOT. *** The flush path at `cpu_sh_instr.c:3706-3765` explicitly checks `SH4_MMUCR_AT` and calls `sh_translate_sq_v2p()` -> `translate_via_mmu()`. The store path does none of it.\n\n**WHY THE SHIPPED DETECTOR CANNOT SEE THIS, and it is not a detector defect:** `regress/sh_halt_probe.py:590-599` pins `sq pr00 evt` and `sq d0 evt` to **NOEXC deliberately** -- those rows exercise the read-type PREF path, where no protection or dirty case exists. **No row exercises the STORE side at all**, so the detector is silent here rather than contradicting the finding.\n\n**ADJACENT, DISTINCT, ALREADY FILED:** the `SQMD` TODO on that same branch is a SECOND gap -- section 4.7.4 requires an ADDRESS ERROR for a user-mode SQ access when `MMUCR.SQMD = 1` even when translation succeeds, and the store path checks neither. `pref_rn` does check it (`cpu_sh_instr.c:3709-3715`) but raises `EXPEVT_RES_INST` instead -- filed as `sh4sqexpevt`.\n\n*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: TRUE and GUEST-REACHABLE. A store into the store-queue window BYPASSES THE MMU, so page protection is not enforced on it. The most severe row in this batch. ***\n\n**The mechanism:** the SQ write path does not go through `sh_translate_v2p()` / `translate_via_mmu()` (`memory_sh.c:353-357`, `:203-241`, with the source's own commentary at `:289-303`), so a store into the SQ window lands directly in the device's `sq` array.\n\n**Guest access sequence, fully specified by the seat:**\n  1. guest kernel enables paging (`MMUCR.AT = 1`);\n  2. guest maps the SQ virtual page `0xe0000000` in the UTLB **valid** (`V=1`) and either **clean** (`D=0`) or **read-only** (`PR=00`);\n  3. guest issues `mov.l r0, @r1` with `r1 = 0xe0000000`.\n\n**Expected on hardware (SH7750 sections 4.7.4 / 3.6.7):** an **Initial Page Write** exception (`EXPEVT = 0x080`) on `D=0`, or a **Data Write Protection** exception on a read-only page.\n**Actual here:** the store simply succeeds.\n\n> **So a guest that maps a page read-only, or clean-for-dirty-tracking, and then stores through the SQ window gets a write that hardware would have faulted.** That is protection and dirty-bit tracking silently not enforced -- not a fidelity nit.\n\n**Observable:** the guest `EXPEVT` register after the store -- `0x080` (or the write-protect code) when correct, unchanged when broken. Readable by an ordinary guest load, so no new probe capability is needed.\n\n**CHECKABLE AGAINST A PRIMARY SOURCE WE HAVE:** the seat cites SH7750 sections 4.7.4 and 3.6.7, and `_scratchpad/sh7750_hardware.txt` is present and greppable. **Confirm the two sections before the round** -- and read the PROSE, not a figure, per the extraction rule.\n\n\n\n*** PROMOTED BY THE CODEX RATING PASS, 2026-09-07, ON A ROW IT WAS NOT ASKED ABOUT -- and the\npromotion is the answer to the brief's Q3, *what does my reading order miss?* ***\n\n*\"Promote `sh4sqwrite` ahead of the latent ASIC proposals.  Its title sounds like missing tests,\nbut `memory_sh.c` (anchor `Store queue region:`) grants read/write access directly, and `dev_sh4.c`\n(anchor `DEVICE_ACCESS(sh4_sq)`) accepts the writes into queue storage.  Hardware requires\nwrite-type exception checks on stores into those queues.\"*  (Renesas SH7750 manual, S4.7.4.)\n\n> **THAT IS A GUEST-REACHABLE MISSING PROTECTION CHECK, not a coverage gap** -- and it was invisible\n> to my keyword scan because the TITLE reads like harness debt.  *\"No known guest depends on it\"\n> does not establish unreachability.*\n\n**And the row's existing witness cannot carry it:** `sq pr00 evt` and `sq d0 evt` in\n`regress/sh_halt_probe.py` exercise **PREF's READ-type judgement**, so they cannot witness a missing\nSTORE-side check.  A witness for this row has to drive a store.\n\n*Filed as the strongest single finding of that pass; it needs a `sev` and a round of its own.*", "note": "\n\n*** 2026-09-11 -- NOW UNBLOCKED, AND IT IS THE BIGGEST MOVE IN THE COHORT. ***\n\nBlocker verbatim: *a witness needs a build, which the machine embargo forbids*.  **Builds are routine; the embargo is gone, residue included.**\n\nThis row was **promoted 2026-09-07 by a codex rating pass on a row it was not asked about** -- *promote `sh4sqwrite` ahead of the latent ASIC proposals; hardware requires write-type exception checks on stores into those queues* -- and filed as **the strongest single finding of that pass, needing a `sev` and a round of its own.**\n\n**And it needs no new probe capability:** its own note records that the observable is *readable by an ordinary guest load*.  **So the highest-value row in the cohort was fully unblocked and still carrying a blocker that suppressed it from every ranking.**\n\nReplacement blocker text: *rung-3 witnessable -- a guest store through the SQ window, oracle EXPEVT, readable by an ordinary guest load.  No new capability and no blocker: this owes a ROUND, not a precondition.  Confirm SH7750 S4.7.4 / S3.6.7 from the PROSE, never a figure.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 ASSESS (codex GPT-6 Astra at max, 13,804 b).  **SUPPORTED** -- the first row tonight NOT refuted by its own assessment, and the finding is stronger than the filing. ***\n\n*VERDICT: SUPPORTED at the translation/device layer: SQ fills omit write-type MMU checks.  Complete instruction-path reachability and external-memory consequences remain unverified.*\n\n**`sh_translate_v2p()` handles the SQ window `0xe0000000-0xe3ffffff` by returning the original address with access result 2 (read/write available).  That branch consults NEITHER the write flag NOR MMU state, UTLB entries, PR, D, or privilege; it leaves `MMUCR.SQMD` an explicit TODO; and it RETURNS BEFORE the subsequent user-mode check** (`memory_sh.c:323-369`).  The window is registered to `dev_sh4_sq_access`, whose write branch copies bytes into the 64-byte queue with modulo indexing and returns success -- **no protection judgment, no exception dispatch** (`dev_sh4.c:2865-2867`, `:1574-1587`).\n\nBy comparison, ordinary MMU-translated U0/P0/P3 accesses enter `translate_via_mmu()`, deriving the access type from `FLAG_WRITEFLAG` and evaluating valid entries, VPN match, ASID, protection and dirty state: **PR 0/2 reject privileged writes; PR 1/3 permit writes only without an initial-write fault when D is set.**\n\n> *** THE SQ-FILL PATH OMITS THE JUDGMENT RATHER THAN WEAKENING IT.  That is the load-bearing sentence: not a weaker rule applied, but no rule applied. ***\n\n**THE MANUAL EXPRESSLY REQUIRES WRITE JUDGMENT WHEN FILLING THE QUEUES**, so this is a correctness gap rather than a defence-in-depth preference -- which is precisely the distinction that leaves `sh4itlbpr` unlicensed under the addition licence.  *These two SH rows look alike and are not.*\n\n**A SECOND FUNCTION EXISTS AND DOES NOT SUPPLY THE CHECK:** `sh_translate_sq_v2p()` (`memory_sh.c:255-318`) forwards the caller's flags straight to `translate_via_mmu()`.  It neither forces read classification nor guards the ordinary SQ branch -- **and its actual caller is outside the permitted files, so whether it is wired at all is UNVERIFIED.**\n\n**REACHABILITY IS SUPPORTED STRUCTURALLY, NOT CERTIFIED.**  The static chain is guest data access -> installed translation hook -> SQ identity mapping -> registered SQ device -> queue backing array, and all of it is guest-facing rather than debugger-only.  *The missing instruction and generic-memory implementations prevent certifying every link during actual instruction execution* -- stated as a limit rather than glossed.\n\n**THE ROW'S \"READABLE BY AN ORDINARY GUEST LOAD\" CLAIM IS CONFIRMED:** EXPEVT sits at P4 `0xFF000024`, reachable by a privileged `MOV.L @Rm,Rn`, and the device's read case returns `cpu->cd.sh.expevt`.  **So no new probe capability is needed** -- which is what made this row the cheapest high-value item in the cohort.\n\n*** BUT A LOAD FROM THE SQ APERTURE IS A DIFFERENT PROPOSITION, AND THIS IS THE CAUTION I WOULD HAVE MISSED: this emulator's SQ handler supports reads, while the manual defines architectural SQ readback ONLY for SH7750R, through a SEPARATE aperture `0xFF001000-0xFF00103C`.  \"Such emulator readback cannot silently become a universal hardware oracle.\" ***\n\n**STEPPING: SYNCHRONOUS, so a stepping probe is suitable in principle.**  The MMU failure path invokes `sh_exception()` directly with interrupt argument zero, and that function updates EXPEVT and transfers control **with no `single_step` guard**.  So the recorded suppression of ASYNCHRONOUS delivery does not disqualify this path.  **And `FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS` is a separate mechanism that must not be conflated with `single_step`** -- a distinction this project has blurred before.\n\n=== THE ORACLE, five wrong behaviours and what separates each ===\n* **missing write check** -- the exception must be ATTRIBUTABLE to the tested access, with its fault address and saved instruction address;\n* **wrong code** -- **exact EXPEVT comparison; reaching `VBR + 0x100` alone cannot distinguish protection, initial-write and several unrelated exceptions**;\n* **write judgment applied to READS** -- a permitted read keeps read semantics, including no initial-write fault merely because D is clear;\n* **faulting EVERY SQ store** -- permitted fills must complete and show their intended effect;\n* **store never executed** -- *program liveness or an unchanged EXPEVT is INSUFFICIENT*; an old matching EXPEVT value, an unrelated exception, or a read without established execution all produce a FALSE PASS.\n\n*** AND A VACUITY WARNING ABOUT A ROW THAT LOOKS SOUND: a passing READ control does NOT automatically kill a mutation at the SQ-fill guard ***, because `sh_translate_sq_v2p()` enters the common MMU helper directly and ordinary translated reads need not traverse the new guard at all.  Establishing that requires the omitted caller and the actual mutation site.\n\n**SCOPE HELD, AND IT BOUNDS THE SEVERITY HONESTLY: this does NOT establish that an SQ fill overwrites protected external RAM.**  The operation changes queue SRAM; the external transfer is a SEPARATE operation.  No privilege escalation, no host compromise, no severity ranking and no runtime reproduction are claimed.\n\n**STATUS: ready for a round.**  The defect is established at the translation and device layers, the observation register exists, the exception is synchronous, and the oracle is specified.  What is owed is the instruction-path link and the external-memory question -- both measurable."}, {"id": "fx_noregress", "title": "sh4sqwrite  The SQ WRITE half is never judged -- so an SQ page can raise no initial-page-write and no write-side protection fault, while the PREF still raises the read-side set", "state": "closed", "commits": "abc1234", "hold": "", "witness": "none yet -- GUEST-REACHABLE and witnessable at rung 3 (a real guest store through real decode, observable in EXPEVT), but a witness needs a build, which the machine embargo forbids.", "closure": {"by": "", "badge": "", "note": ""}, "entries": [{"seat": "codex", "phase": "review", "date": "2026-09-19", "note": "answer", "ev": "", "d": "✓"}], "state_note": "*** SCOPE CORRECTION 2026-08-27: THE DEFECT IS THE **WRITE HALF ONLY**, and saying the window 'bypasses the MMU ENTIRELY' reintroduces wording this row's title was already corrected away from. ***\n\n**Why the unqualified form is wrong:** the round's own `sq miss evt` detector observes a **read-type TLB miss (`0x040`)** on the fixed build, so the read side is NOT unrouted. `memory_sh.c:290-301` carries the corrected scope.\n\n> **THE CORRECT STATEMENT: no initial-page-write exception and no write-side protection check on stores into the SQ window.** The read/prefetch path translates; the store path does not. That asymmetry IS the defect, and it is narrower and more defensible than 'bypasses the MMU'.\n\n**AND THE SECTION CITATIONS ARE BOTH RIGHT, FOR DIFFERENT THINGS -- reconciled here so the next reader does not have to choose:**\n  * **section 3.6.7** *defines* the Initial Page Write exception and its code: *\"occurs when the D bit is 0 even though a UTLB entry contains address translation information matching the virtual address to which a data access (write) is made... Sets exception code H'080 in EXPEVT.\"* (`sh7750_hardware.txt:9918-9928`)\n  * **section 4.7.4, 'SQ Protection'** *applies* it to the store queues: *\"Write type exception judgment is performed for writes to the SQs... a TLB miss exception, protection violation exception, or initial page write exception is generated.\"* (`:11742-11748`)\n**The code cites 4.7.4; I cited 3.6.7. Cite BOTH -- 4.7.4 is the one that makes it apply to this window, and 3.6.7 is the one that says what the exception is.**\n\n**Ranking, from today's adjudication: 7th of 8.** Latent -- no known guest depends on SQ write-protection. *It is a fidelity gap, not a live corruption, and my earlier 'most severe row in the batch' was severity within that batch, not across the queue.*\n\n*** INDEPENDENTLY RE-VERIFIED 2026-08-27, and the step that could have KILLED it -- whether SH-4 exempts the store queue from translation -- goes the OTHER WAY. The manual refutes the exemption. (Already documented in-source and filed; today's work is re-verification.) ***\n\n**THE BYPASS IS UNCONDITIONAL.** `memory_sh.c:353-357`:\n```c\n/*  Store queue region:  */\nif (vaddr >= 0xe0000000 && vaddr < 0xe4000000) {\n        /*  Note/TODO: Take SH4_MMUCR_SQMD into account.  */\n        *return_paddr = vaddr;\n        return 2;\n}\n```\n**No `MMUCR.AT` test, no UTLB walk, no PR/D check**, and it returns 2 (read/write). `cpu_sh.c:106` is the only assignment of `translate_v2p`, and the sole SH consumer of `0xe0000000` in `src/cpus/` is `pref_rn` -- **no store instruction special-cases the window**, so every `mov.l Rm,@Rn` into it takes this branch. The preceding arm (`:339-350`) matches neither, so the path is identical with AT=1 and AT=0.\n\n*** THE MANUAL SETTLES IT AGAINST THE EXEMPTION, from three directions: ***\n  * **section 4.7.4, SQ Protection** (`sh7750_hardware.txt:11742-11748`): *\"When MMU is on -- Operation is in accordance with the address translation information recorded in the UTLB, and MMUCR.SQMD. **Write type exception judgment is performed for writes to the SQs** ... a TLB miss exception, protection violation exception, or **initial page write exception** is generated.\"*\n  * **section 4.7.3** (`:11691-11696`): *\"the ASID, V, SZ, SH, PR, and D bits have the same meaning as for normal address translation.\"*\n  * **QACR is explicitly the MMU-OFF-only mechanism** (`:11719-11723`, `:10473`), so it is NOT an alternative translation path under AT=1.\n\n**AND PRIVILEGE DOES NOT RESCUE IT.** The Data TLB Protection Violation table (`:12632-12640`) gives `PR=00` as **\"Only read access possible\"** in PRIVILEGED mode, with EXPEVT `0x0A0` read / `0x0C0` write at `:12650`.\n\n*** THE ASYMMETRY IS THE CLEANEST STATEMENT OF THE DEFECT: THE READ SIDE IS TRANSLATED AND THE WRITE SIDE IS NOT. *** The flush path at `cpu_sh_instr.c:3706-3765` explicitly checks `SH4_MMUCR_AT` and calls `sh_translate_sq_v2p()` -> `translate_via_mmu()`. The store path does none of it.\n\n**WHY THE SHIPPED DETECTOR CANNOT SEE THIS, and it is not a detector defect:** `regress/sh_halt_probe.py:590-599` pins `sq pr00 evt` and `sq d0 evt` to **NOEXC deliberately** -- those rows exercise the read-type PREF path, where no protection or dirty case exists. **No row exercises the STORE side at all**, so the detector is silent here rather than contradicting the finding.\n\n**ADJACENT, DISTINCT, ALREADY FILED:** the `SQMD` TODO on that same branch is a SECOND gap -- section 4.7.4 requires an ADDRESS ERROR for a user-mode SQ access when `MMUCR.SQMD = 1` even when translation succeeds, and the store path checks neither. `pref_rn` does check it (`cpu_sh_instr.c:3709-3715`) but raises `EXPEVT_RES_INST` instead -- filed as `sh4sqexpevt`.\n\n*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: TRUE and GUEST-REACHABLE. A store into the store-queue window BYPASSES THE MMU, so page protection is not enforced on it. The most severe row in this batch. ***\n\n**The mechanism:** the SQ write path does not go through `sh_translate_v2p()` / `translate_via_mmu()` (`memory_sh.c:353-357`, `:203-241`, with the source's own commentary at `:289-303`), so a store into the SQ window lands directly in the device's `sq` array.\n\n**Guest access sequence, fully specified by the seat:**\n  1. guest kernel enables paging (`MMUCR.AT = 1`);\n  2. guest maps the SQ virtual page `0xe0000000` in the UTLB **valid** (`V=1`) and either **clean** (`D=0`) or **read-only** (`PR=00`);\n  3. guest issues `mov.l r0, @r1` with `r1 = 0xe0000000`.\n\n**Expected on hardware (SH7750 sections 4.7.4 / 3.6.7):** an **Initial Page Write** exception (`EXPEVT = 0x080`) on `D=0`, or a **Data Write Protection** exception on a read-only page.\n**Actual here:** the store simply succeeds.\n\n> **So a guest that maps a page read-only, or clean-for-dirty-tracking, and then stores through the SQ window gets a write that hardware would have faulted.** That is protection and dirty-bit tracking silently not enforced -- not a fidelity nit.\n\n**Observable:** the guest `EXPEVT` register after the store -- `0x080` (or the write-protect code) when correct, unchanged when broken. Readable by an ordinary guest load, so no new probe capability is needed.\n\n**CHECKABLE AGAINST A PRIMARY SOURCE WE HAVE:** the seat cites SH7750 sections 4.7.4 and 3.6.7, and `_scratchpad/sh7750_hardware.txt` is present and greppable. **Confirm the two sections before the round** -- and read the PROSE, not a figure, per the extraction rule.\n\n\n\n*** PROMOTED BY THE CODEX RATING PASS, 2026-09-07, ON A ROW IT WAS NOT ASKED ABOUT -- and the\npromotion is the answer to the brief's Q3, *what does my reading order miss?* ***\n\n*\"Promote `sh4sqwrite` ahead of the latent ASIC proposals.  Its title sounds like missing tests,\nbut `memory_sh.c` (anchor `Store queue region:`) grants read/write access directly, and `dev_sh4.c`\n(anchor `DEVICE_ACCESS(sh4_sq)`) accepts the writes into queue storage.  Hardware requires\nwrite-type exception checks on stores into those queues.\"*  (Renesas SH7750 manual, S4.7.4.)\n\n> **THAT IS A GUEST-REACHABLE MISSING PROTECTION CHECK, not a coverage gap** -- and it was invisible\n> to my keyword scan because the TITLE reads like harness debt.  *\"No known guest depends on it\"\n> does not establish unreachability.*\n\n**And the row's existing witness cannot carry it:** `sq pr00 evt` and `sq d0 evt` in\n`regress/sh_halt_probe.py` exercise **PREF's READ-type judgement**, so they cannot witness a missing\nSTORE-side check.  A witness for this row has to drive a store.\n\n*Filed as the strongest single finding of that pass; it needs a `sev` and a round of its own.*", "note": "\n\n*** 2026-09-11 -- NOW UNBLOCKED, AND IT IS THE BIGGEST MOVE IN THE COHORT. ***\n\nBlocker verbatim: *a witness needs a build, which the machine embargo forbids*.  **Builds are routine; the embargo is gone, residue included.**\n\nThis row was **promoted 2026-09-07 by a codex rating pass on a row it was not asked about** -- *promote `sh4sqwrite` ahead of the latent ASIC proposals; hardware requires write-type exception checks on stores into those queues* -- and filed as **the strongest single finding of that pass, needing a `sev` and a round of its own.**\n\n**And it needs no new probe capability:** its own note records that the observable is *readable by an ordinary guest load*.  **So the highest-value row in the cohort was fully unblocked and still carrying a blocker that suppressed it from every ranking.**\n\nReplacement blocker text: *rung-3 witnessable -- a guest store through the SQ window, oracle EXPEVT, readable by an ordinary guest load.  No new capability and no blocker: this owes a ROUND, not a precondition.  Confirm SH7750 S4.7.4 / S3.6.7 from the PROSE, never a figure.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 ASSESS (codex GPT-6 Astra at max, 13,804 b).  **SUPPORTED** -- the first row tonight NOT refuted by its own assessment, and the finding is stronger than the filing. ***\n\n*VERDICT: SUPPORTED at the translation/device layer: SQ fills omit write-type MMU checks.  Complete instruction-path reachability and external-memory consequences remain unverified.*\n\n**`sh_translate_v2p()` handles the SQ window `0xe0000000-0xe3ffffff` by returning the original address with access result 2 (read/write available).  That branch consults NEITHER the write flag NOR MMU state, UTLB entries, PR, D, or privilege; it leaves `MMUCR.SQMD` an explicit TODO; and it RETURNS BEFORE the subsequent user-mode check** (`memory_sh.c:323-369`).  The window is registered to `dev_sh4_sq_access`, whose write branch copies bytes into the 64-byte queue with modulo indexing and returns success -- **no protection judgment, no exception dispatch** (`dev_sh4.c:2865-2867`, `:1574-1587`).\n\nBy comparison, ordinary MMU-translated U0/P0/P3 accesses enter `translate_via_mmu()`, deriving the access type from `FLAG_WRITEFLAG` and evaluating valid entries, VPN match, ASID, protection and dirty state: **PR 0/2 reject privileged writes; PR 1/3 permit writes only without an initial-write fault when D is set.**\n\n> *** THE SQ-FILL PATH OMITS THE JUDGMENT RATHER THAN WEAKENING IT.  That is the load-bearing sentence: not a weaker rule applied, but no rule applied. ***\n\n**THE MANUAL EXPRESSLY REQUIRES WRITE JUDGMENT WHEN FILLING THE QUEUES**, so this is a correctness gap rather than a defence-in-depth preference -- which is precisely the distinction that leaves `sh4itlbpr` unlicensed under the addition licence.  *These two SH rows look alike and are not.*\n\n**A SECOND FUNCTION EXISTS AND DOES NOT SUPPLY THE CHECK:** `sh_translate_sq_v2p()` (`memory_sh.c:255-318`) forwards the caller's flags straight to `translate_via_mmu()`.  It neither forces read classification nor guards the ordinary SQ branch -- **and its actual caller is outside the permitted files, so whether it is wired at all is UNVERIFIED.**\n\n**REACHABILITY IS SUPPORTED STRUCTURALLY, NOT CERTIFIED.**  The static chain is guest data access -> installed translation hook -> SQ identity mapping -> registered SQ device -> queue backing array, and all of it is guest-facing rather than debugger-only.  *The missing instruction and generic-memory implementations prevent certifying every link during actual instruction execution* -- stated as a limit rather than glossed.\n\n**THE ROW'S \"READABLE BY AN ORDINARY GUEST LOAD\" CLAIM IS CONFIRMED:** EXPEVT sits at P4 `0xFF000024`, reachable by a privileged `MOV.L @Rm,Rn`, and the device's read case returns `cpu->cd.sh.expevt`.  **So no new probe capability is needed** -- which is what made this row the cheapest high-value item in the cohort.\n\n*** BUT A LOAD FROM THE SQ APERTURE IS A DIFFERENT PROPOSITION, AND THIS IS THE CAUTION I WOULD HAVE MISSED: this emulator's SQ handler supports reads, while the manual defines architectural SQ readback ONLY for SH7750R, through a SEPARATE aperture `0xFF001000-0xFF00103C`.  \"Such emulator readback cannot silently become a universal hardware oracle.\" ***\n\n**STEPPING: SYNCHRONOUS, so a stepping probe is suitable in principle.**  The MMU failure path invokes `sh_exception()` directly with interrupt argument zero, and that function updates EXPEVT and transfers control **with no `single_step` guard**.  So the recorded suppression of ASYNCHRONOUS delivery does not disqualify this path.  **And `FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS` is a separate mechanism that must not be conflated with `single_step`** -- a distinction this project has blurred before.\n\n=== THE ORACLE, five wrong behaviours and what separates each ===\n* **missing write check** -- the exception must be ATTRIBUTABLE to the tested access, with its fault address and saved instruction address;\n* **wrong code** -- **exact EXPEVT comparison; reaching `VBR + 0x100` alone cannot distinguish protection, initial-write and several unrelated exceptions**;\n* **write judgment applied to READS** -- a permitted read keeps read semantics, including no initial-write fault merely because D is clear;\n* **faulting EVERY SQ store** -- permitted fills must complete and show their intended effect;\n* **store never executed** -- *program liveness or an unchanged EXPEVT is INSUFFICIENT*; an old matching EXPEVT value, an unrelated exception, or a read without established execution all produce a FALSE PASS.\n\n*** AND A VACUITY WARNING ABOUT A ROW THAT LOOKS SOUND: a passing READ control does NOT automatically kill a mutation at the SQ-fill guard ***, because `sh_translate_sq_v2p()` enters the common MMU helper directly and ordinary translated reads need not traverse the new guard at all.  Establishing that requires the omitted caller and the actual mutation site.\n\n**SCOPE HELD, AND IT BOUNDS THE SEVERITY HONESTLY: this does NOT establish that an SQ fill overwrites protected external RAM.**  The operation changes queue SRAM; the external transfer is a SEPARATE operation.  No privilege escalation, no host compromise, no severity ranking and no runtime reproduction are claimed.\n\n**STATUS: ready for a round.**  The defect is established at the translation and device layers, the observation register exists, the exception is synchronous, and the oracle is specified.  What is owed is the instruction-path link and the external-memory question -- both measurable."}, {"id": "fx_regdone", "title": "sh4sqwrite  The SQ WRITE half is never judged -- so an SQ page can raise no initial-page-write and no write-side protection fault, while the PREF still raises the read-side set", "state": "closed", "commits": "abc1234", "hold": "", "witness": "none yet -- GUEST-REACHABLE and witnessable at rung 3 (a real guest store through real decode, observable in EXPEVT), but a witness needs a build, which the machine embargo forbids.", "closure": {"by": "", "badge": "", "note": ""}, "entries": [{"seat": "fable5", "phase": "regress", "date": "2026-09-19", "note": "[HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5] owed", "ev": "", "d": "HELD"}, {"seat": "fable5", "phase": "regress", "date": "2026-09-20", "note": "REGRESS CLEAN", "ev": "", "d": "✓"}], "state_note": "*** SCOPE CORRECTION 2026-08-27: THE DEFECT IS THE **WRITE HALF ONLY**, and saying the window 'bypasses the MMU ENTIRELY' reintroduces wording this row's title was already corrected away from. ***\n\n**Why the unqualified form is wrong:** the round's own `sq miss evt` detector observes a **read-type TLB miss (`0x040`)** on the fixed build, so the read side is NOT unrouted. `memory_sh.c:290-301` carries the corrected scope.\n\n> **THE CORRECT STATEMENT: no initial-page-write exception and no write-side protection check on stores into the SQ window.** The read/prefetch path translates; the store path does not. That asymmetry IS the defect, and it is narrower and more defensible than 'bypasses the MMU'.\n\n**AND THE SECTION CITATIONS ARE BOTH RIGHT, FOR DIFFERENT THINGS -- reconciled here so the next reader does not have to choose:**\n  * **section 3.6.7** *defines* the Initial Page Write exception and its code: *\"occurs when the D bit is 0 even though a UTLB entry contains address translation information matching the virtual address to which a data access (write) is made... Sets exception code H'080 in EXPEVT.\"* (`sh7750_hardware.txt:9918-9928`)\n  * **section 4.7.4, 'SQ Protection'** *applies* it to the store queues: *\"Write type exception judgment is performed for writes to the SQs... a TLB miss exception, protection violation exception, or initial page write exception is generated.\"* (`:11742-11748`)\n**The code cites 4.7.4; I cited 3.6.7. Cite BOTH -- 4.7.4 is the one that makes it apply to this window, and 3.6.7 is the one that says what the exception is.**\n\n**Ranking, from today's adjudication: 7th of 8.** Latent -- no known guest depends on SQ write-protection. *It is a fidelity gap, not a live corruption, and my earlier 'most severe row in the batch' was severity within that batch, not across the queue.*\n\n*** INDEPENDENTLY RE-VERIFIED 2026-08-27, and the step that could have KILLED it -- whether SH-4 exempts the store queue from translation -- goes the OTHER WAY. The manual refutes the exemption. (Already documented in-source and filed; today's work is re-verification.) ***\n\n**THE BYPASS IS UNCONDITIONAL.** `memory_sh.c:353-357`:\n```c\n/*  Store queue region:  */\nif (vaddr >= 0xe0000000 && vaddr < 0xe4000000) {\n        /*  Note/TODO: Take SH4_MMUCR_SQMD into account.  */\n        *return_paddr = vaddr;\n        return 2;\n}\n```\n**No `MMUCR.AT` test, no UTLB walk, no PR/D check**, and it returns 2 (read/write). `cpu_sh.c:106` is the only assignment of `translate_v2p`, and the sole SH consumer of `0xe0000000` in `src/cpus/` is `pref_rn` -- **no store instruction special-cases the window**, so every `mov.l Rm,@Rn` into it takes this branch. The preceding arm (`:339-350`) matches neither, so the path is identical with AT=1 and AT=0.\n\n*** THE MANUAL SETTLES IT AGAINST THE EXEMPTION, from three directions: ***\n  * **section 4.7.4, SQ Protection** (`sh7750_hardware.txt:11742-11748`): *\"When MMU is on -- Operation is in accordance with the address translation information recorded in the UTLB, and MMUCR.SQMD. **Write type exception judgment is performed for writes to the SQs** ... a TLB miss exception, protection violation exception, or **initial page write exception** is generated.\"*\n  * **section 4.7.3** (`:11691-11696`): *\"the ASID, V, SZ, SH, PR, and D bits have the same meaning as for normal address translation.\"*\n  * **QACR is explicitly the MMU-OFF-only mechanism** (`:11719-11723`, `:10473`), so it is NOT an alternative translation path under AT=1.\n\n**AND PRIVILEGE DOES NOT RESCUE IT.** The Data TLB Protection Violation table (`:12632-12640`) gives `PR=00` as **\"Only read access possible\"** in PRIVILEGED mode, with EXPEVT `0x0A0` read / `0x0C0` write at `:12650`.\n\n*** THE ASYMMETRY IS THE CLEANEST STATEMENT OF THE DEFECT: THE READ SIDE IS TRANSLATED AND THE WRITE SIDE IS NOT. *** The flush path at `cpu_sh_instr.c:3706-3765` explicitly checks `SH4_MMUCR_AT` and calls `sh_translate_sq_v2p()` -> `translate_via_mmu()`. The store path does none of it.\n\n**WHY THE SHIPPED DETECTOR CANNOT SEE THIS, and it is not a detector defect:** `regress/sh_halt_probe.py:590-599` pins `sq pr00 evt` and `sq d0 evt` to **NOEXC deliberately** -- those rows exercise the read-type PREF path, where no protection or dirty case exists. **No row exercises the STORE side at all**, so the detector is silent here rather than contradicting the finding.\n\n**ADJACENT, DISTINCT, ALREADY FILED:** the `SQMD` TODO on that same branch is a SECOND gap -- section 4.7.4 requires an ADDRESS ERROR for a user-mode SQ access when `MMUCR.SQMD = 1` even when translation succeeds, and the store path checks neither. `pref_rn` does check it (`cpu_sh_instr.c:3709-3715`) but raises `EXPEVT_RES_INST` instead -- filed as `sh4sqexpevt`.\n\n*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: TRUE and GUEST-REACHABLE. A store into the store-queue window BYPASSES THE MMU, so page protection is not enforced on it. The most severe row in this batch. ***\n\n**The mechanism:** the SQ write path does not go through `sh_translate_v2p()` / `translate_via_mmu()` (`memory_sh.c:353-357`, `:203-241`, with the source's own commentary at `:289-303`), so a store into the SQ window lands directly in the device's `sq` array.\n\n**Guest access sequence, fully specified by the seat:**\n  1. guest kernel enables paging (`MMUCR.AT = 1`);\n  2. guest maps the SQ virtual page `0xe0000000` in the UTLB **valid** (`V=1`) and either **clean** (`D=0`) or **read-only** (`PR=00`);\n  3. guest issues `mov.l r0, @r1` with `r1 = 0xe0000000`.\n\n**Expected on hardware (SH7750 sections 4.7.4 / 3.6.7):** an **Initial Page Write** exception (`EXPEVT = 0x080`) on `D=0`, or a **Data Write Protection** exception on a read-only page.\n**Actual here:** the store simply succeeds.\n\n> **So a guest that maps a page read-only, or clean-for-dirty-tracking, and then stores through the SQ window gets a write that hardware would have faulted.** That is protection and dirty-bit tracking silently not enforced -- not a fidelity nit.\n\n**Observable:** the guest `EXPEVT` register after the store -- `0x080` (or the write-protect code) when correct, unchanged when broken. Readable by an ordinary guest load, so no new probe capability is needed.\n\n**CHECKABLE AGAINST A PRIMARY SOURCE WE HAVE:** the seat cites SH7750 sections 4.7.4 and 3.6.7, and `_scratchpad/sh7750_hardware.txt` is present and greppable. **Confirm the two sections before the round** -- and read the PROSE, not a figure, per the extraction rule.\n\n\n\n*** PROMOTED BY THE CODEX RATING PASS, 2026-09-07, ON A ROW IT WAS NOT ASKED ABOUT -- and the\npromotion is the answer to the brief's Q3, *what does my reading order miss?* ***\n\n*\"Promote `sh4sqwrite` ahead of the latent ASIC proposals.  Its title sounds like missing tests,\nbut `memory_sh.c` (anchor `Store queue region:`) grants read/write access directly, and `dev_sh4.c`\n(anchor `DEVICE_ACCESS(sh4_sq)`) accepts the writes into queue storage.  Hardware requires\nwrite-type exception checks on stores into those queues.\"*  (Renesas SH7750 manual, S4.7.4.)\n\n> **THAT IS A GUEST-REACHABLE MISSING PROTECTION CHECK, not a coverage gap** -- and it was invisible\n> to my keyword scan because the TITLE reads like harness debt.  *\"No known guest depends on it\"\n> does not establish unreachability.*\n\n**And the row's existing witness cannot carry it:** `sq pr00 evt` and `sq d0 evt` in\n`regress/sh_halt_probe.py` exercise **PREF's READ-type judgement**, so they cannot witness a missing\nSTORE-side check.  A witness for this row has to drive a store.\n\n*Filed as the strongest single finding of that pass; it needs a `sev` and a round of its own.*", "note": "\n\n*** 2026-09-11 -- NOW UNBLOCKED, AND IT IS THE BIGGEST MOVE IN THE COHORT. ***\n\nBlocker verbatim: *a witness needs a build, which the machine embargo forbids*.  **Builds are routine; the embargo is gone, residue included.**\n\nThis row was **promoted 2026-09-07 by a codex rating pass on a row it was not asked about** -- *promote `sh4sqwrite` ahead of the latent ASIC proposals; hardware requires write-type exception checks on stores into those queues* -- and filed as **the strongest single finding of that pass, needing a `sev` and a round of its own.**\n\n**And it needs no new probe capability:** its own note records that the observable is *readable by an ordinary guest load*.  **So the highest-value row in the cohort was fully unblocked and still carrying a blocker that suppressed it from every ranking.**\n\nReplacement blocker text: *rung-3 witnessable -- a guest store through the SQ window, oracle EXPEVT, readable by an ordinary guest load.  No new capability and no blocker: this owes a ROUND, not a precondition.  Confirm SH7750 S4.7.4 / S3.6.7 from the PROSE, never a figure.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 ASSESS (codex GPT-6 Astra at max, 13,804 b).  **SUPPORTED** -- the first row tonight NOT refuted by its own assessment, and the finding is stronger than the filing. ***\n\n*VERDICT: SUPPORTED at the translation/device layer: SQ fills omit write-type MMU checks.  Complete instruction-path reachability and external-memory consequences remain unverified.*\n\n**`sh_translate_v2p()` handles the SQ window `0xe0000000-0xe3ffffff` by returning the original address with access result 2 (read/write available).  That branch consults NEITHER the write flag NOR MMU state, UTLB entries, PR, D, or privilege; it leaves `MMUCR.SQMD` an explicit TODO; and it RETURNS BEFORE the subsequent user-mode check** (`memory_sh.c:323-369`).  The window is registered to `dev_sh4_sq_access`, whose write branch copies bytes into the 64-byte queue with modulo indexing and returns success -- **no protection judgment, no exception dispatch** (`dev_sh4.c:2865-2867`, `:1574-1587`).\n\nBy comparison, ordinary MMU-translated U0/P0/P3 accesses enter `translate_via_mmu()`, deriving the access type from `FLAG_WRITEFLAG` and evaluating valid entries, VPN match, ASID, protection and dirty state: **PR 0/2 reject privileged writes; PR 1/3 permit writes only without an initial-write fault when D is set.**\n\n> *** THE SQ-FILL PATH OMITS THE JUDGMENT RATHER THAN WEAKENING IT.  That is the load-bearing sentence: not a weaker rule applied, but no rule applied. ***\n\n**THE MANUAL EXPRESSLY REQUIRES WRITE JUDGMENT WHEN FILLING THE QUEUES**, so this is a correctness gap rather than a defence-in-depth preference -- which is precisely the distinction that leaves `sh4itlbpr` unlicensed under the addition licence.  *These two SH rows look alike and are not.*\n\n**A SECOND FUNCTION EXISTS AND DOES NOT SUPPLY THE CHECK:** `sh_translate_sq_v2p()` (`memory_sh.c:255-318`) forwards the caller's flags straight to `translate_via_mmu()`.  It neither forces read classification nor guards the ordinary SQ branch -- **and its actual caller is outside the permitted files, so whether it is wired at all is UNVERIFIED.**\n\n**REACHABILITY IS SUPPORTED STRUCTURALLY, NOT CERTIFIED.**  The static chain is guest data access -> installed translation hook -> SQ identity mapping -> registered SQ device -> queue backing array, and all of it is guest-facing rather than debugger-only.  *The missing instruction and generic-memory implementations prevent certifying every link during actual instruction execution* -- stated as a limit rather than glossed.\n\n**THE ROW'S \"READABLE BY AN ORDINARY GUEST LOAD\" CLAIM IS CONFIRMED:** EXPEVT sits at P4 `0xFF000024`, reachable by a privileged `MOV.L @Rm,Rn`, and the device's read case returns `cpu->cd.sh.expevt`.  **So no new probe capability is needed** -- which is what made this row the cheapest high-value item in the cohort.\n\n*** BUT A LOAD FROM THE SQ APERTURE IS A DIFFERENT PROPOSITION, AND THIS IS THE CAUTION I WOULD HAVE MISSED: this emulator's SQ handler supports reads, while the manual defines architectural SQ readback ONLY for SH7750R, through a SEPARATE aperture `0xFF001000-0xFF00103C`.  \"Such emulator readback cannot silently become a universal hardware oracle.\" ***\n\n**STEPPING: SYNCHRONOUS, so a stepping probe is suitable in principle.**  The MMU failure path invokes `sh_exception()` directly with interrupt argument zero, and that function updates EXPEVT and transfers control **with no `single_step` guard**.  So the recorded suppression of ASYNCHRONOUS delivery does not disqualify this path.  **And `FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS` is a separate mechanism that must not be conflated with `single_step`** -- a distinction this project has blurred before.\n\n=== THE ORACLE, five wrong behaviours and what separates each ===\n* **missing write check** -- the exception must be ATTRIBUTABLE to the tested access, with its fault address and saved instruction address;\n* **wrong code** -- **exact EXPEVT comparison; reaching `VBR + 0x100` alone cannot distinguish protection, initial-write and several unrelated exceptions**;\n* **write judgment applied to READS** -- a permitted read keeps read semantics, including no initial-write fault merely because D is clear;\n* **faulting EVERY SQ store** -- permitted fills must complete and show their intended effect;\n* **store never executed** -- *program liveness or an unchanged EXPEVT is INSUFFICIENT*; an old matching EXPEVT value, an unrelated exception, or a read without established execution all produce a FALSE PASS.\n\n*** AND A VACUITY WARNING ABOUT A ROW THAT LOOKS SOUND: a passing READ control does NOT automatically kill a mutation at the SQ-fill guard ***, because `sh_translate_sq_v2p()` enters the common MMU helper directly and ordinary translated reads need not traverse the new guard at all.  Establishing that requires the omitted caller and the actual mutation site.\n\n**SCOPE HELD, AND IT BOUNDS THE SEVERITY HONESTLY: this does NOT establish that an SQ fill overwrites protected external RAM.**  The operation changes queue SRAM; the external transfer is a SEPARATE operation.  No privilege escalation, no host compromise, no severity ranking and no runtime reproduction are claimed.\n\n**STATUS: ready for a round.**  The defect is established at the translation and device layers, the observation register exists, the exception is synchronous, and the oracle is specified.  What is owed is the instruction-path link and the external-memory question -- both measurable."}]}
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\recordfix_20261013\adj_20261013\ledger_HEAD.json:4280:     "note": "REAL — TSTR start unchecked (dev_sh4.c:1176-1195) but consequence is dead timer, not divide-by-zero (hz is dividend only, :204)",
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\recordfix_20261013\adj_20261013\ledger_HEAD.json:4343:     "note": "REAL - TSTR write (:1176-1194) starts timers with no timer_hz check; consequence is step=0 and a silently frozen timer, not a crash. landisk unaffected (OpenBSD writes TCR first).",
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\recordfix_20261013\adj_20261013\ledger_HEAD.json:5620:     "note": "*** REPRODUCED, AND THE FIX IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED. *** Probe built on the testarm/SA1110 cold debugger with a four-byte raw stub; every encoding re-verified through the emulator's own unassemble before any row was believed. Three binaries in a private tree:\n* HEAD scores 9/15 rows against the architecture; MASK AT THE ACCESS scores 15/15; MASK AT ENTRY scores 14/15 and CORRUPTS THE BASE WRITEBACK (0x00070004 for 0x00070006). *** #357's 'mask at entry corrupts the writeback' is REAL, NOT FOLKLORE -- the mutant was built and measured. ***\n* The divergence, three ways: the SAME LDM run twice against one page returns 0x77881122 then 0x11223344; ONE LDM WITH TWO REGISTERS returns r1 from a raw unaligned address and r3 from an aligned one -- MIXED WITHIN A SINGLE INSTRUCTION, because r1's own access warmed the page; and a cold STM SPILLS INTO THE NEXT WORD (0x00FF3344 / 0x5566FFFF).\n* IT SIDESTEPPED THE `put w` WARMS / `put b` DOES NOT TRAP RATHER THAN TRUSTING IT: the slow path's own memory_rw inserts the page on its way out (memory_rw.c:732, no NO_EXCEPTIONS), so the first bdt access to a cold page is slow and every later one is fast. Running the IDENTICAL instruction twice makes the divergence a difference between two executions rather than a value that depends on trusting a seeding rule.\n* FIX: two lines, cpu_arm_instr.c:1563 (load) and :1752 (store), addr -> addr & ~(uint32_t)3. Confirmed from the CODE (`uint32_t addr = *np` at :1514, `if (w_bit) *np = addr` at :1624 -- addr IS the writeback value), from the LOCAL manual (A5.4.2 p.A5-43; LDM(1) p.A4-37, STM(1) p.A4-190), and from the ENTRY mutant. NUANCE it caught: the Multi-word row of Table A2-10 is on the CONTINUATION page A2-41, not A2-40 where the table starts.\n* IN-TREE PRECEDENT it did not expect: generate_arm_multi.c:117 already emits `addr &= 0xffc` and the generated combiners write back from the UNMASKED register -- upstream's own generated LDM combiners already use exactly this shape, and they fall back to instr(bdt_load) on a cold page, so they inherit both the defect and the fix. Thumb PUSH/POP (cpu_arm.c:1670,1677) call the same two functions; arm_pop/arm_push have exactly four callers, all enumerated.\n* *** THE EXISTING GATE IS BLIND TO IT, MEASURED: arm_writeback_probe.py scores 22/22 on ALL THREE binaries including the ENTRY mutant that corrupts the writeback, and gate 14's 261 checks touch neither arm_pop nor arm_push. *** The single check separating the correct fix from the #357 trap is the W=1 writeback row. So the gate row is NOT redundant and must land in the SAME commit as the fix, being scored against architecture rather than against HEAD.\n* Side effect named rather than left to be found: masking also removes the 4 KB page-straddle path for bdt, where a raw access at P+0xFFE currently translates once and takes its tail from the physical continuation.\n* It also caught a records defect against the main loop: _scratchpad/CHECKPOINT.md had gone stale, naming HEAD 4d0981d and 'Tree CLEAN' when HEAD was 9bcae1c and two files were uncommitted. Re-derived.  [agent armbdt 2026-08-19]"
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\recordfix_20261013\adj_20261013\ledger_HEAD.json:5894:   "note": "*** COMMITTED 2026-09-12 as 4dfc716 (local only).  gate 14 PASS 910 checks at the 43 pin (regress/artifacts/armbdt/killtable_20260911h_floor/gate_arm_43rows.log); all four trees cleared both parts after cpu_arm_instr.c was propagated (three trees held an older comment).  Pass-2 receipt in regress/pass2_ledger.tsv.  State stays `held`: the closure records are owed. ***\n\n*** GATE PIN RAISED 39 -> 43 (2026-09-12).  THE GATE MUST BE RE-RUN; ITS LAST GREEN WAS AT 39. ***\n\nTable 7 added four rows to close the floor -- the three store-side modes that had NO ROW AT ALL\n(`ARM_STM_IB_R7`, `ARM_STM_DA_R7`, `ARM_STM_S_R7`) and the tenth cell (`ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7`) -- so\nthe probe is 43 rows and the literal moved by hand with its reason.  **A pin that follows the probe\ncannot tell a probe that GAINED rows from one that LOST them**, which is why it is a literal.\n\nOWED FOR `#524`, IN ORDER:\n  1. re-run `gate_arm.sh` at the 43 pin;\n  2. re-add the `arm_bdt_probe.py` MANIFEST entry to `check_probe_wiring.py` **in the same commit\n     as the probe** -- the exact line is preserved in a comment there.  The entry without the file\n     is a GHOST; the file without the entry is UNCLASSIFIED.  Both are red, in opposite\n     directions, and I hit each in turn today;\n  3. restore the `#524` CHANGELOG block (`python _scratchpad/land_shared.py round524`) and record\n     table 7 in it -- the block currently describes the closure at 39 rows;\n  4. commit, AFTER `#525`.\n\n---\n\n*** TABLE 7 CLOSES THE CHAIN: ALL TEN FLOOR CELLS KILL, AND THE ONLY ESCAPE IS ONE THE CONTRACT\nNAMED IN ADVANCE.  2026-09-12 02:32. ***  Cited: regress/artifacts/armbdt/killtable_20260911h_floor/SCORES.txt\n\n    BASE            43/43  CONTROL=OK  FAILING none\n    -- regression, the six from table g: all still die --\n    M_POPP_NONE     41/43  IB_P2, DB_P2         M_POPP_WEAK  41/43  IB_P2, DB_P2\n    M_NPMASK        41/43  IB_P2, DB_P2         M_SBIT_NONE  (dies)\n    M_POPW_WEAK     40/43  DB_P2, S_P2, MULTI_P2_R1          M_POPU_WEAK  (dies)\n    -- the six NEW floor mutants: ALL DIE --\n    M_PUSHS_NONE    42/43  ARM_STM_S_R7          <- E1, the MEASURED L2 breach\n    M_POPP_MASK2    41/43  IB_P2, DB_P2          <- the ~2 gap, live until residue 7\n    M_PUSH_IB_WEAK  42/43  ARM_STM_IB_R7         <- a mode with NO ROW AT ALL\n    M_PUSH_DA_WEAK  42/43  ARM_STM_DA_R7         <- likewise\n    M_PUSHPC_NONE   42/43  ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7      <- the tenth cell\n    M_PUSHP_OVER7   41/43  ARM_STM_IB_R7, ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7   <- E4\n    -- disclosed under clause (e), EXPECTED to escape --\n    D_PUW_WEAK      43/43  FAILING none          <- ESCAPED, exactly as disclosed\n    D_POPSW_WEAK    42/43  ARM_LDM_S_P2          <- DIED, better than disclosed\n    RESTORE         cmp-vs-SEC CLEAN, binary FRESH, SENTINEL CLEARED (both parts)\n\n**`D_POPSW_WEAK` dying is the contract being CONSERVATIVE rather than wrong**: upgrading `S_P2` to\n`LDMIA r0!,{r1}^` at residue 7 closed the (s&&w) joint for free, which the PRETABLE predicted.\n\n*** WHAT MAKES THIS TABLE DIFFERENT FROM THE SIX BEFORE IT IS WHERE THE ROWS CAME FROM. ***  It is\nthe first whose row set is enumerated from the HELPERS' OWN INPUTS rather than from the mutant\nlist -- which is what the class-closure rule always said, and what pass C invoked but did not do.\nEvery earlier repair rowed at the residue of the mutant it had just found, so each pass\nnecessarily discovered the next strength.  **Residue 7 (mod 8) discriminates `~1`, `~2`, `~3` AND\n`~7` from ONE access**, so no future strength needs a new residue.\n\nThe `pc` row also settled a question its designing seat left open: it could not establish\nunconditional cold-path reachability from source (it depends on `host_store[0x21] == NULL` and on\nthe decoder not substituting an optimised handler).  **`ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7` is GREEN on BASE, so the\ncold path was taken** -- the measurement answered what the reading could not.\n\n---\n\n*** THE FLOOR IS TEN CELLS, DERIVED FROM THE HELPERS' INPUTS -- NOT FROM THE MUTANT LIST. ***\nAdjudicated 2026-09-11; full text `regress/artifacts/armbdt/passD/fable_floor_20260911.md`.\n\n    FLOOR = {cold first access} x {pop, push} x {IA, IB, DA, DB}\n          + {push} x {s = 1}\n          + {push} x {pc in list}          = TEN CELLS, SEVEN OPEN\n    one cold DATA row per cell, access address = 7 (mod 8)\n\n**RESIDUE 7 MOD 8 IS THE WHOLE GEOMETRIC FIX**: it discriminates `~1`, `~2`, `~3` and `~7` at once,\nso no future strength needs a new residue.  Every previous repair placed its rows at the FOUND\nMUTANT'S residue, which is why each pass found the next one.\n\nOPEN: pop IB / DA / DB for the `~2` strength (all three closure rows sit at `0x20006`, bit 0 CLEAR);\npush IB and push DA (NO ROWS AT ALL -- STM is IA, Thumb PUSH is DB); push s=1 (**E1, MEASURED,\nL2**); push pc-in-list (which also closes E4 for free).\n\n---\n\n*** PASS D DID NOT CLOSE THE CHAIN EITHER. FIVE MORE ESCAPES, MEASURED AT 39/39. THIS IS THE\nFOURTH CONSECUTIVE PASS TO FIND A BREACH, AND THE COUNT IS RISING: 1, 2, 5, 5. ***\n\n    E1 M_PUSHS_NONE   s_bit ? addr : (addr & ~3)               PUSH only   39/39\n    E2 M_POPSW_WEAK   addr & ((s_bit && w_bit) ? ~1 : ~3)      POP         39/39\n    E3 M_POPS_OVER7   addr & (s_bit ? ~7 : ~3)                 POP         39/39\n    E4 M_PUSHP_OVER7  addr & (p_bit ? ~7 : ~3)                 PUSH only   39/39\n    E5 M_PUW_WEAK     addr & ((p&&u&&w) ? ~1 : ~3)             BOTH        39/39\n\n**THE SHAPE IS THE SAME EVERY TIME AND IT IS MY CLOSURE'S OWN SHAPE: I CLOSED THE LOAD SIDE, AT ONE\nRESIDUE, AT ONE STRENGTH.**  E1 and E4 are the PUSH-side halves; E3 is the same axis at a\nBIT2-SET residue; E2 and E5 are joints.  Pass C closed `{site}` for the p axis and left `s`; pass D\nfinds `s` at the other site, and `p` at the other strength.\n\nGOVERNING FACT, CHECKED NOT ASSUMED: **all four bits are used in CONDITIONS by BOTH bodies** --\n`arm_pop` has `if (s_bit)`, `if (p_bit)`, `if (!p_bit)`, `u_bit? :` and `w_bit`; `arm_push`\nlikewise.  So they are BODY-TESTED PARTITIONS and the flagship's `xscaleinten` ruling today (a\nparameter that touches nothing the body reads is clause (e)) does NOT rescue them.\n\n**PROPOSED SPLIT, PUT TO THE ADJUDICATOR RATHER THAN DECIDED HERE:** single-axis cells (E1, E3, E4)\nare FLOOR, L2, close in-round; multi-axis JOINTS (E2, E5) are clause-(e) DEBT on the pass-11\nprecedent.  If that holds, the remaining floor is `{p,u,s,w} x {pop,push} x {none, ~1, ~7}` minus\nwhat is rowed -- finite.  **The second question asked of the adjudicator is whether this chain is\nCONVERGING AT ALL, or has become the `#392` shape wearing the class-closure rule's clothes.**\n\n*** ONE OF MY SEVEN CLOSURE ROWS IS VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION, and the reason is sharper than the\n'kills nothing' I disclosed: `ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_R2` CANNOT fail under a slow-path mutant, because\nTHE FIRST COLD ACCESS WARMS THE PAGE and the second register's access takes the FAST PATH. ***\nMeasured by kimi; glm independently called the table *\"internally impossible as stated\"* from the\nsame evidence without knowing why.  **A reading seat and a measuring seat converging on one defect\nfrom opposite ends is the panel working as designed.**\n\n---\n\n*** GATE GREEN AT THE NEW 39-ROW PIN, 2026-09-11 19:4x. ***  `/tmp/gate_arm_524g.log` (100,681 b),\nrun UNPIPED with no timeout wrapper (a pipe makes the exit status the filter's; a timeout kills the\ngate while reporting a clean completion -- both are documented traps here).\n\n    GATE_EXIT=0\n    ok   bdt rows run       39      <- the class-closure pin, moved 32 -> 39 by hand\n    ok   bdt rows correct   39\n    ^FAIL rows: 0     SKIP occurrences: 0     <- no silent coverage loss\n    gate 14: ARM/Thumb flags (#311/#312/#319-#322/#328/#329): PASS (910 checks)\n\nSTILL OWED BEFORE COMMIT: **PASS D on the closure diff.**  Passes A, B and C each found a breach\n(one, then two, then five), so the class closure owes its own pass -- and the chain closes only on\nthe first VALID pass finding ZERO breaches **with its denominator**.  A bare zero does not close\nit.\n\n---\n\n*** THE CLASS CLOSURE IS MEASURED AND ALL FIVE PASS-C ESCAPES ARE DEAD. 2026-09-11 23:37. ***\nCited: regress/artifacts/armbdt/killtable_20260911g_closure/SCORES.txt  (frozen 39-row probe, one instrument for every leg)\n\n    LEG            was      now      killed by\n    BASE                    39/39    -- CONTROL=OK, FAILING none\n    M_POPP_NONE    32/32    37/39    ARM_LDM_IB_P2, ARM_LDM_DB_P2\n    M_POPP_WEAK    32/32    37/39    ARM_LDM_IB_P2, ARM_LDM_DB_P2\n    M_POPW_WEAK    32/32    37/39    ARM_LDM_DB_P2, ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_R1\n    M_NPMASK       32/32    37/39    ARM_LDM_IB_P2, ARM_LDM_DB_P2\n    M_SBIT_NONE    32/32    38/39    ARM_LDM_S_P2\n    RESTORE                          cmp-vs-SEC=CLEAN, binary=FRESH, SENTINEL CLEARED\n\n**Every mutant that was a TOTAL ESCAPE now reds with NAMED rows, and BASE is clean at 39/39.**\nSeven rows closed the load side of the p/u/s/w x site product: `ARM_LDM_IB_P2` (p=1,u=1),\n`ARM_LDM_DA_P2` (u=0), `ARM_LDM_DB_P2` (p=1,u=0), `ARM_LDM_S_P2` (s=1), and the three-assertion\n`ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_*` leg.  **The multi leg is the bargain: ONE session, THREE assertions, closing\nw_bit x residue-2 AND register-list-length together.**  It had to sit at residue 2 -- at the\nexisting `WB_UNAL2` base (0x20001/0x20005) the low bits are `01`, where `~1` and `~3` AGREE, so\nthat row is structurally blind to a w_bit-conditioned weakening.\n\n*** THREE OF THE SEVEN NEW ROWS KILL NOTHING IN THIS TABLE, AND THAT IS RECORDED RATHER THAN\nGLOSSED: `ARM_LDM_DA_P2`, `ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_R2`, `ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_WB`. ***  Their channels are\nreal but no BUILT mutant varies them -- `DA_P2` exists for the `u_bit=0` cell, which the pass-C\nseat ENUMERATED and explicitly did NOT build (*\"derived (same legs)\"*).  A condemning prediction\ngets built, so `M_POPU_WEAK` -- `addr & (u_bit ? 0xfffffffc : 0xfffffffe)` -- WAS BUILT AND\nMEASURED: **37/39, FAILING ARM_LDM_DA_P2 and ARM_LDM_DB_P2.**  `DA_P2` is therefore\nDISCHARGED BY A MEASURED KILL and the u_bit cell is closed rather than derived.\n*Six mutants built, six dead.*  The other two are secondary assertions on a leg whose primary assertion\nalready kills; they are disclosed under clause (e), not claimed as coverage.\n\nTHE REPAIR-CHAIN STATUS: passes A, B and C each found a breach (M_MASK1; M_MASK7 + M_PBITCOND;\nthen five at once).  **This diff is the CLASS CLOSURE the rule demands after two consecutive\npasses of one class -- the full enumeration in ONE diff rather than another point row -- and it\nowes its own pass.  A zero does not close the chain unless it carries its denominator; the\ndenominator is the table above plus the enumeration the pass-C seat swept (mask value, placement,\nsite, predicate x 2 sites x 4 bits x 2 strengths = 16 cells, operand, joint (p,u), reg-list\nlength, byte order, null medium).**\n\n---\n\n*** PASS C DID NOT CLOSE THE CHAIN. 2026-09-11 17:52. FOUR SEATS OF SIX FOUND A CLAUSE-(d)\nESCAPE; TWO DID NOT.  `#524` DOES NOT COMMIT. ***\n\n**`M_POP_PBITCOND` -- found INDEPENDENTLY by codex and grok**, the read-side counterpart of the\nmeasured `M_PBITCOND`:\n    /*  at the arm_pop() read call, fix present at BOTH sites, then:  */\n    addr & (p_bit ? 0xfffffffe : 0xfffffffc)\n\n**Predicted 32/32, a TOTAL SWEEP**, because every tested load carries p_bit=0 -- established by\nDECODING the probe's literal instruction words (`0xe8900002`, `0xe8b00002`, `0xe8b00006`, all IA;\nThumb `0xbc02`), not by assertion.\n\n*** THIS IS THE `#499` SHAPE FOR THE THIRD TIME IN ONE DAY, AND THE FLAGSHIP ADJUDICATION PREDICTED\nIT HOURS EARLIER: *\"Each pass today found a new cell; extrapolate.\"* ***  Pass A found `M_MASK1`;\npass B found `M_MASK7`/`M_PBITCOND`; pass C finds the SYMMETRIC CELL OF PASS B'S OWN REPAIR -- the\nclass closure repaired the store-side cell `M_PBITCOND` used and left the load-side open.\n\n**grok named the enumeration that was missing, in one sentence:** *\"Mask-width (~1 / ~7 / ~15) is\nCLOSED; the predicate x site product is NOT.\"*  So the owed closure is\n**{unconditional, p_bit-conditioned} x {arm_pop, arm_push}** in ONE diff, not another single cell --\nthe class-closure rule, which pass B already invoked and applied to only one axis.\n\n**AN EQUIVALENT-MUTANT TRAP INSIDE THE MUTANT, from grok:** the form must be truthy `p_bit ?`, NOT\n`p_bit == 1`.  `bdt_load` passes `iw & 0x01000000`, so `== 1` is DEAD on every ARM encoding and\nwould measure EQUIVALENT for the wrong reason.\n\n*** IT IS A PREDICTION AND IS RECORDED AS OWED, NOT AS A SCORE. ***  Both seats said so plainly --\ngrok: *\"I could not exec a scratch rebuild (WSL was blocked in this seat)... treat the 32/32 as a\npredicted sweep that still owes a clone run.\"*  Per the flagship ruling on predictions: **a\nCONDEMNING prediction gets BUILT FIRST** (a wrong condemnation costs one build; a wrong excuse ships\na hole).  Build `M_POP_PBITCOND`, score it, and if it sweeps that is **L2 -- repair in-round**.\n\nOpus 5 was fired on the same brief and is still running; agy deliberately not fired.\n\n---\n\n*** 2026-09-11 INVESTIGATION -- **CONFIRMED AT HEAD, AND THE SPLICE IS TRACED TO THE memcpy, NOT ASSERTED.  The row is right in every particular and the artifact genuinely does not exist.** ***\n\n**THE MECHANISM, read at HEAD (`cpu_arm_instr.c`, unmodified in `git status`):**\n  * `arm_pop()` `:1602-1764` and `arm_push()` `:1770-1859` each hold one running `addr = *np`, the RAW base-register value, and branch on host page residency per register.\n  * **FAST path** (page resident): `p32[(addr & 0xfff) >> 2]` -- the `>>2` truncates the low two bits, so it is **architecturally correct** for the implemented A=0/U=0 state (DDI0100I LDM(1) p.A4-37 / STM(1) p.A4-190: *ignore the least significant two bits of the address*).\n  * **SLOW path** (cold): `cpu->memory_rw(..., addr, data, 4, ...)` with the **RAW, UNMASKED addr** (`:1653-1654`, `:1842-1843`).  **Traced into `memory_rw.c:728-782`: `offset = paddr & offset_mask` is PAGE-granularity, NOT word-rounded, and the transfer is a raw `memcpy` at `:770-772`/`:780-782`.  So an unaligned addr genuinely SPLICES BYTES ACROSS AN ARCHITECTURAL WORD BOUNDARY** -- confirmed at the lowest level rather than inferred.\n\n> **The same instruction, the same unaligned base, produces two different byte patterns purely according to HOST PAGE-CACHE WARMTH -- a condition invisible to the guest architecture.**\n\n*** AND THE WRITEBACK IS CORRECT AS IT STANDS, WHICH IS WHY THE FIX SHAPE MATTERS: *** `if (w_bit) *np = addr;` uses the unmasked running addr, and **Mode 4 writeback DERIVES from unmasked Rn**.  **So the repair must mask ONLY at the two `memory_rw` call sites and NEVER at entry** -- masking at entry corrupts writeback.\n\n**THE IN-TREE PRECEDENT FOR EXACTLY THAT SHAPE ALREADY EXISTS:** `src/cpus/generate_arm_multi.c:117` emits `addr &= 0xffc;` -- the code-generated fast dispatch already masks the ACCESS while leaving writeback unmasked.  *A transferred warrant, verified at the cited line.*\n\n**FOUR CALLERS, all confirmed:** `X(bdt_load)` `:1889`, `X(bdt_store)` `:1921`, and Thumb PUSH/POP at `cpu_arm.c:1670`/`:1677`.  **Thumb inherits the defect and has ZERO probe coverage of any kind.**\n\n=== NO ARTIFACT EXISTS.  SEARCHED BY THE DEFECT'S VOCABULARY, NEVER BY GUESSING A FILENAME ===\n*(the brief warned against filename invention because I did it twice today; the search was done by function names and mnemonics instead)*\n  * filename patterns `*armbdt*`, `*bdt*`, `*block*transfer*`, `*blockdata*`: **0 hits**\n  * **127** artifact directories: none for this item\n  * content-grep of all **93** `regress/*.py` for LDM/STM mnemonics: **2 files**, both testing something else\n  * `bdt_load|bdt_store|arm_pop|arm_push`: **1 hit**, a comment\n  * `CHANGELOG.md`: **0** occurrences -- **no round has ever shipped against it**\n\n**GATE 14's 358 IS CORRECT AND CURRENT**, confirmed two independent ways -- `gate_arm.sh:561-563`'s comment AND commit `6014b7e`'s message recording the gate's own run output.  *(The carrier records this figure was stale once at 261, so both were treated as claims.)*  **And it does not cover this defect under any name:**\n  * `arm_writeback_probe.py` scopes itself explicitly to *the single-register path* -- a different generator entirely;\n  * `arm_endian_probe.py` DOES reach `arm_pop`/`arm_push`, but only on byte order and **with a word-aligned base (`0x10000`), where masking is a no-op** -- so it cannot discriminate this defect whichever path a row takes;\n  * measured corroboration: three binaries (HEAD / mask-at-access / mask-at-entry) all scored **22/22 identical** on `arm_writeback_probe.py`, **including the entry-mask mutant that corrupts writeback**;\n  * and the regress adjudication of `6014b7e` states it plainly -- *gate_arm passed 16/16 WITH armbdt PRESENT.*\n\n*** THE MANDATORY ROW: a WRITEBACK row threading an unaligned base through a W=1 transfer and asserting the base register equals the UNMASKED arithmetic result. ***  It is the row `arm_writeback_probe.py` is structurally blind to, and **the only one that discriminates the correct fix from the plausible-but-wrong mask-at-entry mutant.**\n\n**CITATION DRIFT, noted rather than silently fixed:** the 2026-08-19 entries cite `:1563`/`:1752`; HEAD has the same code at `:1653`/`:1842` -- about 90 lines, from unrelated intervening edits.  The mechanism is unchanged; only the numbers moved.\n\n**STILL LIVE AT HEAD**, checked from source rather than from the row: `git log -10` on that file shows the recent touches are all unrelated to the `memory_rw` calls.\n\n*Investigated by a SONNET subagent while both Anthropic seats were walled -- see [[agentmodelwall]].  The work is of the right standard for a READING task, which is the half of that row's claim this supports; it says nothing about measuring equivalence.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 WITNESS + KILL TABLE (agy 3.8, measuring seat, independent transport).  **THE WITNESS EXISTS.  RUNG 3, UNMODIFIED testarm, 17/25 PRE-FIX AND 25/25 POST-FIX -- after three weeks in which this row's own record said the artifact genuinely did not exist.** ***\n\n**THE DEFECT IS UNCHANGED AND IS NOW MEASURED RATHER THAN DERIVED.**  The fast path (`p32[(addr & 0xfff) >> 2]`) truncates the low two bits and is architecturally CORRECT per DDI 0100I LDM(1) p.A4-37 / STM(1) p.A4-190; the slow path passes the **RAW, UNMASKED `addr`** to `memory_rw`, whose `offset = paddr & offset_mask` is PAGE-granularity and whose transfer is a raw `memcpy`.  **So the identical instruction at the identical unaligned base yields DIFFERENT BYTES according to host page-cache warmth -- a condition invisible to the guest architecture.**\n\n=== THE WITNESS, AND IT CLEARS EVERY LADDER CLAUSE ===\n**Rung 3, cold interactive debugger, on the UNMODIFIED in-tree `testarm`** (`gxemul -V -A -T -E testarm -M 64`) driven through a pty.  **No `device_add`, no custom machine -- which is the laundering clause, and it is met by construction rather than by assertion.**\n\n*** THE PAGE-CACHE CONDITIONING IS THE PART THAT MAKES IT A WITNESS AT ALL, and it turns on a fact this project got BACKWARDS once and had two shipped rounds leaning on the wrong version: *** `put b` writes byte-wise with `CACHE_NONE | NO_EXCEPTIONS`, populating physical RAM **without** updating the translation tables, so `host_load`/`host_store` stay NULL -- the COLD seed; `put w` writes words with `CACHE_DATA` and **WARMS** the host page -- the WARM seed.  *The carrier records `put w` WARMS and `put b` does not; the witness uses exactly that asymmetry as its instrument.*\n\n**AND IT VERIFIES THE PATH RATHER THAN ASSUMING IT:** before every transfer the probe runs `tlbdump 0,r` to confirm the target is genuinely ABSENT from the host cache on a cold run and RESIDENT on a warm one.  **That is the row establishing which path each run took -- without it the core comparison proves nothing**, which is the one thing the brief insisted on.\n\n=== SCORES, and the artifact for every one of them is named ===\n*(`regress/artifacts/armbdt/killtable_20260911/`, `KILLTABLE_DONE` sentinel present, each score read from its own `probe_<name>.raw`)*\n\n| leg | score | reading |\n|---|---|---|\n| `BASE` (fix applied) | **25/25** | clean |\n| `HEAD` (committed, pre-fix) | **17/25** | all four cold-data rows AND all four divergence checks red -- *test-first satisfied: the symptom is shown on the COMMITTED build and goes green post-fix with the same script* |\n| `M_ENTRY` (mask at entry) | **20/25** | *** KILLED, AND KILLED BY THE WRITEBACK ROWS SPECIFICALLY *** |\n| `M_POP_ONLY` (one site masked) | 21/25 | killed |\n| `M_PUSH_ONLY` (other site masked) | 21/25 | killed |\n| `M_NULL_MEDIUM` (identity) | **6/25** | 19 rows red -- the rows genuinely observe block transfer, discharging clause (c) |\n| `RESTORED` | **17/25** | *** IDENTICAL TO `HEAD`, which is the clean-restore proof: the clone came back to the committed baseline exactly. *** |\n\n*** `M_ENTRY` IS THE VINDICATION OF THE MANDATORY ROW. ***  The brief said the writeback row *'is the only one that discriminates the correct fix from the plausible-but-wrong mask-at-entry mutant'*, and predicted `arm_writeback_probe.py` is structurally blind to it -- which had already been measured: three binaries (HEAD / mask-at-access / mask-at-entry) all scored **22/22 IDENTICAL** on that probe, **including the entry-mask mutant that corrupts writeback.**  The new instrument kills it 20/25.  *A row specified from the defect's own control flow, before any table ran, and it is the row that carries the whole discrimination.*\n\n**ORDER PROVEN BY MTIME, not asserted:** `PRETABLE.out` written **06:46:22**, first table run `probe_BASE.raw` **06:50:06** -- 3m44s earlier, so the pre-table block cannot have been fitted to the answers.\n\n=== THE FIX SHAPE IS CONFIRMED, AND THE TRAP WITH IT ===\n**Mask ONLY at the two `memory_rw` call sites** -- `addr & ~3` at `cpu_arm_instr.c:1653` and `:1842`.  **NEVER at entry:** `if (w_bit) *np = addr;` (`:1715`, `:1858`) uses the unmasked running `addr`, and Mode 4 writeback derives from unmasked `Rn`, so an entry mask corrupts writeback -- which is precisely what `M_ENTRY` measures.  In-tree precedent verified at the cited line: `generate_arm_multi.c:117` emits `addr &= 0xffc;`, masking the ACCESS while leaving writeback alone.\n\n*** HOUSE RULES OBSERVED, VERIFIED BY ME RATHER THAN TAKEN FROM THE REPORT: `git status` shows `src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c` UNMODIFIED in GXEMUL-SEC. ***  The seat worked in `/tmp/gxarmbdt_clone` and wrote only into `regress/artifacts/armbdt/`, which is the measuring-seat rule met exactly.  The live `/tmp/gxop519p2` tail was not touched.\n\n**STATUS: the fix is SPECIFIED AND MEASURED BUT NOT APPLIED TO SEC.**  What remains is the edit itself, propagation into the build trees, a pass-2 panel on the real diff, and the gates -- none of which could be done in this window, because the `#519` regression tail holds the machine and this project's gates carry load-sensitive wall-clock oracles.  *Deliberately left rather than rushed: a measurement taken against a loaded host is the false-FAIL this project has already paid for once.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 IMPLEMENTED.  **FIX APPLIED TO SEC AS `#524`, MASKING THE ACCESS AND NOT THE RUNNING addr** ***\n\n**Round number `#524`, chosen after checking: `#523` is STAKED by `regress/artifacts/wdcerrfamily/codex_design523_20260911.txt` even though it appears in no CHANGELOG entry, source file or commit.**  *An artifact filename is a claim on a number, and taking a staked one would have collided with a round already in design.*\n\n**THE EDIT, both sites, `uint32_t addr` masked with a hex literal to match the file's own idiom (`addr & 0xfff`, `addr &= 0xffc`):**\n    cpu_arm_instr.c  arm_pop()  : cpu->memory_rw(..., addr & 0xfffffffc, data, ..., MEM_READ, ...)\n    cpu_arm_instr.c  arm_push() : cpu->memory_rw(..., addr & 0xfffffffc, data, ..., MEM_WRITE, ...)\n\n**THE TWO WRITEBACK SITES WERE VERIFIED UNTOUCHED AFTER THE EDIT** -- `if (w_bit) *np = addr;` still reads the bare unmasked running value at both.  *That is the whole discrimination: an entry-mask scores 20/25 on this round's detector and **22/22 -- identical to a correct build -- on the pre-existing `arm_writeback_probe.py`.*  The comment records that measurement at the site, so a future edit tempted to hoist the mask meets the evidence rather than the rule.\n\n*Other `memory_rw(..., addr, ...)` calls in this file (the single-register load/store paths and the instruction fetch) were left alone -- checked by grep after the edit: 7 remain bare and none is on the block-data-transfer path.*\n\n**PROPAGATION, and it is deliberately PARTIAL:**\n  * `est/` -- **DONE and verified BYTE-IDENTICAL by `cmp`.**  It was confirmed equal to SEC@HEAD BEFORE the copy, so it is genuinely a shared file rather than a silent divergence being overwritten.  *est/ is a SOURCE tree with no binary, so propagating without rebuilding creates no stale-binary trap.*\n  * `build/`, `build-asan/`, `/tmp/gxsec-build` -- **NOT propagated.**  Three codex jobs are in flight and a rebuild is CPU-heavy; **propagating into a build tree without rebuilding is exactly the trap recorded on [[xscaleinten]] this morning**, where all three trees report `FRESH` over a source that is simply the wrong source.  Recorded in `_scratchpad/CHECKPOINT.md` so a fresh session finds the debt.\n\n**STILL OWED BEFORE THIS COMMITS: propagate+rebuild the three build trees together, a pass 2 on the real diff, and the targeted gate.**  The witness and the kill table are already done (HEAD 17/25, fix 25/25, `M_ENTRY` 20/25, `M_NULL_MEDIUM` 6/25, `RESTORED` = HEAD).\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PASS 2 on the real `#524` diff (codex GPT-6 Astra at max).  **PASS 2: NOT RATIFIED -- THE MASKS ARE RIGHT AND 'ONLY TWO SITES' WAS WRONG.** ***\n\n*VERDICT: NOT RATIFIED -- the two masks are locally correct, but other BDT implementations still use unrounded addresses, and the architectural justification needs qualification.*\n\n**THE MASK AND THE LITERAL ARE CORRECT.**  `addr & 0xfffffffc` clears exactly bits 1 and 0; the unsuffixed hex literal has type `unsigned int` on ordinary implementations (its value does not fit signed `int`), so the result stays unsigned and widens correctly into `memory_rw`'s `uint64_t` parameter.  *A wider host pointer does not change this.*\n\n**FOR AN ALIGNED BASE BOTH CHANGES ARE STRICT NO-OPS** -- every running-address adjustment is a multiple of four, so an initially aligned `addr` stays aligned **including across 32-bit wraparound**, and old and new arguments are identical at both call sites.\n\n=== *** MY 'ONLY TWO SITES ON THE BDT PATH' PREMISE WAS WRONG, AND THE COUNTEREXAMPLES ARE TWO HANDLERS THAT REPLACE LDM/STM OUTRIGHT *** ===\n*'yes for the generic helpers' `memory_rw` calls; no for all implementations of the BDT access rule.'*  **Verified here before filing, all three quotes read from the file:**\n  * **`netbsd_memset`** replaces STM instructions and does **`memset(page + (addr & 0xfff), 0, 128);`** at `:2127` -- raw page offset, no word rounding.  Its runtime guards check page bounds, zero source values and page availability; **none rejects an unaligned address.**\n  * **`netbsd_memcpy`** replaces LDM/STM and copies through raw `& 0xfff` source AND destination offsets (`:2274-2275`), **while its register-publication reads use `& 0xffc`** -- *two different address rules inside one handler.*  Its own comment at `:2252` already acknowledges *'the overlap and unaligned-copy divergences'*.\n  * Their matchers recognise instruction patterns **without establishing address alignment**, and the combined bodies **bypass both edited calls entirely.**\n\n**These predate the `#524` hunks, so they are FILED rather than fixed in-round** per the stopping rule -- [[armbdtcombined]].  *But they mean the round may not claim to have closed the class, only the generic helpers.*\n\n**AND MY CLASSIFICATION OF THE SEVEN REMAINING CALLS WAS WRONG IN DETAIL WHILE RIGHT IN CONCLUSION** -- the count is correct and *'none is a missed generic LDM/STM call'*, but they are not 'generated single-register' calls: `1498/1521` are SWP (already word-rounded at `:1497`), `1540/1547` are SWPB (one-byte, **low bits legitimately select the byte**), `1982/2042` are LDREX/STREX (unaligned rejected before the call), `4009` is the instruction fetch.\n\n=== THUMB: THE FIX REACHES IT, AND A DIFFERENT DEFECT DOES NOT ===\n*'Every transferred register in either helper goes through the same aligned host-word access or the newly masked slow call.  There is no Thumb-specific raw-address branch inside either transfer loop.'*  **So Thumb callers that enter these helpers receive the access fix automatically.**\n\n*Coverage unverified because `cpu_arm.c` was outside the permitted set -- my omission again, the second brief today to exclude the file its own question needed.*  The seat declined to certify the PUSH/POP call sites, whether Thumb LDMIA/STMIA use these helpers, or the reported absence of Thumb probes.\n\n> *** AND IT NAMED A SEPARATE THUMB DEFECT THAT MY PATCH DOES NOT TOUCH: the helper's PC handling CLEARS BOTH LOW PC BITS and calls the ARM pointer-update routine, while DDI0100I's Thumb POP (`ddi0100i.txt:24256`) PRESERVES THE HALFWORD ADDRESS and specifies interworking. ***  An external caller might compensate; that is not establishable from these files.  **Unchanged by `#524` and not claimed by it.**\n\n=== TWO QUALIFICATIONS TO THE ARCHITECTURAL WARRANT, both of which I had not considered ===\n  * **THE HELPERS ARE NOT RESTRICTED TO RAM.**  An unaligned address can reach DEVICE DISPATCH, and *'rounding can change a device-relative offset or even the selected mapping near a device boundary'* -- device selection uses the translated address before the RAM-copy path.\n  * **`ddi0100i.txt:3256` (A2-42) specifies additional UNPREDICTABLE behaviour for ARMv6 unaligned multiword accesses to Device or Strongly Ordered memory**, so *'the unconditional claim that all callers simply ignore those bits is too broad.'*\n\n**AND THE EDIT ROUNDS THE VIRTUAL ADDRESS, BEFORE TRANSLATION** -- `memory_rw` splits translated accesses at **1 KB** (`MRW_SPLIT_MASK == 0x3ff`) while its RAM worker uses a 4 KB offset mask for ARM -- *'the old call can touch, and fault on, a page that the corrected call never accesses.'*  **That is a real behavioural difference beyond the fix**, and it is the intended one (the architecture says ignore the low bits), but it was not in the round's stated reasoning and is recorded now rather than discovered later.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PANEL CONVENED on the combined `#524`/`#525` packet, and **SEAT LEDGER FOR THE #524/#525 PANEL -- THREE SEATS DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED, BY NAME** ***\n\n**FIRED: the eight scriptable transports** via `bash GXEMUL-SEC/tools/panel.sh <absolute brief>` -- codex GPT-6 Astra (max), grok 4.6 (xhigh), kimi 3, and the Ollama cloud seats glm-5.3, deepseek-v4-pro, minimax-m3 (+ the kimi-k3 substitute if configured).  **All four Ollama transports were confirmed REACHABLE by `api/tags` beforehand**, so a non-answer from any of them is a seat failure rather than a dead daemon.\n\n=== *** DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED -- NAMED, BECAUSE A SEAT HELD BACK AND A SEAT THAT FAILED ARE DIFFERENT THINGS AND CONFLATING THEM IS HOW A TWO-SEAT REVIEW GETS REPORTED AS A PANEL *** ===\n  * **agy 3.8** -- RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT by standing owner ruling, and it is the reason both of these rounds have witnesses at all: it built the `armbdt` witness + kill table (25/25) and the `wdcaltdigin` witness + kill table (16/16) earlier today.  *Spending its bounded budget on a reading seven other seats can do is spending the roster's only independent-transport measuring capability on a fungible job.*\n  * **Opus 5** -- HELD.  It answers through the Agent tool and consumes the same Anthropic quota the owner wants preserved for the adjudication.  *Not a failure, not agreement: a budget decision.*\n  * **Fable 5** -- HELD BY EXPLICIT OWNER SEQUENCING: *'don't run fable until all other panel seats have had a say.'*  **This panel is the 'all other seats' step.**  Its availability is to be checked by a plain subagent rather than by Opus when the time comes.\n\n**WHY THE PACKET IS SHAPED AS IT IS:** every fact is INLINED -- both diffs, both full enclosing contexts, both measured kill tables, and **both first-pass NOT-RATIFIED findings** -- because the Ollama transports cannot read files and a brief containing *'check the source'* is a documented way to turn a live seat into a stall that looks exactly like a quota wall.  *The packet also asks the seats to attack the FIRST PASS'S FINDINGS, not only the code, so the corrections get adversarial scrutiny too.*\n\n*** THE LOAD-BEARING QUESTION PUT TO THEM IS THE SELECTOR ONE, and it is imported from a sibling round rather than invented: *** `sgirandomcolour` had **six mutants score a TOTAL ESCAPE** because every detector row probed a single position and each mutant conditioned the repair's effect on a tile or coordinate selector.  **`#524` has 25 rows and `#525` has 16, and neither has had a selector audit.**  The panel is asked to name a conditioning that a plausible edit could produce and the rows could not see.\n\n*Landing remains HELD by owner ruling until the full panel reports -- nothing is committed and no gate has run.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PANEL (`_scratchpad/panel_20260911_103402`).  **SIX of seven scriptable seats answered substantively; the kimi CLI is WALLED and its `kimi-k3:cloud` substitute ANSWERED, so that seat is COVERED, not lost.** ***\n\n**Seat sizes and tails verified individually rather than by size alone:** codex 300,614 b (streams its reasoning; the tail is substantive), grok 11,840 b, `ollama_minimax` 12,053 b, `ollama_glm` 9,695 b, `ollama_kimi` 7,601 b, `ollama_deepseek` 4,219 b.  **`kimi.txt` is 350 b ending `See log: ...kimi-code.log` -- the CLI failure signature, NOT a considered non-answer.**\n\n**CONSENSUS ON THE PRODUCT CODE: NEITHER FIX IS WRONG.**  glm -- *'#524 is architecturally correct and its scope is what was overstated'*; minimax -- *'Both are correct, minimal, and correctly scoped'*; `kimi-k3` -- *'`addr & 0xfffffffc` per-access is provably equivalent'*; deepseek -- *'Neither fix is wrong as a local correction, but #524 is incomplete (combined handlers)'*, **independently reproducing the codex finding about `netbsd_memset`/`netbsd_memcpy`.**\n\n=== *** THE SELECTOR QUESTION GOT REAL ANSWERS, AND THEY ARE FLOOR-BREACH CANDIDATES *** ===\nI asked, importing the shape from [[sgirandomcolour]]: *what conditioning could a plausible edit introduce that the 25 and 16 rows never vary?*\n\n| round | proposed conditioning | seat | predicted |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `#525` | **`if (odata & WDCS_DRDY) odata = 0;`** | grok | **16/16 -- TOTAL ESCAPE** |\n| `#525` | `if (d->unit == 0) odata = 0;` -- a DRIVE-UNIT selector | deepseek | passes if every row uses the primary controller |\n| `#524` | `if (cpu->mem->page_size == 4096) addr &= ~3;` -- a constant property of the test map | deepseek | passes; breaks on any other page size |\n\n**grok also corrected my clause-(d) choice:** deleting `odata = 0` is NOT the discriminating mutant, *because `M_NULL_EFFECT` already scored 14/16* -- the detector sees it.  **The one that escapes keeps the assignment and gates it.**  *That is the difference between a mutant the rows catch and a mutant the rows are blind to, and I had picked the first and called it the floor test.*\n\n**PRESCRIPTION (grok):** *'Product diffs can stand.  The detectors cannot ship as the round's \"we pinned the repair\" claim until `#524` has a `+2` and a `+3` (or an anchored `& 3` mutant) and `#525` has one CORE observation with `WDCS_DRDY` clear.'*  **That is an L3 -- an undischarged obligation, where the row is the sole carrier of something the round claims.**\n\n*** AND A MEASURED-VS-PREDICTED DISCREPANCY IN MY OWN ARTIFACT, caught by grok and verified here: `PRETABLE.out:54` predicts `M_NULL_MEDIUM 4/25`; `SCORES.txt` measures **6/25**. ***  *I cited 6/25 -- the measured one -- so no record is wrong, but the PREDICTION sits in a durable artifact where a future reader can cite it as a measurement.  That is the frozen-number class with a two-row error already in the drawer.*  grok's reading of the remainder: *'PATH/ATTRIB-style controls surviving an early `return`'*.\n\n**Seats DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED, by name: agy** (reserved for measurement; it built both witnesses today), **Opus 5** (held -- same Anthropic quota the adjudication needs), **Fable 5** (held by owner sequencing until every other seat has spoken -- **this panel is that step**).\n\n*** 2026-09-11 FABLE 5 ADJUDICATION (`FABLE_ADJ_20260911`).  **SEAT ALIVE -- token AND a verbatim `CHECKPOINT.md` HEAD quote returned, and it re-ran `git log --oneline -1` itself to confirm the checkpoint is not stale.**  Fired ONLY after all seven other scriptable seats had spoken, per owner sequencing, and via a subagent rather than Opus. ***\n\n=== RULING: **HOLD for ONE MEASUREMENT, then repair IN-ROUND.  Grok MIS-GRADED this: it is not L3, it is CLAUSE (d).** ===\n\n*** IT IS THE CARRIER'S OWN WORKED EXAMPLE -- `#461`'s `m_align1`, a mask narrowed `& 3` -> `& 1`. The fix IS the mask, so a weakened mask is the canonical (d) form, and naming it (d) matters because **a (d) breach cannot be argued around as 'the round does not claim that'.** ***\n\n**CHECKED BY THE ADJUDICATOR, not inferred: every unaligned base in `agy_armbdt_witness_20260911.py` is `+1`** -- `:218` `0x20001`, `:281` `0x21001`, POP `0x22001`, PUSH `0x23005 -> 0x23001`.  **For every row `x+1 & ~1 == x+1 & ~3`, so `0xfffffffc -> 0xfffffffe` at both sites is 25/25 BY ARITHMETIC.**\n\n> **But it must still be BUILT AND SCORED, not asserted.**  Grok said plainly it had not built it, and this project's own rule is that *'it would sweep the table' is a MEASUREMENT, never an inference.*  **Build `M_MASK1` on the clone, score it, and when it escapes add a `+2` DATA row for LDM and one for STM**, re-run all legs, and `M_MASK1` must go red.\n\n**`+3` BUYS NOTHING ENUMERABLE and is DROPPED** -- rows are owed per ENUMERATED MUTANT: `~1` dies at `+2`, `~2` already dies at `+1`.  **Disclose residue 3 in the contract instead of rowing it.**  *That is the row-demand bound doing its job against my instinct to add more rows.*\n\n**Operative licence is L1** (nothing has shipped), not L2.\n\n=== *** THE `PRETABLE` 4/25-vs-6/25 GAP IS EXPLAINED, AND THE EXPLANATION IS A REAL DEFECT *** ===\nThe two extra survivors are **`ARM_STM_COLD_CLOBBER` and `THUMB_PUSH_COLD_CLOBBER`**, found by diffing the row list against the FAILING line.  **A 'neighbour not clobbered' row is an IDENTITY ROW under a medium that writes nothing** -- so `PRETABLE.out`'s claim that *every row genuinely observes block transfer* is **FALSE for those two.**  *The prediction was not merely two off; it rested on a wrong statement about the rows.*\n\n**Write `PRETABLE2.out` for the re-table -- do NOT append to `PRETABLE.out`**: this ledger already records an appended section destroying a PRETABLE's mtime, which is the artifact whose whole evidentiary value is its timestamp.\n\n**TWO L4s IN MY OWN COMMENT, BOTH APPLIED:**\n  1. *'DDI0100I says to ignore the low two bits'* was UNQUALIFIED.  **Verified at `ddi0100i.txt:7954-7959`: that holds for `CP15_reg1_Ubit == 0` AND alignment checking DISABLED; `Ubit == 1` faults on every non-word-aligned access.**  And the fork is internally inconsistent -- `cpu_arm.c:129` SETS `ARM_CONTROL_ALIGN` in the default control register, `:474` only PRINTS it, and **nothing consults it.**  *** THE ROW'S OWN 2026-08-29 fable5 ENTRY ALREADY SAID 'the record must NOT say unqualifiedly' -- so the comment I wrote today broke an obligation this row had already recorded. ***\n  2. *'Measured: ... `arm_writeback_probe.py` scores it 22/22'* -- **`22/22` appears in NO measurement artifact.**  Verified: it occurs only in briefs and seat answers; `probe_M_ENTRY.raw` backs the 20/25 alone.  **Restated BY CONSTRUCTION** (that probe scopes itself to the single-register path and never reaches these helpers), with the word *Measured* removed from it.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 TABLES 2 AND 3.  **THE CLAUSE-(d) BREACH IS MEASURED, CLOSED AND RE-MEASURED: M_MASK1 25/25 -> 27/29.** ***\n\n=== TABLE 2 (`killtable_20260911b/`): THE BREACH, MEASURED ===\n**`M_MASK1: 25/25` -- a TOTAL ESCAPE.**  The fix present, its mask weakened `0xfffffffc` -> `0xfffffffe`: the carrier's own `#461 m_align1` shape (`& 3` -> `& 1`), and **the canonical clause-(d) form for this round because the fix IS the mask.**\n\n*Fable predicted it by arithmetic -- every unaligned base the probe drove was `+1`, and `x+1 & ~1 == x+1 & ~3` -- and ruled that it be BUILT anyway, because this project's rule is that 'it would sweep the table' is a MEASUREMENT, never an inference.  It was right to insist: the prediction was correct, and a round that had accepted it unmeasured would have shipped a floor claim it had not tested.*\n\n*** AND TABLE 1's NUMBERS REPRODUCED EXACTLY ON THE REPAIRED DRIVER -- 7 of 7 legs match. ***  **So table 1's MEASUREMENT was sound even though its DRIVER was defective**; what [[killtablebaseline]] cost was reproducibility, not correctness.  *That is worth knowing before discarding anything from it, and it is the opposite of what a defective-driver finding usually implies.*\n\n=== TABLE 3 (`killtable_20260911c/`): THE CLOSURE, RE-MEASURED ===\n\n| leg | score | rows it fails |\n|---|---|---|\n| `BASE` (the fix) | **29/29** | none |\n| `HEAD` | 19/29 | 10 -- the defect rows, including BOTH new `+2` rows |\n| **`M_MASK1`** | **27/29** | *** exactly 2: `ARM_LDM_COLD_DATA_P2`, `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_P2` *** |\n| `M_ENTRY` | 24/29 | 5 -- **ALL FIVE WRITEBACK ROWS AND NOTHING ELSE** |\n| `M_POP_ONLY` | 24/29 | 5 -- the STM-side rows |\n| `M_PUSH_ONLY` | 24/29 | 5 -- the LDM-side MIRROR of those |\n| `M_NULL_MEDIUM` | 8/29 | 21, including BOTH control rows |\n| `RESTORED` | 19/29 | = `HEAD`, the clean-restore proof (now against GIT) |\n\n> *** EVERY LEG DIES ON A DISTINCT, SEMANTICALLY CORRECT SET, AND THAT IS THE PART WORTH MORE THAN THE SCORES. ***  `M_ENTRY` fails ONLY writeback rows; `M_POP_ONLY` and `M_PUSH_ONLY` fail mirror-image sets; `M_MASK1` fails ONLY the two rows written for it.  *Compare `wdcaltdigin`, where four legs all scored 14/16 and only the row identity distinguished them -- here the row identity and the score agree, which is what a well-separated instrument looks like.*\n\n**THE CLOSURE IS TIGHT, NOT OVER-ROWED: `M_MASK1` fails exactly TWO rows -- the minimum.**  *Rows are owed per enumerated mutant, and two mutant-killing rows plus their two PATH controls is what the enumeration demanded.*\n\n=== THE PREDICTIONS WERE RIGHT TO THE BYTE, WHICH IS THE CHANNEL RULE WORKING ===\n`PRETABLE2.out` named, before the run, the observable each new row would show under `M_MASK1`:\n  * `ARM_LDM_COLD_DATA_P2` -- predicted `0x77881122`, **measured `0x77881122`**;\n  * `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_P2` -- predicted `0x33440000`, **measured `0x33440000`**.\n\n*The channel line requires naming the mutant a row kills AND the observable that differs under it, at writing time, before any table runs.  Both were named and both were exact.*\n\n**ORDER PROVEN BY MTIME:** `PRETABLE2.out` 12:15:13, first table run 12:16:31 -- **78 s earlier**.  Written as a SEPARATE FILE rather than appended to `PRETABLE.out`, because this ledger records an append destroying a PRETABLE's mtime once, and the mtime is the artifact's entire evidentiary value.\n\n*** A PREDICTION OF MINE THAT MISSED, recorded because it is the same class I spent today cataloguing: I predicted `BASE 30/30` and the measurement is `29/29`. ***  I derived 30 from `grep -c 'record('` -- **a CORRELATE of the scored-row count, not the count itself** (one `record()` call is not scored).  *The deriver-keys-on-a-correlate rule, in my own pre-table prediction, on the same day I recorded it against three other censuses.  The error is harmless here because predictions and measurements are in separate sections and every citation names `SCORES.txt` -- which is exactly why they are kept apart.*\n\n**AND THUMB IS COVERED AFTER ALL, which settles part of an open question:** the failing-row lists name `THUMB_POP_WB_UNAL`, `THUMB_PUSH_WB_UNAL`, `THUMB_POP_COLD_DATA` and `THUMB_PUSH_COLD_DATA` -- so the instrument does exercise the Thumb callers, and `M_ENTRY` kills the Thumb writeback rows alongside the ARM ones.  *The `#524` pass-2 seat could not verify this because `cpu_arm.c` was outside its permitted set; the table settles it from the other direction.*\n\n**FLOOR STATUS FOR `#524`: (a) HEAD red, (b) reinstatement dies, (c) null medium 8/29, (d) `M_MASK1` KILLED at 27/29.  The breach is closed in-round under L1** -- nothing had shipped, so this is pre-ship construction rather than an L2 repair.  **Residue 3 disclosed in the contract rather than rowed**, because `~1` dies at `+2` and `~2` already dies at `+1`, so a `+3` row kills no enumerated mutant.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 TABLE 4 (`killtable_20260911d/`).  **TABLE 4: THE CLASS CLOSURE WORKS AND **BASE IS RED ON ONE OF MY OWN NEW ROWS** -- ROUND HELD.** ***\n\n=== THE REPAIR-CHAIN PASS FOUND A FLOOR BREACH AND REFUSED TO CLOSE THE CHAIN ===\n*VERDICT: FLOOR BREACH FOUND; the repair correctly detects `M_MASK1` but does not close the repair chain.*  **Two more clean-sweep candidates against the 29-row composite, both STATIC DEDUCTIONS rather than measured -- which is why each became a leg:**\n  * **`M_MASK7` (`& ~7`, OVER-alignment):** every base the instrument drove -- `0x10000`, `0x2000{1,2}`, `0x2100{1,2}` -- sits in the **FIRST WORD of its 8-byte block, where `~3` and `~7` AGREE.**  *And the two-register LDM does not rescue it: it asserts only `r0` writeback, never either loaded register.*\n  * **`M_PBITCOND` (a CONDITIONAL weakening on an EXISTING dominating predicate):** mask `~1` when `p_bit`, `~3` otherwise, at the STORE site only.  ARM STM is IA (`p_bit=0`, correct mask); Thumb PUSH is DB (`p_bit=1`, weakened) **but ran at exactly ONE residue, `+1`, where `~1` and `~3` agree.**  *The predicate axis was exercised at a single point.*\n\n**TWO CONSECUTIVE PASSES FOUND MASK-WEAKENING BREACHES, so the next repair owed CLASS CLOSURE rather than a third point fix.**  Space from the helpers' own inputs: **{site} x {residue} x {word position in the 8-byte block} x {p_bit}**.  Three uncovered cells closed in ONE diff.\n\n=== AND BOTH NEW MUTANTS DIED, ON THE CELLS WRITTEN FOR THEM ===\n\n| leg | score | killed on |\n|---|---|---|\n| `M_MASK7` | 30/32 | `ARM_LDM_COLD_DATA_BIT2`, `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2` |\n| `M_PBITCOND` | 30/32 | `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2`, `THUMB_PUSH_COLD_DATA_P2` |\n| `M_MASK1` | 28/32 | its two P2 rows **plus both new ones** |\n| `M_ENTRY` | 26/32 | the writeback rows |\n| `M_NULL_MEDIUM` | 8/32 | 21+ rows incl. both controls |\n| `HEAD` / `RESTORED` | 19/32 | identical -- clean restore against GIT |\n\n*** BUT `BASE: 31/32`.  THE FIX ITSELF FAILS `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2` -- one of the rows I ADDED THIS PASS -- and a red BASE row means the round DOES NOT SHIP. ***\n\n**The row reports `w0=0x00000000 w1=0x00000000` on the FIXED build.**  Expected: base `0x21005` masks to `0x21004`, so the word lands in `PAGE_STM+4`.  **Nothing landed in either word.**\n\n> **AND I COULD NOT MAKE THE ARITHMETIC CLOSE BY REASONING.**  Under HEAD the same row reports `w1=0x00000011`, which implies the stored bytes ENDED at `0x21004` -- i.e. a base of `0x21001`, not the `0x21005` my literal sets.  That contradicts `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA`, which calibrates correctly at base `0x21001` giving `0x22334400`.  *Two readings of the same mechanism that cannot both be right.*\n\n**SO I STOPPED REASONING AND INSTRUMENTED**, which is this project's own rule when a value is surprising: a diagnostic rebuilds BASE and **dumps a WIDE WINDOW around `PAGE_STM`, so the bytes can be LOCATED rather than guessed at.**  *I had been deriving where the store went without looking at where it actually is.*\n\n**TWO POSSIBILITIES, AND WHICH ONE IS NOT YET KNOWN:** my row is mis-specified -- most likely, since every other BASE row passes including `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_P2`, which uses the same mechanism at `+2` -- or it has found something real about the store path at bit-2-set addresses.  **Recorded as open rather than resolved in either direction.**\n\n*Also fixed this pass, from the panel: the mutator now emits **`0xfffffffc`, the EXACT shipped literal**, so `BASE` measures the working-tree text rather than a semantic equivalent -- grok raised that the table had not measured what would be committed.*\n\n*** DISCLOSED: THE PATH ROWS ARE FIXTURE CHECKS, NOT MUTATION DISCRIMINATORS, AND THE REPAIR PASS WAS RIGHT ABOUT THE ONES I ADDED. ***  Every `in_tlb_*` is captured BEFORE `run_prog`, so no helper-local mutation of a mask, operand or predicate can retroactively change it.  They establish the run was cold -- which the core comparison needs -- and discriminate NO enumerated mutant.  Kept for that purpose, disclosed rather than counted toward the floor.\n\n*And my `'a +3 row buys no enumerated mutant'` was TOO BROAD as a claim about the COMPOSITE: it holds within a fixed cell, but the missing distinction is the PREDICATE/RESIDUE COMBINATION, and residue 3 is one possible witness among several rather than one that buys nothing.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 TABLE 5 (`killtable_20260911e/`).  **THE RED BASE ROW WAS A DEFECT IN THE WITNESS, NOT THE FIX -- AND IT HAD CORRUPTED 8 OF 10 LEGS.  BASE IS NOW 32/32 AND THE FLOOR IS CLEAN.** ***\n\n=== HOW IT WAS FOUND, INCLUDING THE PART I DID BADLY ===\nTable 4 reported `BASE 31/32` -- the FIX failing `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2`.  **A red BASE row blocks the round, so it was diagnosed rather than explained away.**\n\n*** I FIRST TRIED TO REASON IT OUT FROM THE BYTE PATTERNS AND COULD NOT MAKE THE ARITHMETIC CLOSE. ***  Under HEAD the row implied the store ended at `0x21004` -- a base of `0x21001` -- while `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA` calibrates correctly at that same base.  **Two readings of one mechanism that cannot both be true**, and I spent four rounds of inference on it before instrumenting.\n\n**The dump settled it in ONE run:**\n    r0 = 0x21005                                 <- the literal loaded fine\n    0x00021000  00000000 44332211 00000000 ...   <- the store LANDED at 0x21004\n\n> **THE STORE WAS CORRECT ALL ALONG.  THE READ WAS WRONG.**\n\n=== THE INSTRUMENT DEFECT, MEASURED DIRECTLY ===\n    dump from an ALIGNED start:\n      0x00021000  00000000 44332211\n    dump from an UNALIGNED start -- what `read_mem32` actually issued:\n      0x00021000           44332211 00000000\n                  ^^^^^^^^ the leading group is BLANK-PADDED\n\n`read_mem32()` searched for the line labelled `(addr & ~0xF)` -- **which matches in BOTH cases** -- then indexed `line[12:48].split()` by `(addr & 0xF) // 4`.  **When the dump starts unaligned the leading groups are ABSENT and `.split()` collapses the padding, so every index shifts and `parts[idx]` silently returns a NEIGHBOURING WORD.**\n\n    read_mem32(0x21004) -> parts == ['44332211', '00000000'], idx 1 -> 0\n\n*This is the padded-column trap this project already records, in a new place: the label matched, so every check that looked for the right line passed, and the CONTENT came from the wrong column.*\n\n**REPAIR: dump from `(addr & ~0xF)` for 16 bytes, so all four groups are always present -- which is what the index arithmetic already assumed.  One line.**\n\n*** SCOPE, AND IT IS WIDER THAN THE ONE RED ROW: 8 OF 10 LEGS CHANGED VALUE WHEN THE INSTRUMENT WAS REPAIRED. ***\n\n| leg | table 4 | table 5 |\n|---|---|---|\n| `BASE` | 31/32 | **32/32** |\n| `HEAD` | 19/32 | **17/32** -- the pre-fix build was failing MORE rows than reported |\n| `RESTORED` | 19/32 | 17/32 (= HEAD) |\n| `M_ENTRY` | 26/32 | 27/32 |\n| `M_MASK1` | 28/32 | 29/32 |\n| `M_PBITCOND` | 30/32 | 31/32 |\n| `M_POP_ONLY` | 25/32 | 23/32 |\n| `M_PUSH_ONLY` | 25/32 | 26/32 |\n| `M_MASK7`, `M_NULL_MEDIUM` | unchanged | unchanged |\n\n**The defect was in the probe AS ORIGINALLY BUILT**, so it affected every `read_mem32` call at a nonzero 16-byte offset -- the STM and Thumb PUSH clobber rows -- across tables 1 through 4.  **Every number those rows produced before this table is superseded.**  *Grepped: no other probe in the tree shares the shape, so the blast radius is this round's instrument alone.*\n\n=== THE FLOOR, NOW CLEAN ===\n\n| clause | leg | score | killed on |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| -- | `BASE` | **32/32** | **nothing -- clean** |\n| (a) pre-fix red | `HEAD` | 17/32 | the defect rows |\n| (b) reinstatement | `RESTORED` | 17/32 | = HEAD exactly |\n| (c) null medium | `M_NULL_MEDIUM` | 8/32 | 24 rows |\n| **(d)** | `M_MASK1` | 29/32 | the three `P2` rows |\n| **(d)** | `M_MASK7` | 30/32 | the two `BIT2` rows |\n| **(d)** | `M_PBITCOND` | 31/32 | **`THUMB_PUSH_COLD_DATA_P2` alone -- the minimum** |\n| de-escalation | `M_ENTRY` | 27/32 | **all five writeback rows, nothing else** |\n\n**Every mutant dies on exactly the cells written for it, and `M_PBITCOND` on precisely one.**  *The class closure holds under a correct instrument, which is the thing table 4 could not establish.*\n\n*A small one of mine, recorded because I spent today cataloguing this class: my own shell check for a clean BASE tested `[ -z \"$B\" ]`, but the probe prints `FAILING: none` when clean -- a NON-EMPTY string -- so the check took its else branch and printed 'BASE still red on: none'.  The score `32/32` is what settled it.  A check that reports the opposite of the truth on a green run is the same family as the ones I filed against other people's scripts today.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PROMOTED TO regress/ AND WIRED INTO gate_arm.sh -- 32/32 AGAINST THE GATE'S OWN BINARY. ***\n\n**This discharges the obligation the round could not previously claim.**  Fable's adjudication found that *neither of today's probes is under `regress/` proper, so 'shipped a detector with the fix' is not yet true of either round.*  For `#524` it is true now.\n\n=== THE PROMOTION IS A COPY, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE ===\n`regress/artifacts/armbdt/agy_armbdt_witness_20260911.py` **stays in place as the AS-RUN record of tables 1-5**; `regress/arm_bdt_probe.py` is the copy that evolves and that the gate executes.  *Editing the artifact copy would falsify what those tables actually measured* -- the same reason the repaired kill-table driver was written as a new file rather than over the old one.\n\n**The promoted copy differs only by two PRINT-ONLY lines (`ARMBDT_CONTROL=`, `ARMBDT_RESULT=`, matching the gate idiom the other ARM probes use) and a header note.  Verified: the `record()` count is unchanged, so no row was added or lost in the copy.**  Its header also carries the `read_mem32` alignment warning, so the next probe copied from it does not inherit [[probereadalign]].\n\n=== MEASURED AGAINST THE GATE'S OWN BINARY, NOT THE SCRATCH CLONE ===\n`$PMAX` is `build/gxemul`.  After the propagate+rebuild, the promoted probe run directly against it returns **`SCORE 32/32`, `ARMBDT_CONTROL=OK`, `ARMBDT_RESULT=32/32`, `FAILING: none`**.  *A pre-check worth the thirty seconds: wiring the probe in BEFORE the rebuild would have run it against a pre-fix binary and produced a red gate that meant nothing about the fix.*\n\n=== THE GATE SECTION, AND WHY THE DENOMINATOR IS A LITERAL ===\nFollows `arm_writeback_probe.py`'s shape exactly: run -> `gate_skip` if no result line -> print the ok/FAIL rows -> check the control -> **`check \"bdt rows run\" \"$bwant\" 32`** -> check correct == run.\n\n> **The denominator is pinned to a LITERAL deliberately.  The probe grew 25 -> 29 -> 32 rows across three kill tables as clause-(d) mutants were closed, and a gate that trusts the probe's own denominator cannot tell a probe that LOST rows from one that never had them.**\n\nThe section also records all five measured-dead mutants with their scores and the row family that kills each (`M_ENTRY` 27/32 writeback rows, `M_MASK1` 29/32 the `+2` rows, `M_MASK7` 30/32 the bit-2 rows, `M_PBITCOND` 31/32 Thumb PUSH `+2`, `M_NULL_MEDIUM` 8/32), **so a future reader meets the evidence at the gate rather than having to find the artifacts.**\n\n*Targeted gate run in flight.  Nothing committed; the owner's hold stands until it is green.*",
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\recordfix_20261013\adj_20261013\ledger_HEAD.json:9180:     "note": "COMPILED AND BOOTED BOTH RIGS, and overturned the brief, the other six seats' consensus, and one of my own statements to the owner. Private build in its own tmp tree; GXEMUL-SEC/est/build/gxsec-build all verified untouched, git status clean, no gate run.\n* Q1 -- THE SIX-SEAT CONSENSUS 'DENOMINATOR UNSOUND' IS TOO STRONG. 220 REPRODUCES EXACTLY at 84f442d, the HEAD it was recorded on; it is 215 today because #433 removed the five m8820x exits. The record is STALE BY ONE ROUND, not wrong. What does NOT survive is the 114 (115 today, 119 at its own HEAD) and the 17-init bucket (undercounts by ~8: it counted only the DEVINIT() macro form and missed longhand dev_*_init).\n* Q1's REAL FINDING, invisible to every pattern in the brief: misc.h:190 CHECK_ALLOCATION -> :180 FAILURE -> exit(1), **183 such sites in src/devices/, 5 inside a DEVICE_ACCESS handler** -- incl. dev_wdc.c:873 malloc(512 * count) with a GUEST-SET count. Total is 215 direct + 183 macro = 398 terminating call sites, 120 in-handler.\n* AND THE TEXTUAL COUNT IS ANTI-CORRELATED WITH THE DEFECT: 87 of 302 matches are comments or identifiers, and 22 are prose written by THIS PROJECT'S OWN FIX ROUNDS (dev_8253.c:143 '#223: warn+ignore, don't exit() the host', footbridge:387, kn02ba:117/161, vga:896). The raw count GROWS every time a site is fixed.\n* Q2 -- 'mostly' resolved: 50 of 82 helper sites genuinely DEVICE_ACCESS-reachable, 31 not, 1 tick-only. Corrected guest-reachable total 115+50+5 = **170**, not 114. States what its method cannot see: function pointers (dev_lca.c:73/85 are interrupt callbacks, guest-ADJACENT not access-reachable).\n* Q3 -- **THE BRIEF WAS WRONG THAT ONLY LUNA88K IS REPRODUCIBLE: landisk IS a bootable rig** (gate_crossfamily.sh:199 boots openbsd76-landisk-bsd.rd), and dev_sh4.c carries 21 in-handler + 7 reachable-helper sites. HONEST NEGATIVE RESULT: it did NOT demonstrate a kill there -- all eight one-value guards in sh4_pcic_access are written by the healthy guest with exactly the hardcoded constant on every boot (598 accesses), and -M 32/64/128 are byte-identical because machine_landisk.c:84 hardcodes 64 MB regardless of -M (predicted from source first, then measured).\n* Q4 -- BITS 25..0 ARE PROVABLY DEAD IN THE MODEL: luna88k_interrupt_assert sets bit (line+25) and devinit registers lines 1..6 ONLY, so interrupt_status can only hold bits 26..31 and reassert computes status & enable. No bit below 26 can affect any interrupt decision. So store-as-is and store-masked are behaviourally identical for delivery, and 'return without storing' is the ONE option that differs materially -- it leaves the old mask in force while the guest believes its SPL changed. Recommends store-as-is: masking would INVENT a hardware behaviour the tree has no source for. luna88k_board.h's INT_SET_LV0..LV7 are DEFINED AND NEVER READ -- the TIMER_EXTERNAL trap a second time -- though their nine architected values all have bits 25..0 zero. No LUNA-88K manual exists locally, so the HARDWARE meaning is an honest UNKNOWN.\n* Q5 -- HEAT MEASURED, AND THE LATCH IS NOW MANDATORY BY MEASUREMENT: INT_ST_MASK0 takes **4,192,908 writes in 320 s** and 3,895,294 of them inside the 130 s boot => ~29,960/s during boot, 37% of every access to the device. The fatal() string is 76 bytes, so unlatched = **2.28 MB/s, ~296 MB per boot**, 6.2x the 4,849/s SCR rate already ruled cflood. And writes_with_LOW_BITS_SET = **0 of 4,192,911**: the exit sits on the COLDEST branch of the HOTTEST register -- the #433 shape exactly. Boot vacuity-checked (LUNA-88K BOOT x1, M88100 x1, login: x1).\n* Q5 -- AND IT KILLED THE BRIEF'S OWN DETECTOR: 0x00000001 is the WRONG probe value, because the read path is odata = currentMask >> 8, so idata bits 7..0 SHIFT OUT ENTIRELY and the row cannot distinguish store-as-is from store-masked from not-storing. Use a bit in 25..8 and two writes with different architected prefixes. Plus four more vacuity paths, incl. a mutant that latches once then exits on the SECOND hit, which passes any single-store row.\n* Q6 -- exitsweep must EXCLUDE dev_sh4.c:193 (the brief cited :191, which is the fatal(); the exit is :193). It is tick-only, reached from the SIGALRM handler via timer.c:283, so it is not even in exitsweep's class; timer.c:275-279 ALREADY records it. Landing the generic shape first makes it WORSE -- it keeps the unsafe fatal() and ADDS a plain-int latch written from both the handler and the main loop, a data race sigunsafe must then undo. The right idiom already exists: timer.c:65's volatile sig_atomic_t.  [panel_20260817_112300]",
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\recordfix_20261013\adj_20261013\ledger_HEAD.json:9750:     "note": "VERDICT: SHIP, 117 KB -- the largest answer of the panel.",
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\recordfix_20261013\adj_20261013\ledger_HEAD.json:11374:     "note": "CHECKED RATHER THAN ARGUED, because the m88k panel split on this row and I recorded that it was checkable. The answer is SPLIT and both halves matter.\n\nHALF REFUTED: the filing says the spy 'counts CALLBACK calls'. It does not merely count. diff_m8invread.c:117-133 captures the RECEIVER, the ADDRESS and the FLAGS of every call, and derives inv_all_were_ALL / inv_all_addr_zero / inv_cpu_mismatch from them. Its own comment names exactly the hazard the row raises -- 'a detector that counts calls and ignores their arguments is green for any argument -- the constblind shape, and mutant A walks straight through it'. So the count-only weakness was already found and fixed by an earlier round, and the row's premise is stale on that point.\n\nHALF CONFIRMED, AND IT IS THE HALF THAT MATTERS: the detector observes the CALLBACK, never the resulting STATE. `grep -n 'patc\\|PATC' regress/diff_m8invread.c` returns NOTHING -- no row inspects the PATC at all. So a mutant that reaches (or fails to reach) the same invalidation by ANY route other than the callback is invisible to every row in the file. That is the row's essential claim and it stands.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE SPLIT: the two packet-fed seats ranked this first on vacuity grounds and they were right that a gap exists; the two file-reading seats ranked m8patc first as the only SEMANTIC fix, and that is untouched by this. Both readings survive -- what is now settled is that the gap is REAL, so if this row is worked the round is 'assert the PATC state, not only the callback', not 'stop counting calls'.",
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\recordfix_20261013\adj_20261013\ledger_HEAD.json:11376:     "ev": "regress/diff_m8invread.c:117-133",
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\recordfix_20261013\adj_20261013\ledger_HEAD.json:16257:     "note": "MEASURED at rung 3. A legal single-byte counter load in LSB-only mode produces ZERO hz recomputes and timer0 is never created or retimed; dev_8253.c:117-121 breaks before the hz block. The IDENTICAL byte through MSB-only gives `counter 0 set to 13312 (90 Hz)`. The 16-bit arm needs nothing -- it routes its recompute through MSB, correctly -- so this is a DIFFERENT defect from i8253zero, in a different arm, and must not be folded into it.",
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\recordfix_20261013\adj_20261013\ledger_HEAD.json:17865:   "note": "\n\n*** 2026-09-11 ASSESS (codex GPT-6 Astra at max, 12,716 b).  THE DEFECT IS REAL AND THE SEVERITY AS FILED IS REFUTED. ***\n\nVerdict: *Disabled-state accrual exists, but **the backlog is BOUNDED and drains through repeated acknowledgements.  DISABLED TIME EARNS NO TICK.***\n\n**SO THE ROW IS WRONG IN TWO WAYS.**  It says a disabled timer *accrues ticks forever* and then *delivers the whole backlog at once*.  Neither holds:\n  * `footbridge_pending()` (`dev_footbridge.c:114`) **RESETS the counter to one at `:117` when its bound is reached** -- so the accrual is bounded, not unbounded;\n  * **disabled time earns no tick at all**, so nothing accumulates in proportion to how long the timer was off.  The backlog drains through repeated acknowledgements rather than arriving as one burst.\n\n**WHAT IS CONFIRMED:** the accrual path is real and unguarded.  `reload_timer_value()` (`:329`) creates or re-frequencies a timer with one of `timer_tick0..3` (`:122`), **and those four callbacks call `footbridge_pending()` UNCONDITIONALLY** -- there is no enable check on that path.  The increment is `dev_footbridge.c:119`.\n\n*** QUESTION 0 -- AND THIS ONE I GOT RIGHT, WHICH IS WORTH RECORDING GIVEN THE NIGHT: **arm X of `fbpending_drain_probe.py` EXISTS and the filename attribution is CORRECT.** ***  It writes the disabled control value at `:218-221`, re-enables at `:237-240`, and is invoked with `disable=True` at `:584`.\n\n**BUT THE ROW OVERSTATES WHAT THAT BUYS: the arm exists and its ORACLE does not fit.**  *Its acceptance criteria currently REJECT the clean zero-backlog result*, so they must change before the arm can serve this row.  **A reproduction scenario is not an acceptance test**, and the hold text conflated them -- which is why the row read as cheaper than it is.\n\n*Scope held honestly: the permitted files do not establish the empty `witness` field, the ranking behaviour, the committed history, or that a reproduction has ever been run successfully.*"
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\recordfix_20261013\adj_20261013\ledger_HEAD.json:17928:   "note": "\n\n*** 2026-09-11 -- MISCLASSIFIED ENTIRELY: THE FIX SHIPPED AS #474, TWO WEEKS AGO. ***\n\nThe `state_note` reads *NOT FIXED: needs a build for the detector ... queued behind the embargo with the other build-blocked rows*.  **Both halves are false.**  `src/devices/dev_footbridge.c:681-687` carries the `/* #474: a READ must not perform the clear. */` tag; `CHANGELOG.md:11749` heads the round block; two kill tables sit under `regress/artifacts/fbclearread/`.\n\n**The row\"s own `witness` field, corrected 2026-08-27, already says the fix shipped with a measured detector** -- so the row contradicts itself, and the half a reader meets first is the wrong one.\n\n**THE REAL RESIDUAL IS AN UNDECIDED OWNER POLICY, already written in its `hold_question`:** does a fix shipped on rung-1 (offline differential) evidence alone need an OWNER EXEMPTION, or must it wait for a rung-3 probe?  **THREE rounds sit on that question** -- `b115` (#472), this one, and its sibling.\n\n**CLASSIFICATION: STILL BLOCKED, DIFFERENT REASON -- and this is NOT a blocker rewrite, it is a STATE CORRECTION.**  A row recorded held / no commits / NOT FIXED on work that shipped is the `es438` class the ledger\"s own semantics block warns about, **and it must not ride in as a one-line text edit.**"
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\recordfix_20261013\adj_20261013\ledger_HEAD.json:18603:   "state_note": "*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: TRUE, HARNESS-ONLY, and ALREADY DOCUMENTED AND FIXED IN-TREE -- in THREE places in the same gate. Recommend CLOSE. ***\n\n**The mechanism:** `stdbuf` works by `LD_PRELOAD`ing `libstdbuf.so`, which then precedes the ASan runtime in the initial library list; ASan rejects that startup order and aborts with *'ASan runtime does not come first in initial library list'*. The host process dies at launch, **before the guest CPU powers on**, so there is no guest observable at all.\n\n**All three already in `regress/gate_asan_sweep.sh`, verified by reading:**\n  * `:104-105` -- *\"verify_asan_link_order=0 is REQUIRED, not cosmetic. `stdbuf` works by LD_PRELOADing libstdbuf.so, which then precedes the ASan runtime...\"*\n  * `:117` -- the remedy: `export ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0:abort_on_error=0:print_summary=1:verify_asan_link_order=0`\n  * `:115` -- **the second hazard, recorded verbatim**: *\"ASan silently ignores `no_such_asan_flag_xyz=0` and runs normally.\"*\n\n*** THAT SECOND ONE IS THE SHARPER FINDING AND IT IS ALREADY WRITTEN DOWN: because this ASan silently ignores bogus options, ANY check that validates `ASAN_OPTIONS` by asking whether ASan ACCEPTS them is vacuous. *** It answers identically for a valid and an invalid flag -- the check-that-cannot-fail shape, in the environment rather than in a script. The gate knew and said so.\n\n*Ninth row today describing work already done or already recorded. See [[baretitle]] -- the queue's dominant defect is stale entries, not undiscovered bugs.*\n\n"
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\recordfix_20261013\adj_20261013\ledger_HEAD.json:20961:   "state_note": "*** SCOPE CORRECTION 2026-08-27: THE DEFECT IS THE **WRITE HALF ONLY**, and saying the window 'bypasses the MMU ENTIRELY' reintroduces wording this row's title was already corrected away from. ***\n\n**Why the unqualified form is wrong:** the round's own `sq miss evt` detector observes a **read-type TLB miss (`0x040`)** on the fixed build, so the read side is NOT unrouted. `memory_sh.c:290-301` carries the corrected scope.\n\n> **THE CORRECT STATEMENT: no initial-page-write exception and no write-side protection check on stores into the SQ window.** The read/prefetch path translates; the store path does not. That asymmetry IS the defect, and it is narrower and more defensible than 'bypasses the MMU'.\n\n**AND THE SECTION CITATIONS ARE BOTH RIGHT, FOR DIFFERENT THINGS -- reconciled here so the next reader does not have to choose:**\n  * **section 3.6.7** *defines* the Initial Page Write exception and its code: *\"occurs when the D bit is 0 even though a UTLB entry contains address translation information matching the virtual address to which a data access (write) is made... Sets exception code H'080 in EXPEVT.\"* (`sh7750_hardware.txt:9918-9928`)\n  * **section 4.7.4, 'SQ Protection'** *applies* it to the store queues: *\"Write type exception judgment is performed for writes to the SQs... a TLB miss exception, protection violation exception, or initial page write exception is generated.\"* (`:11742-11748`)\n**The code cites 4.7.4; I cited 3.6.7. Cite BOTH -- 4.7.4 is the one that makes it apply to this window, and 3.6.7 is the one that says what the exception is.**\n\n**Ranking, from today's adjudication: 7th of 8.** Latent -- no known guest depends on SQ write-protection. *It is a fidelity gap, not a live corruption, and my earlier 'most severe row in the batch' was severity within that batch, not across the queue.*\n\n*** INDEPENDENTLY RE-VERIFIED 2026-08-27, and the step that could have KILLED it -- whether SH-4 exempts the store queue from translation -- goes the OTHER WAY. The manual refutes the exemption. (Already documented in-source and filed; today's work is re-verification.) ***\n\n**THE BYPASS IS UNCONDITIONAL.** `memory_sh.c:353-357`:\n```c\n/*  Store queue region:  */\nif (vaddr >= 0xe0000000 && vaddr < 0xe4000000) {\n        /*  Note/TODO: Take SH4_MMUCR_SQMD into account.  */\n        *return_paddr = vaddr;\n        return 2;\n}\n```\n**No `MMUCR.AT` test, no UTLB walk, no PR/D check**, and it returns 2 (read/write). `cpu_sh.c:106` is the only assignment of `translate_v2p`, and the sole SH consumer of `0xe0000000` in `src/cpus/` is `pref_rn` -- **no store instruction special-cases the window**, so every `mov.l Rm,@Rn` into it takes this branch. The preceding arm (`:339-350`) matches neither, so the path is identical with AT=1 and AT=0.\n\n*** THE MANUAL SETTLES IT AGAINST THE EXEMPTION, from three directions: ***\n  * **section 4.7.4, SQ Protection** (`sh7750_hardware.txt:11742-11748`): *\"When MMU is on -- Operation is in accordance with the address translation information recorded in the UTLB, and MMUCR.SQMD. **Write type exception judgment is performed for writes to the SQs** ... a TLB miss exception, protection violation exception, or **initial page write exception** is generated.\"*\n  * **section 4.7.3** (`:11691-11696`): *\"the ASID, V, SZ, SH, PR, and D bits have the same meaning as for normal address translation.\"*\n  * **QACR is explicitly the MMU-OFF-only mechanism** (`:11719-11723`, `:10473`), so it is NOT an alternative translation path under AT=1.\n\n**AND PRIVILEGE DOES NOT RESCUE IT.** The Data TLB Protection Violation table (`:12632-12640`) gives `PR=00` as **\"Only read access possible\"** in PRIVILEGED mode, with EXPEVT `0x0A0` read / `0x0C0` write at `:12650`.\n\n*** THE ASYMMETRY IS THE CLEANEST STATEMENT OF THE DEFECT: THE READ SIDE IS TRANSLATED AND THE WRITE SIDE IS NOT. *** The flush path at `cpu_sh_instr.c:3706-3765` explicitly checks `SH4_MMUCR_AT` and calls `sh_translate_sq_v2p()` -> `translate_via_mmu()`. The store path does none of it.\n\n**WHY THE SHIPPED DETECTOR CANNOT SEE THIS, and it is not a detector defect:** `regress/sh_halt_probe.py:590-599` pins `sq pr00 evt` and `sq d0 evt` to **NOEXC deliberately** -- those rows exercise the read-type PREF path, where no protection or dirty case exists. **No row exercises the STORE side at all**, so the detector is silent here rather than contradicting the finding.\n\n**ADJACENT, DISTINCT, ALREADY FILED:** the `SQMD` TODO on that same branch is a SECOND gap -- section 4.7.4 requires an ADDRESS ERROR for a user-mode SQ access when `MMUCR.SQMD = 1` even when translation succeeds, and the store path checks neither. `pref_rn` does check it (`cpu_sh_instr.c:3709-3715`) but raises `EXPEVT_RES_INST` instead -- filed as `sh4sqexpevt`.\n\n*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: TRUE and GUEST-REACHABLE. A store into the store-queue window BYPASSES THE MMU, so page protection is not enforced on it. The most severe row in this batch. ***\n\n**The mechanism:** the SQ write path does not go through `sh_translate_v2p()` / `translate_via_mmu()` (`memory_sh.c:353-357`, `:203-241`, with the source's own commentary at `:289-303`), so a store into the SQ window lands directly in the device's `sq` array.\n\n**Guest access sequence, fully specified by the seat:**\n  1. guest kernel enables paging (`MMUCR.AT = 1`);\n  2. guest maps the SQ virtual page `0xe0000000` in the UTLB **valid** (`V=1`) and either **clean** (`D=0`) or **read-only** (`PR=00`);\n  3. guest issues `mov.l r0, @r1` with `r1 = 0xe0000000`.\n\n**Expected on hardware (SH7750 sections 4.7.4 / 3.6.7):** an **Initial Page Write** exception (`EXPEVT = 0x080`) on `D=0`, or a **Data Write Protection** exception on a read-only page.\n**Actual here:** the store simply succeeds.\n\n> **So a guest that maps a page read-only, or clean-for-dirty-tracking, and then stores through the SQ window gets a write that hardware would have faulted.** That is protection and dirty-bit tracking silently not enforced -- not a fidelity nit.\n\n**Observable:** the guest `EXPEVT` register after the store -- `0x080` (or the write-protect code) when correct, unchanged when broken. Readable by an ordinary guest load, so no new probe capability is needed.\n\n**CHECKABLE AGAINST A PRIMARY SOURCE WE HAVE:** the seat cites SH7750 sections 4.7.4 and 3.6.7, and `_scratchpad/sh7750_hardware.txt` is present and greppable. **Confirm the two sections before the round** -- and read the PROSE, not a figure, per the extraction rule.\n\n\n\n*** PROMOTED BY THE CODEX RATING PASS, 2026-09-07, ON A ROW IT WAS NOT ASKED ABOUT -- and the\npromotion is the answer to the brief's Q3, *what does my reading order miss?* ***\n\n*\"Promote `sh4sqwrite` ahead of the latent ASIC proposals.  Its title sounds like missing tests,\nbut `memory_sh.c` (anchor `Store queue region:`) grants read/write access directly, and `dev_sh4.c`\n(anchor `DEVICE_ACCESS(sh4_sq)`) accepts the writes into queue storage.  Hardware requires\nwrite-type exception checks on stores into those queues.\"*  (Renesas SH7750 manual, S4.7.4.)\n\n> **THAT IS A GUEST-REACHABLE MISSING PROTECTION CHECK, not a coverage gap** -- and it was invisible\n> to my keyword scan because the TITLE reads like harness debt.  *\"No known guest depends on it\"\n> does not establish unreachability.*\n\n**And the row's existing witness cannot carry it:** `sq pr00 evt` and `sq d0 evt` in\n`regress/sh_halt_probe.py` exercise **PREF's READ-type judgement**, so they cannot witness a missing\nSTORE-side check.  A witness for this row has to drive a store.\n\n*Filed as the strongest single finding of that pass; it needs a `sev` and a round of its own.*",
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\recordfix_20261013\adj_20261013\ledger_HEAD.json:20985:   "state_note": "*** FIRST EVIDENCE 2026-08-27, from a farmed codex job. The mask arithmetic is CONFIRMED exactly; the SEVERITY is lower than the seat implied, and the reason is a phrase in the manual the seat did not weigh. ***\n\n**CONFIRMED, and recomputed here from the header's own constants rather than transcribed:** `dev_sh4.c:1020-1021` builds the ITLB data-array-1 mask with `SH4_PTEL_PR_MASK` = **0x60, a TWO-bit field**, giving 0x1ffffdfa. Narrowing PR to the ITLB's own `SH4_ITLB_DA1_PR` = 0x40 gives 0x1ffffdda. Both values match the seat digit for digit. **The extra bit is 0x20.**\n\n**THE PRIMARY SOURCE AGREES, and it was read as PROSE, not off a figure** (`sh7750_hardware.txt:10035-10036`, section 3.7.2):\n  *\"In the data field, PPN is indicated by bits [28:10], V by bit [8], SZ by bits [7] and [4], PR by bit [6], C by bit [3], and SH by bit [1].\"*\n**Bit [6], singular** -- so for the ITLB, bit 5 is not part of PR. The sibling `sh4_utlb_da1` at `:1220-1224` keeps the two-bit mask and is CORRECT: the UTLB's PR really is [6:5].\n\n*** BUT THE SAME FIGURE'S LEGEND (`:10062`) SAYS RESERVED BITS ARE \"0 write value, UNDEFINED READ VALUE\" -- SO RETURNING 0x20 IS ARCHITECTURALLY PERMITTED, NOT WRONG. *** That single clause is what moves this from 'defect' to 'fidelity nit', and no seat quoted it.\n\n**THE SEAT'S LOAD-BEARING PREMISE -- 'no translation decision uses that bit' -- IS TRUE, BUT ONLY VIA A TWO-STEP CHAIN IT DID NOT GIVE, AND I FIRST SCORED IT FALSE.** The single consumer is `memory_sh.c:163`, `pr = (lo & SH4_PTEL_PR_MASK) >> SH4_PTEL_PR_SHIFT` -- a TWO-bit read, and `lo` **can** be `itlb_lo` (`:120-122`). That looks like the bit reaching a decision. It does not, because:\n  1. `:114-117` -- ITLB entries are walked **only** when `FLAG_INSTR` is set;\n  2. `:195-200` -- the instruction arm tests **only** `if (!(pr & 2))`, i.e. `pr` bit 1, which is `lo` bit 6, the REAL PR bit.\nBit 5 becomes `pr` bit 0, which that arm never reads. The data-side `switch (pr)` at `:206`/`:219` DOES separate 0/1/2/3 -- but the ITLB never reaches it.\n\n> **THE LATENT COUPLING, which is the part actually worth carrying:** the bit is inert because of ONE arm's specific test, not because of any structural guarantee. An edit making instruction protection 'more complete' -- replacing `if (!(pr & 2))` with a `switch (pr)` mirroring the data path -- would make a guest-stored reserved bit LIVE, flipping pr=2 into pr=3. Narrowing the store mask would make the inertness structural. *That is the real argument for the change, and it is a defence-in-depth argument, not a correctness one.*\n\n**NOT CHANGED, and the cost is why.** Six SHIPPED rows in `#452`'s detector pin the current mask -- R3 and A4i in `sh4_tlbda2_probe.py` (`:37`, `:251`), C1 in `sh4_tlbda2_witness.py` (`:126`) -- all keyed off `ITLB_MASK = ... | _PR | ...  # 0x1ffffdfa` (`probe:71`, `witness:53`), which the probe uses as a **per-device signature** (`:68`) to tell ITLB from UTLB. The ADDITION LICENCE wants a measured delta or a transferred warrant; the manual supplies neither, since it makes the present read value legal. And the ORACLE-CORRECTION RULE requires re-pinning those rows FIRST and measuring the committed build RED under the new expectations **before** touching code -- which needs a build. **Correctly queued behind the machine embargo, not skipped.** (The signature property would survive a narrowing: 0x1ffffdda vs the UTLB's 0x1ffffdff still differ.)\n\n*Apparatus note, against my own checking rather than the seat's: my first grep for the header constants used `[ \t]` in ERE, which is the set {space, backslash, t} and does NOT match a tab -- so it returned NOTHING and I nearly scored the seat unverifiable. Second instance of the same class today, after the `0x3e0` literal-vs-symbol miss on `sh4pcic15`.*\n\n",
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\recordfix_20261013\adj_20261013\ledger_HEAD.json:24244:     "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260826_085454/ollama_deepseek.txt (7,117 chars). PACKET-FED, self-declared.",
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\recordfix_20261013\adj_20261013\ledger_HEAD.json:28072:     "note": "[NOT A SEAT ANSWER] (#534 R2: was the stage-scoped no-panel token, which this entry never meant)  *** AND THE REASON THIS TOKEN IS HERE IS ITSELF THE FINDING: I RECORDED THIS VERIFICATION AS `seat: opus5' AND IT PROMOTED landiskaux's assess stage FROM A ONE-SEAT FILING TO 2/9, REDDENING BOTH STAGE GATES -- one hour after `#532' shipped the token for exactly that class, against its own author. ***  No panel has been convened on `landiskaux'; pass 1 is about to run.  The reading below is MAIN-LOOP work, not a seat answer, and crediting it to opus5 would have manufactured a review that never happened -- the same defect `#532' found in `gen_dashboard.py' this morning, arriving from the authoring side instead of the rendering side.  *The lesson is not `remember the token'.  It is that the entry-writing step has no check on whether a `seat' field names something that actually answered, and the gate can only see the count.*\n\n*** EVERY FACT UNDER THIS ROUND RE-VERIFIED INDEPENDENTLY BEFORE ITS PANEL, and the one that mattered most was NOT in the row: the proposed address could have COLLIDED with the main mapping, and nothing had checked. ***\n\n  1. `dev_wdc.c:2098` switches on `devinit->machine->machine_type` with exactly THREE cases -- MACPPC (`+0x160`), HPCMIPS (a hardcoded `0x14000386`, guarded on `addr == 0x14000180`) and IQ80321 (`+0x402`).  No MACHINE_LANDISK.  CONFIRMED.\n  2. `src/include/device.h:53` declares `int addr_mult`, and `dev_wdc.c` uses it at `:1174` (`relative_addr /= d->addr_mult`), `:2083`, `:2123`.  The prescribed form is well-formed, not invented.  CONFIRMED.\n  3. `machine_landisk.c:87-88` builds the device string `\"wdc irq=... addr_mult=2 addr=0x14000000\"`.  **`addr_mult=2` read from the string itself**, not inferred from the 0x2c consistency.  CONFIRMED.\n  4. `machine.h:268` defines `MACHINE_LANDISK 6004`.  CONFIRMED.\n\n*** TWO THINGS THE ROW SAID LOOSELY, CORRECTED HERE BECAUSE A FUTURE READER WILL GREP FOR THEM AND FIND NOTHING: ***\n  * The row says landisk *falls through to the default*.  **There is no `default:` arm.**  The switch ends at `:2110` with a bare `}`.  The value comes from a PRE-SWITCH initialisation at `:2095`, `alt_status_addr = devinit->addr + 0x206`.  The substance is right and the mechanism is not what the word `default' implies -- and the distinction matters to the fix, because a `case` that falls out of the switch without assigning simply keeps `:2095`'s value.\n  * `alt_status_addr` is NOT part of the main device mapping.  `:2120` registers a SEPARATE TWO-BYTE device, `\"wdc_altstatus\"`, at that address; `:2122` registers the main one at `devinit->addr` for `DEV_WDC_LENGTH * addr_mult` bytes.\n\n*** THE COLLISION CHECK NOBODY HAD RUN, and it is the reason the second point above is worth the space: *** `DEV_WDC_LENGTH` is **8** (`dev_wdc.c:45`), so landisk's main wdc device spans `8 * 2 = 16` bytes, `0x14000000..0x1400000f` -- which is exactly the range `machine_landisk.c:86`'s own comment claims (`port 0x14000000-0x1400000f`).  The proposed altstatus lands at `0x14000000 + 0x16 * 2 = 0x1400002c`, i.e. **0x2c = 44 >= 16, comfortably beyond the main span: NO COLLISION.**  Had the multiplier been larger, or `DEV_WDC_LENGTH` bigger, the fix would have registered a two-byte device INSIDE an existing one.\n\nAND THE DEFECT ITSELF IS CONFIRMED ARITHMETICALLY: today landisk gets `0x14000000 + 0x206 = **0x14000206**` while the OpenBSD 7.6 driver writes **0x1400002c** -- so the twelve measured Device Control writes land on memory no device claims.  The fix closes exactly that gap.\n\n*STILL OWED BEFORE THE EDIT, and not skipped: the round's `witness` is `none`, so per the 2026-08-27 lifecycle ruling ONE seat must be asked to try a rung-3 probe before this is recorded as rung-1.  `DEVINIT` is invisible to the offline differential, but that is an argument about one instrument, not a demonstration that no guest-path witness exists -- and the last time that ask was made, a seat built one in a single invocation.*",
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\recordfix_20261013\adj_20261013\ledger_HEAD.json:30359:   "state_note": "[2026-10-09, queue 7n-2, Fable 5.1 + GPT-6 Astra] CLAUSE (c): by construction via regress/gate_arm.sh's sgi_re_width section, leg NOT run -- it pins rows emitted / green / red at 46 / 46 / 0 by EQUALITY, and live64 / devsig read 'died' on a dead session.  Astra: a non-emulator that exits 0 leaves the six 'sw surv' rows green (they test only exit status), so a dead run scores 6/46 -- and the equality pins still reject it.  The probe's '# ---- controls / identity (c)' label is a TRUE by-construction claim.  ||  OPEN, and the committed artifact in `witness` is the REPRODUCTION, not the fix.  `regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py` shipped with #497 and carries SIX `ld kill` rows that assert THE HOST STILL DIES on an 8-byte access at each of the six windows -- they pin the defect, they do not repair it.  #497 de-fatalised the WIDTH guard only; the width test is evaluated FIRST, so a legal `ld` still reaches the kill.  *** THE ORDERING OBLIGATION IS ALREADY RECORDED IN #497's ROUND BLOCK AND IS THE REASON THIS ROW MUST NOT BE CLOSED BY THE CHECKER: fixing sgirekills will turn a GREEN GATE RED BY SUCCEEDING, six rows' worth. ***  It therefore inherits the oracle-correction ordering -- re-pin the six rows FIRST, measure the committed build RED under the new expectations, THEN edit the code.  A row whose own detector must invert is exactly the case the shipped-work heuristic cannot read.\n\n*** COUNT CHECK 2026-09-06, AND MY FIRST CORRECTION WAS ALSO WRONG.  THE LIVE COUNT IS TWELVE. ***\n\nThree numbers have now been attached to this row: the title says TWENTY-ONE, my correction an hour ago said\nNINETEEN, and the live count is **TWELVE**.\n\n    grep -c 'exit(1)'                    19   <- ALL occurrences, comments included\n    grep -cE '//[[:space:]]*exit\\(1\\)'    4   <- commented-out kills (:218, :227, :689, :1251)\n    (three more sit inside comment PROSE at :321, :325, :945, discussing exit(1))\n    grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'    12   <- LIVE, statement position\n\n*** THE INSTRUCTIVE PART IS WHY I BELIEVED 19, BECAUSE IT IS A NEW COROLLARY OF A RULE THIS PROJECT ALREADY\nHAS. ***  I wrote that `grep -c 'exit('` and `grep -c 'exit(1)'` BOTH returned 19, and treated the agreement\nas ruling out the very error that had just made `lcabadval`'s enumeration wrong -- a commented-out kill\ncounted as live.  **Both greps share the same blindness: neither excludes `//`.**  The carrier says when two\nderivers DISAGREE neither is trustworthy until you look at what each matched.  The corollary, learned here:\n**when two derivers AGREE that is not evidence either, if they can fail the same way.**  Agreement between\nmethods is worth exactly the independence of the methods -- which is the unanimity-on-a-brief-supplied-fact\nrule, one level down and pointed at greps instead of seats.\n\nAnd it is the third instance tonight of *a correction is not safer than the thing it corrects*: `#503`'s\npass-3 refutation displaced a truer sentence, `83df95c` sharpened a vague claim while reading as though it\nfixed a false one, and this correction replaced a wrong number with another wrong number.\n\n**THE TITLE IS LEFT AS WRITTEN AND IS NOT SILENTLY RE-GRADED**: 21 may have been right when the row was\nopened, with sites removed since -- four of the nineteen are commented out, which is consistent with someone\nhaving disabled them.  A round on this file DERIVES the count from the statement-position pattern above\nbefore acting, and does not trust any of the three numbers in this row.\n\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-07 (triage stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nL4 -- TITLE CORRECTED.  The state_note has carried the derived count of **TWELVE** since 2026-09-06, and the\nTITLE still said TWENTY-ONE.  **A note that corrects a title does not correct the title**, and the title is\nwhat every queue listing, dashboard row and triage pass shows first -- so the corrected figure was sitting\none field away from the wrong one, losing every time.\n\nOld title, kept because the quote-what-you-refute rule applies to my own records too:\n  \"dev_sgi_re.c carries 21 live terminating calls -- 15 of them inside a DEVICE_ACCESS and 13 in DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re) alone -- and the six READ arms are all guest-reachable\"\n\nRe-derived at HEAD before this edit: `grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);' src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c' = **12**,\nagainst 19 raw hits.  The new title NAMES ITS DERIVER, per the counts rule, so the next reader re-runs it\ninstead of trusting it -- which is the whole remedy for a class that has now had three generations on this\none row.\n\n**The DEVICE_ACCESS half of the old title (`15 of them inside a DEVICE_ACCESS ... 13 in DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)\nalone') IS DELIBERATELY NOT REPLACED WITH A NEW NUMBER**, because [[exitcensus]] is open against exactly that\nattribution: the census's construct split *\"attributed every call in a plain non-static function to the\npreceding DEVICE_ACCESS -- inflating the bucket used to rank reachability\"*.  Quoting a fresh figure out of a\ntool with an open defect against the very field I am quoting would be the same error in a newer coat.  The\nreachability claim is left to the round that does the work.\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-07 (assess stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nPREP (no code touched) -- the RE-PIN DESIGN, taken from the sibling that already did this rather than\ninvented.  `#499' re-pinned the neighbouring `other kill' row in this same probe when it removed the\ncatch-all kill, and its comment records that **BOTH obvious oracles were MEASURED VACUOUS before the third\nwas chosen**.  That is a transferable warrant, not a suggestion, because this row inverts the same way.\n\n**WHAT THE SIX ROWS ASSERT TODAY** -- `row(nm + \" ld kill\", how + (\"+txt\" if ok_txt else \"-txt\"),\n\"exit1+txt\")', i.e. the host DIES and the per-site literal `TODO: read from <sym>' appears.  Fixing the six\nread kills turns all six RED **by succeeding**, which is why the row carries the ordering obligation.\n\n**THE TWO ORACLES `#499` MEASURED VACUOUS -- do not re-propose either:**\n  * **DEATH-ONLY** (`how == \"exit1\"'): observes THAT it killed, never WHICH kill.  A message change leaving\n    `exit(1)' standing scored **35/35** against a matched control at 34/35.  Filed as [[otherkilldeath]].\n  * **`exit0`**: exit status cannot separate *declined, then quit* from *died with status 0*.  The variant\n    `mexit0' scores GREEN on an `exit0+txt' oracle while `alive=False'.  Filed as [[exit0oracle]].\n\n**THE THREE-CHANNEL SHAPE THAT SURVIVED, which the six should copy:**\n  * `how' -- the host survives;\n  * `txt' -- **ONE CONTIGUOUS literal including the address and data fields.**  Two independent substring\n    tests are a WEAKER claim: `-- declined.' is emitted by the six `#497' width declines and by `#498's\n    sgi_de decline too, so a bare substring cross-matches.  These six already have per-site symbols, which\n    is the natural discriminator;\n  * `v0' -- **a declined READ reaches the common tail and lands a DEFINED 0.**  `v0' is preset to SENTINEL\n    by `load()', so *wrote odata (0)* and *never landed* are distinguishable.  This is the channel that kills\n    `mret0' (return 0 -> memory_rw coerces to -1 -> EXCEPTION_DBE) and `mrdexit0' (exit(0) in the READ branch\n    only), **neither of which any write-only oracle can see** -- and these six ARE read arms, so this channel\n    is not optional here.\n\n**ORDER, per the oracle-correction rule:** re-pin the six FIRST, measure the COMMITTED build RED under the new\nexpectations, THEN edit `dev_sgi_re.c'.  `#499' additionally showed the stronger form -- its oracle had been\nPRE-REGISTERED six hours before its own baseline -- and this row inherits that: the design above is being\nwritten down BEFORE the round starts, in a dated entry, for exactly that reason.\n\n**Cite the sites by SYMBOL, never by line.**  The probe's own header lists `dev_sgi_re.c lines 245 262 279\n296 346 362'; `#504' inserted ~40 lines above them, so those numbers are already stale.  The probe carries a\n`SITES' table of symbolic names -- use it.\n\n*** CENSUS + RANKING, 2026-09-07 (codex, `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/codex_census_20260907.txt`).\nTHE ANSWER TO 'WHICH ARE CHEAP' IS 'NONE ARE', AND THAT IS THE USEFUL PART. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"The count is twelve.  ELEVEN ARE GUEST-REACHABLE; one is unreachable.  **There are no\ntwo or three genuine one-line repairs already covered by success tests.**  The cheapest plausible\nfunctional pair is LINEAR_A and LINEAR_B readback -- but both need corrected oracles.\"*\n\n**The count is CONFIRMED at twelve** (I re-ran the deriver before writing the brief; the title's 21\nand the intermediate 19 both counted comments).  **Eleven of twelve guest-reachable** is a stronger\nseverity statement than this row carried.\n\n*** I ASKED FOR THE CHEAP WINS AND WAS TOLD THERE ARE NONE.  Recorded as a NEGATIVE RESULT, because\nit stops the next round hunting for them. ***\n\n**THE COST RANKING, which gives a coherent round instead of a scattering:**\n\n    rank  site                        new latch bit   work\n    1     #12 dead MTE width branch   no              source cleanup only -- NO RUNTIME IMPROVEMENT\n    2=    #5  LINEAR_A read           no              replace the kill oracle with readback\n    2=    #6  LINEAR_B read           no                assertions; extend EXISTING sessions\n    3=    #1  TLB_A read              no              reuse existing sessions; add returned-value,\n    3=    #2  TLB_B read              no                packing and cell-selection assertions\n    3=    #3  TLB_C read              no\n    4=    #7  STIPPLE                 ONE             new targeted MTE rows AND session scenario\n    4=    #9  linear COPY             ONE             same\n    4=    #10 linear depth            ONE             same\n\n**RANKS 2 AND 3 ARE ONE CLASS: the five READ arms.**  No new latch bits, no new sessions, and they\nshare the oracle inversion this row already predicted -- the six `ld kill` rows in\n`regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py` that assert the host still dies.  **That is the round to run**, and it\nis the class-closure shape rather than a cell at a time: `#505` paid three floor breaches for\nrepairing cell-by-cell, and this row's sites are already grouped for me.\n\n**Rank 1 agrees with the separate `sgimtedepth4dead` audit**, which independently said DEFER and\nreduce that row to a comment correction -- two briefs, two seats, same disposition, reached from\ndifferent directions.\n\n**RANK 4 IS THE EXPENSIVE GROUP AND SHOULD NOT BE MIXED IN**: one latch bit each plus new MTE\nsessions.  Those are three separate rounds, not part of the read-arm round.\n\n*** GROK DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07.  I ASKED IT TO ASSUME THE THREE `lcabadval` ERRORS WERE REPEATED\nHERE.  ALL THREE WERE. ***\n\n**1. THE CLASS COUNT IS WRONG IN THE HEADLINE, FOR THE FOURTH TIME ON THIS ROW.**  `grep -cE\n'^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'` gives 12 STATEMENTS, but `:1537` **cannot run**: it is the trailing `else`\nof `if (len == 4) ... else if (len != 4) ... else { fatal; exit(1); }` in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_mte)` --\nan exhaustive pair with a third arm.  Already filed as [[sgimtewidth]].\n\n> **ELEVEN guest-reachable kills, in two classes: 6 DIRECTION + 5 MTE-feature.**  This row has now\n> carried 21 -> 19 -> 12 -> 11, and every correction was the same comment-or-reachability blindness.\n\n*Two more shapes sit beside the MTE five and are NOT among the twelve: `dst_y_step` fatals with its\n`exit` commented out, and the `dst_tlb` `default:` fatals and CONTINUES.  Same unimplemented-feature\nclass, different severity.  CLIP_IDS at `0x16e0` is a seventh NAMED RE window but is the `#499`\ncatch-all, not a seventh read kill.*\n\n*** 2. MY S3 WAS THE FLOOR-(a) MISS I ASKED ABOUT -- AND THE INSTRUCTION IN IT IS WRONG. ***\nI wrote that *\"a guest `lw` of each register kills the host on HEAD, so the rows are RED pre-fix by\nconstruction\"*.  **A `lw` does not kill: width is checked FIRST and `len != 8` DECLINES.**  The\nshipped detector already holds the counter-example -- `load()` defaults to `OP_LW_V0_AT` and every\n`lw surv` row wants `exit0/0x0`.  **The kill is `ld` (len = 8).**\n\n> **So a detector of six `lw` reads would be GREEN ON HEAD** -- precisely the miss I claimed this row\n> was immune to, arrived by a different door.  *The asymmetry I asserted between this row and\n> `lcabadval` was real in direction and wrong in fact.*\n\n**AND THE `lcabadval` ANALOGY IS THE WRONG CLASS ANYWAY:** those are WRITE VALUE-GUARDS, where legal\nwrites already survive; these are DIRECTION kills on a legal-width READ.  That difference is why S3\ncould look like the opposite of the `lcabadval` miss until `lw` is put in the sentence.\n\n**3. THE CITED PRECEDENT IS A FILED DEFECT, AND I CITED THE WRONG PROBE TOO.**\n  * S2 claimed *the model already stores the right thing* for all six.  **For TEX that warrant IS\n    [[sgitexindex]]**: `re_tex` is written with `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff`, consecutive\n    `sd`s overlap by three, 81 of 112 cells are unreachable, and there are **zero consumers**.\n    Backing state exists; the correct answer does not.\n  * S4 cited `sgi_re_rgb_probe.py` -- which is uncommitted `#505` work that **never issues an RE\n    register read**.  The committed vehicle is `sgi_re_width_probe.py`, whose six `ld kill` rows ARE\n    the witness -- **and they invert the day this round succeeds.**\n  * I also mis-cited `#508` for a packing hazard; `#508` is `alphapfnoffset` (the page walk dropping\n    the offset).  The packing hazard here is real and is a different defect.\n\n*** THE DESIGN THAT SURVIVES: FIVE ACCEPT, TEX SPLITS, MTE STAYS OUT. ***\nOne SEMANTIC class (a legal `ld` into an implemented window dies) but TWO members:\n  * **TLB_A/B/C and LINEAR_A/B -> member 4 ACCEPT.**  Stores already land and consumers exist\n    (`horrible_getputpixel` for the three TLBs and LINEAR_A; the MTE linear fill for LINEAR_A/B).\n    One round, one detector, five value rows.\n  * **TEX -> DECLINE, do NOT mirror.**  A mirrored `>> 3` read is the identity only for *write one\n    slot, read it back, touch nothing else*: a store at `+8` writes cells `[1..4]` and one at `+16`\n    writes `[2..5]`, so a mirrored read at `+8` returns a MIX.  **Mirroring also plants a green\n    round-trip that `sgitexindex` must later invert.**\n\n**AND A FIVE-ARM ROUND MAY NOT CLAIM \"the RE read arms no longer kill\"** -- the contract has to name\nTEX as still fatal.\n\n**THE ROWS ACTUALLY OWED, which is not \"six survival rows\":**\n\n    5 packed `ld' round-trips, one per ACCEPT arm            floor (b)/(d), packing-sensitive\n    the same five at a slot with bit 3 SET *and* at the       near AND far bound; `+8' alone is\n      window's LAST 8-byte slot                               the `#497' write-accept shape\n    5 pre-write `ld' -> 0                                     the memset ABI, distinct from\n                                                              \"the stored value came back\"\n    TEX: a 3-channel decline (how / contiguous TODO or new    a different member\n      latch text / sentinel -> 0)\n    RE-PIN all six existing `ld kill' rows FIRST              oracle-correction: today they want\n                                                              exit1 + text\n\n*Survival-only is enough for verbatim `exit(1)` reinstatement and NOT enough for the null-repair.*\n\n*** THE grok DESIGN ANSWER WAS UNREAD FOR A DAY, AND IT REFUTES FIVE OF THE BRIEF'S PREMISES --\nINCLUDING ONE THAT WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A DETECTOR THAT IS GREEN ON HEAD.  Read in full and\nSPOT-CHECKED 2026-09-07; artifact `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/grok_design2_20260907.txt`. ***\n\n**This is the fourth recorded instance of the unread-review class in this project, and by some\nmargin the most consequential: the answer names a floor-clause-(a) failure in the detector the round\nwas about to build.**\n\n### 1.  *** THE ACCEPT ROWS WERE ABOUT TO USE THE WRONG INSTRUCTION.  VERIFIED, NOT TAKEN ON TRUST. ***\n\nThe brief's S3 said *\"a guest `lw` kills\"*.  **It does not.**  Measured on the tree today:\n\n    regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py:196   def load(A, addr, op=OP_LW_V0_AT):      <- defaults to `lw'\n    src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:421,440,459    if (len != 8) {                     <- WIDTH CHECKED FIRST\n\nThe `len != 8` arm DECLINES, so a `lw` never reaches the direction kill.  **The kill is `ld`\n(len == 8).**  An accept suite written with `lw` rows would be **GREEN ON HEAD** -- vacuous by\nconstruction, floor clause (a), the detector-that-cannot-fail shape.  grok put it exactly: *\"Counting\n'one read per register' as six `lw`s gives a detector that is green on HEAD.\"*\n\n### 2.  THE COUNT IS WRONG FOR THE FOURTH TIME, AND THIS TIME THE DERIVER IS INNOCENT\n\n    1537|   if (len == 4) {\n    1542|   } else if (len != 4) {        <- the EXACT NEGATION of the if\n    1550|   } else {\n    1552|           exit(1);              <- PROVABLY UNREACHABLE\n\n`grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'` returns **12**, and it is right: there are twelve\n*statements*.  **One of them cannot run**, so there are **11 guest-reachable kills**.\n\n> **THE DERIVER RULE'S OWN LESSON, LANDING ON THE ROW THAT ALREADY EMBODIES IT.**  This title was\n> corrected twice for counting comments (21 -> 19 -> 12) and the deriver was added precisely so the\n> number would be re-derivable.  **It still says LIVE, and no text pattern can test reachability** --\n> so the deriver measures a CORRELATE of the class, which is the failure mode the rule names.  A\n> census keyed on what defines the class has to ask whether the arm can execute, and that is a\n> reading, not a grep.\n\n**AND TWO ROWS OF THIS QUEUE DISAGREE ABOUT ONE ARM.**  `sgimtewidth`'s own title calls `:1552`\n*\"its dead arm\"* -- so the knowledge was already in the ledger, under a different id, while this row\ncounted the same statement as live.  *Not a new fact; a fact filed where this row does not look.*\n\n### 3.  THE CITED IN-FILE PRECEDENT IS ITSELF A FILED DEFECT\n\nS2 claimed *\"the model already stores the right thing\"* for all six arms.  For TEX that warrant **is\n`sgitexindex`**: `re_tex` is written with `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff`, consecutive `sd`s\noverlap by three, 81 of 112 cells are unreachable, and there are ZERO consumers.  *Backing state\nexists; the correct answer does not.*  So TEX cannot be member 4 (ACCEPT) on today's stores.\n\n### 4.  THE BRIEF CITED THE WRONG PROBE AND THE WRONG ROUND NUMBER\n\n* S4 cited `sgi_re_rgb_probe.py`.  That is **uncommitted `#505` work that never issues an RE\n  register read at all.**  The committed vehicle is `sgi_re_width_probe.py`, whose six `ld kill`\n  rows **are** the witness -- and they must be **RE-PINNED FIRST**, because today they want\n  `exit1+txt` and a correct fix turns them red.  That is the oracle-correction rule's ORDER\n  obligation, and citing the width probe as an accept suite treats a filed obligation as discharged.\n* `#508` is `alphapfnoffset`, a different defect.  The packing hazard it was cited for is real; the\n  citation is not.\n\n### 5.  WHAT THE ROUND SHOULD BE (grok's design, recorded as the seat's position)\n\n**Five ACCEPT (TLB_A/B/C, LINEAR_A/B) + TEX DECLINE, latched, falling through to the tail.**  MTE\nstays OUT: different function, different trigger, different member, different detector.\n\n*A five-arm ACCEPT that leaves TEX fatal **cannot honestly take the headline** \"the RE read arms no\nlonger kill\" -- one of six still does.*\n\nThe packing is the whole substance of the fix, and a naive version is a null repair:\n\n    TLB_A/B/C   uint16_t[256], i = ((relative_addr & 0x1ff) >> 1) & 0xff, FOUR BE halfwords\n                odata = (tlb[i]<<48) | (tlb[i+1]<<32) | (tlb[i+2]<<16) | tlb[i+3];\n                naive `odata = d->re_tlb_a[i]' keeps 16 of 64 bits\n    LINEAR_A/B  uint32_t[32],  i = ((relative_addr & 0x7f) >> 2) & 0x1f, TWO words\n                odata = ((uint64_t)lin[i] << 32) | lin[i+1];\n                naive form drops [i+1]\n\n**Do NOT mirror `>> 3` for TEX**: a mirrored read is identity only for write-one-slot-read-it-back;\na store at `+8` writes `[1..4]` and one at `+16` writes `[2..5]`, so a mirrored read returns a mix --\nand it plants a green round-trip that `sgitexindex` would later have to invert.  **Do not widen the\nTEX window toward `+0x100` either: `CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0` is `CRIME_RE_CLIP_IDS`, already `#499`.**\n\n### 6.  THE CLAUSE-(d) MUTANTS IT ENUMERATED, all of which a SURVIVAL row leaves green\n\n    delete exit(1), never assign odata          -> host lives, guest reads 0\n    odata = d->re_tlb_a[i]                      -> host lives, 16 of 64 bits\n    LINEAR without [i+1]                        -> host lives, high word wrong\n    ACCEPT only at the window BASE              -> green while the probe ld's at base\n    shrink one window into the #499 catch-all   -> host lives, 0, catch-all text\n\n> **The oracle for the five ACCEPT arms is the VALUE, not `alive`** -- ACCEPT is a success-effect\n> site.  Use a pattern whose every halfword lane is load-bearing (`0x1122334455667788`, already\n> `ACC_VAL`); a value with zero lanes hides a 16-bit pack.  **TEX is the refusal-effect case** and\n> takes the `#499` three-channel shape (survival + contiguous announce + sentinel -> 0), not a\n> round-trip.  The in-tree proof that survival at a refusal site is vacuous is `#497`'s\n> `d_returnzero` 35/35 escape.\n\n**Site omission is the expected mutant and is why the detector needs SIX rows, not five:** leaving\nLINEAR_B's `else` fatal keeps every other row green.\n\n### 7.  RESIDUALS IT RAISED (filed, not actioned here)\n\n* **`:1404` must NOT be merged with `sgimtedepth4dead`.**  `depth = 8 << field` is 8/16/32/64 and\n  never 4; the dead `if (depth == 4)` is on the TILED path.  *\"Fixing depth == 4 alone would mask\n  the depth-32 leak and leave depth 16.\"*\n* Two further shapes sit beside the MTE five and are in NEITHER class: `dst_y_step` fatals with its\n  `exit` commented out, and `dst_tlb`'s `default:` fatals and CONTINUES.\n* Opening LINEAR reads makes `sgilinearoob` observable from a guest (`re_linear_a[32]` aliases\n  `re_linear_b[0]`) -- a residual witness, **not** a `put d` reproduction.\n* **The witness obligation for the read class is ALREADY DISCHARGED:** the six `ld kill` rows are\n  rung-3 at all six windows on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32`.  What remains is oracle-correction\n  ORDER, not building a probe.\n\n*The brief also carried a stale HEAD (`7a41b3f` against the checkpoint's `a264a34`), which the seat\ncaught and worked around by verifying the arms in the tree rather than trusting the packet.*\n\n*** THE codex ROUND DESIGN, 2026-09-08.  It SPLITS the round, corrects a false correction of mine,\nand names a gate coupling that would have turned the battery red. ***\n\n### 1.  THE ROUND SPLITS: FIVE ACCEPT NOW, TEX DECLINE SEPARATELY\n\ngrok's design joined them.  codex refuses the join **using this project's own scope rule**: the five\nACCEPT sites share one contract (*\"reconstruct the complete stored value\"*) while TEX DECLINE is a\ndifferent one (*\"completed read returning zero, a specific diagnostic, and independent once-only\nsuppression\"*).  *\"Putting both contracts in one Python file does not establish 'same detector, no\nnew oracle'.  Your scope rule also requires ONE FIX DESIGN.\"*\n\nAnd it refuses the other direction too: **do not wait for the TEX index repair**, because that\n*\"unnecessarily couples read termination to backing-state corruption\"* -- and *\"declining TEX does\nnot close `sgitexindex`\"* either.\n\n### 2.  THE PACKING, with the index expressions stated against the ADJUSTED `relative_addr`\n\n    TLB_A/B/C   uint16_t H[256]   i = ((r & 0x1ff) >> 1) & 0xff   four halfwords, highest first\n    LINEAR_A/B  uint32_t W[32]    i = ((r & 0x7f)  >> 2) & 0x1f   two words, highest first\n    TEX         (separate round)  no array read; leave odata == 0, announce via its own latch\n\n### 3.  *** A GATE COUPLING THAT WOULD HAVE TURNED THE BATTERY RED.  VERIFIED HERE. ***\n\n`regress/gate_arm.sh:1181` names **all six `ld kill` rows by label** in its one-line row list, and\n`:1185-1190` **pins a 35-row denominator in BOTH halves** (35 emitted AND all 35 green, deliberately\ntwo checks so a LOST row is distinguishable from a FAILING one).\n\n> **So re-pinning those rows in the probe WITHOUT editing the gate leaves the gate RED.**  codex:\n> *\"A successful probe edit can otherwise leave the gate red.\"*  **The round owes a gate edit\n> alongside the detector edit**, and the gate's own comments at `:1178-1180` warn about precisely\n> this family -- an unsatisfiable check whose row quietly stops being tested, and a row list that\n> once gained a bogus word and shipped a red gate.\n\n### 4.  *** C4 WAS A FALSE CORRECTION AND I RECORDED IT WITHOUT CHECKING. ***\n\nI wrote that `sgi_re_rgb_probe.py` is *\"uncommitted `#505` work\"*, on grok's say-so.  **It is\ncommitted** -- `git ls-files` lists it, last changed in `7a41b3f`.  One command would have settled\nit and I ran none.\n\n**grok's OTHER half survives and is the load-bearing one:** the RGB probe writes RE state and\nobserves framebuffer memory *without reading RE registers*, so `sgi_re_width_probe.py`'s six\n`ld kill` rows remain the relevant witness.  *A correction can be half right, and the half I\nrepeated was the checkable half.*\n\n### 5.  C2, C3 AND C5 CONFIRMED, each with a better derivation than mine\n\n* **C2** -- 12 active statements, inspected by enclosing control flow: **six RE read exits, five in\n  `do_mte_transfer`, one unreachable** in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_mte)`, whose chain really is\n  `len == 4` / `len != 4` / `else`.  **11 reachable exit sites file-wide, six in the RE handler** --\n  *\"source-derived reachability, not eleven fresh demonstrations\"*, which is the honest label.\n* **C3** -- all 28 aligned TEX positions enumerated: starting indices 0-27, touched cells 0-30, so\n  **31 touched and 81 untouched**, and no functional consumer of `re_tex`.\n* **C5** -- the ordering is right, but **its scope depends on the round**: for five ACCEPT sites,\n  re-pin those FIVE and leave TEX's existing expectation until its own round.  The baseline RED\n  measurement under the revised expectations is still owed.\n\n### 6.  A SEVENTH NAMED RE REGION -- and the distinction it forces\n\n`CRIME_RE_CLIP_IDS` (`crmfbreg.h:234`, `0x16e0`) is a named region with **no dedicated handler arm**;\nit falls into the existing catch-all.  Verified: zero occurrences in `dev_sgi_re.c`.  **So \"six\ndedicated read-kill ARMS\" is correct and \"only six named RE REGIONS\" would be false.**\n\n*** THE STANDING RULINGS THAT GOVERN THIS ROW, COPIED IN 2026-09-08 -- because the round before this\none was designed against a ruling that lived only in the CHANGELOG, and the fix for that\n(`rulingnotinrow`) is to put the disposition WHERE THE DECIDER LOOKS. ***\n\n**1.  THIS ROUND IS SANCTIONED.**  The 2026-09-02 flagship adjudication ranks it:\n\n> *\"2. `sgirekills` (high, open) -- named for honesty, not scheduled here: the six RE read arms are\n> the same live-kill severity and **the natural next round**.\"*\n\n**There is no do-not-code here** -- the opposite.  *(I checked because the previous round was\nstopped by one, not because I expected one.)*\n\n**2.  WHAT THE \"SIX-WINDOW SINGLE ROUND REFUSED\" RULING ACTUALLY FORBIDS, and it is NOT this row.**\n\n> *\"Q2.1 six-window single round -- REFUSED.  It bundles at least three semantic classes (read-exit\n> de-fatalisation, index scale, OOB bounds), which the round-scope rule forbids.  The file's own\n> comment already partitions those windows across named rows: `sgirekills`, `sgireunmapped`,\n> `sgilinearoob`, `sgidewidth`, `sgirelatchenum`.\"*\n\n**The refusal is of a round bundling ACROSS THOSE ROWS.**  `sgirekills` alone is ONE class --\nread-exit de-fatalisation -- and is permitted.  *That also independently vindicates the codex design\nanswer, which split TEX out for a different reason (its ACCEPT depends on `sgitexindex`, which is\nunder a DO-NOT-CODE).*\n\n**3.  THE ORDERING OBLIGATION IS ALREADY RECORDED AND IS NOT OPTIONAL.**  From the `#497` block:\n\n> *\"**Fixing `sgirekills` will turn a green gate RED by succeeding**, six rows' worth, so it inherits\n> the oracle-correction ordering -- re-pin first, measure the committed build red, then edit.\"*\n\n**And codex independently found the mechanism:** `regress/gate_arm.sh:1181` names all six `ld kill`\nrows by label and `:1185-1190` pins a 35-row denominator in BOTH halves.  **So the round owes a GATE\nEDIT alongside the detector edit**, or a successful fix leaves the battery red.\n\n> **THE ROUND, as it now stands: five ACCEPT (TLB_A/B/C, LINEAR_A/B) with the packed inverse of each\n> store loop; TEX DECLINED and latched rather than ACCEPTed, because its index repair is barred; the\n> six `ld kill` rows RE-PINNED FIRST and measured RED on the committed build; and `gate_arm.sh`'s row\n> list and denominator updated in the same commit.**\n\n*** THE RE-PIN TEMPLATE IS ALREADY IN THE FILE, MEASURED, WITH ITS VACUOUS ALTERNATIVES NAMED.\n`#499` did this exact re-pin one arm over (`other decl`, `sgi_re_width_probe.py`), and its comment\nis the round's instructions. ***\n\n**IT PINS THREE THINGS, and the comment says why each is load-bearing:**\n\n    how   the host SURVIVES the access\n    txt   ONE CONTIGUOUS literal including the address and data fields.  *\"Two independent\n          substring tests are a weaker claim\"* -- `-- declined.` is emitted by the six `#497`\n          width declines AND by `#498`'s sgi_de decline, so a bare substring cross-matches.\n    v0    the access REACHES THE COMMON TAIL and lands a DEFINED value.  **`v0` is preset to\n          SENTINEL by `load()`**, so \"wrote odata\" and \"never landed\" are distinguishable.\n\n*** AND IT NAMES THE ORACLES IT MEASURED VACUOUS, WHICH IS WHAT SAVES THIS ROUND A TABLE: ***\n\n* **`how` ALONE IS VACUOUS.**  Variant `mexit0` -- reinstate the death as `exit(0)` -- prints the\n  announce and then dies, and *\"exit status CANNOT distinguish 'declined, then quit' from 'died with\n  status 0'\"*.  The `exit0+txt` oracle scored GREEN with `alive=False`.  Filed as `exit0oracle`.\n* **A WRITE-ONLY oracle misses two more:** `mret0` (`return 0` -> `memory_rw.c` coerces to -1 ->\n  EXCEPTION_DBE) and `mrdexit0` (`exit(0)` in the READ branch only).  **Only the `v0` channel sees\n  them.**\n\n**THE ORDER PROOF THAT ROUND USED IS THE ONE THIS ROUND OWES:** its new oracle was measured RED on\nthe COMMITTED pre-fix build BEFORE the code changed, and it was PRE-REGISTERED six hours before the\nbaseline -- *\"not fitted to the fix either\"*.\n\n> **SO THE PLAN, and every element of it is now sourced rather than invented:**\n> 1. **RE-PIN the six `ld kill` rows FIRST** -- they are generated by ONE loop at\n>    `sgi_re_width_probe.py:269-274` all wanting `exit1+txt`.  Five become value round-trips\n>    (`how`/`v0` against the packed inverse of each store loop); TEX becomes a DECLINE\n>    (`how`/contiguous-literal/`v0 == 0`), because its ACCEPT is barred by `sgitexindex`'s\n>    DO-NOT-CODE.\n> 2. **MEASURE THE COMMITTED BUILD RED** under the new expectations -- same script, opposite\n>    colour -- and preserve that artifact.\n> 3. **ONLY THEN** edit `dev_sgi_re.c`: five ACCEPT arms packing four BE halfwords (TLB) or two\n>    words (LINEAR), TEX declined and latched.\n> 4. **EDIT `gate_arm.sh` IN THE SAME COMMIT** -- `:1181` names all six `ld kill` rows by label and\n>    `:1185-1190` pins a 35-row denominator in BOTH halves, so a successful fix leaves the gate RED\n>    otherwise.\n> 5. Panel pass 2 on the diff; the flagship stages stay HELD for the 09-09 adjudication.\n\n*** THE STEP-1 RE-PIN PANEL REFUSED MY ROW SET AND SUPPLIED A BETTER ONE.  grok, 2026-09-08:\n\"do not write the proposed six-row re-pin.\" ***\n\n**TWO FLOOR-SHAPED HOLES IN WHAT I PROPOSED**, plus one oracle this file had already measured wrong:\n\n1. **The read-side FAR BOUND is unpinned.**  A row at the window BASE tests the near bound twice and\n   the far bound never -- the accept-row rule, which I QUOTED IN THE BRIEF and then did not apply to\n   my own row set.\n2. *** THE TEX TRIPWIRE WOULD HAVE BEEN VACUOUS, and this is the sharpest thing in the answer: a\n   LOAD-ONLY decline row wants v0 == 0, and a future ACCEPT round returning the ZEROED ARRAY also\n   yields 0.  The row could not redden when TEX later becomes ACCEPT. ***  That is exactly the\n   question I asked as Q3 and could not answer.  **The fix is to STORE a non-zero value first**, so\n   an ACCEPT would return it and the row goes red.\n3. **exit0 is the wrong oracle** and #499 measured why: an exit(0) reinstatement prints and dies,\n   scoring green with the process dead.  **Survival is implied by the VALUE.**\n\n*** AND MY Q2 WAS NOT AN OPEN QUESTION. ***  I asked whether an anchored form makes one row per arm\nenough instead of two.  **#499 already measured on this same device that it does not.**  Asking it\nas open was a failure to read the record I was otherwise citing.\n\n### THE ROW SET TO WRITE -- 14 rows replacing 6\n\n**FIVE ACCEPT ARMS, TWO ROWS EACH (window base and last slot):**\n\n    tlb_a ld lo    0xb5001000    store VAL_LO then ld    v0 == VAL_LO, TODO-text ABSENT,\n                                                         store debug re_tlb_a[0] = ... present\n    tlb_a ld hi    0xb50011f8    store VAL_HI then ld    same shape, debug re_tlb_a[252] = ...\n    tlb_b, tlb_c   +0x200/+0x400                         the same pair; indices still 0 and 252\n    linear_a lo/hi 0xb5001700 / 0xb5001778               debug re_linear_a[0]/[1], [30]/[31]\n    linear_b lo/hi 0xb5001780 / 0xb50017f8               debug re_linear_b[0]/[1], [30]/[31]\n\n**VAL_LO may stay 0x1122334455667788; VAL_HI MUST DIFFER IN EVERY HALFWORD LANE** (e.g.\n0xa1b2c3d4e5f60718), so a lane-swapping edit cannot round-trip.  **Want the SAME 64-bit value that\nwas stored**, not a separately invented packed encoding -- *inventing one would re-implement the\npacking inside the test and grade the code against my arithmetic instead of against the store.*\n\n*** THE ANCHOR IS THE STORE'S OWN debug(), WHICH NAMES THE INDEX. ***  That is what makes these rows\nABSOLUTE rather than relative: re_tlb_a[252] = ... pins WHICH CELL, so a translation that slides the\nwindow reddens.  **And no new debug() goes on the read path** -- the store's existing one is the\nexternal channel, and adding a print would be an unlicensed addition.\n\n**TEX -- FOUR ROWS, decline, and NOT the #499 template:**\n\n    tex ld lo     store VAL_LO at 0xb5001600 then ld    v0 == 0x0, ONE CONTIGUOUS TEX-read-decline\n                                                        literal, TODO-text absent\n    tex ld hi     the same at the last slot 0xb50016d8  same, and the TEX literal -- NOT the\n                                                        catch-all unimplemented-read literal\n    tex rd lat1   two ld's at two different offsets     TEX-read literal count == 1\n    tex xbit      fire the other five width latches     TEX-read literal still appears exactly once\n                  first, then a TEX ld\n\n*The xbit row is the latch-collision shape #490 measured in another file, applied here before it can\nbite.*\n\n*** PASS 2: SIX SEATS, ALL SIX ANSWERED, THREE SAID DO NOT SHIP -- AND TWO OF THE THREE WERE\nRIGHT. ***\n\n    codex     DO NOT SHIP   an L2 FLOOR BREACH, with a predicted score and its mechanism\n    grok      DO NOT SHIP   records; and it re-measured every claim against the tree\n    glm       DO NOT SHIP   FALSE ALARM -- but it named the settling fact instead of guessing\n    kimi      SHIP, cond.   the enum record -- a real condition, now met\n    deepseek  SHIP\n    minimax   SHIP\n\n## THE L2 -- a clause-(d) floor breach, repaired in-round\n\n`tex xbit` fires the six WIDTH latches only, so it sweeps bits 0-5.  codex named the escape:\n\n    SGIRE_W_TEX_RD = 1 << 11  ->  1 << 8  (= SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD)      PREDICTED 44/44\n\nand gave the reason from the detector's own session schedule -- **the four TEX-read scenarios and\nthe unmapped-read scenario never share an emulator instance**, so bit 8 is clear whenever TEX is\nread.  *\"M10 establishes detection of a width-bit collision, not every existing-bit collision\"* --\nwhich is exactly the over-generalisation my CHANNEL table had made in a parenthesis.\n\n**Repaired with two SAME-INSTANCE rows, `tex/unmap` and `unmap/tex`, in BOTH orderings** (one\nordering would leave the mirror open -- the `#499` shape).  Gate raised 44 -> 46 in the same edit.\n\n## THREE WRONG RECORDS, ALL MINE, ALL CORRECTED IN PLACE\n\n* **the enum comment** -- *\"The next free bit is 1 << 11; the enum ends HERE on purpose\"* went false\n  twice over when this diff took `1 << 11`.  *** THE FILE DOCUMENTS THIS EXACT FAILURE TEN LINES\n  ABOVE WHERE I EDITED *** -- the identical claim on `SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP` went false when\n  `SGIRE_W_BADMODE` was added below it, and that comment records both the failure and the repair.\n  **I repeated a documented failure while editing the enum that documents it.**  Found by kimi,\n  confirmed independently by codex and grok.\n* **my LINEAR comment** claimed the `len == 8` guard \"keeps tlbi even\".  **Width does not imply\n  alignment** -- the guard tests LENGTH; CPU-side alignment is what keeps it even, and the OLDER\n  surrounding comment says so correctly.  My new wording was worse than the one it sat beside.\n* **the probe's contract header** still described six live read kills and row names this round\n  deleted -- clause (e), the thing a future reader trusts about what the detector claims.\n\n## *** A CLAIM OF MINE WAS CONFOUNDED, AND I HAD REPEATED IT ALL EVENING ***\n\nI said `M8_texaccept` measures the store-first TEX design.  codex: **M8 also removes the announce**,\nso its four failures do not isolate the store-first contribution -- both channels red under it.\nIsolating it needs a mutant that converts TEX to ACCEPT while KEEPING the announce.  *The\nstore-first design is still right for the reason grok gave at design time; what is not established\nis that M8 measured it.*\n\n## THE `odata` GAP -- three seats, independently\n\ncodex, glm and grok each found the same missing cell in my (d) enumeration.  **Coverage is\nunaffected** -- `M2_datafill` IS that mutant and it kills -- but the ENUMERATION could not have\ngenerated it.  Corrected in `PRETABLE.out` as an L4.\n\n## WHAT glm'S FALSE ALARM ACTUALLY FOUND -- a defect in MY BRIEF\n\nIt reasoned from HUNK BOUNDARIES that `check \"sgi_re controls live\"` sits in no hunk while the\ncontrol count went 2 -> 3, and enumerated two ways that could break the gate.  Reading the line\nsettles it: the check greps `OPUS497T_CONTROL=OK`, does not count controls, and the probe emits\nthat token over all three.  grok reached the same resolution independently and refused to ratify\nthe inference.\n\n> **A DIFF-ONLY PACKET OMITS UNCHANGED CONTEXT BY CONSTRUCTION, so a seat asking \"is the\n> surrounding code still consistent with this change?\" -- which is what pass 2 is FOR -- cannot\n> see it.**  That is the sketch rule one level up: the sketch rule says inline the enclosing\n> FUNCTION for a proposed edit; this says inline the enclosing BLOCK for a reviewed one.\n\n*Not proposed as a general rule on one instance: the seat named its settling fact, the cost was one\n`sed`, and a brief that inlines every adjacent block is a brief nobody reads.*\n\n*** THE L2 REPAIR IS MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED.  L2VERIFY.txt, scratch clone, frozen instrument. ***\n\n    BASE_pass2        46/46   the repaired detector, CLEAN\n    M13_latchcollide  44/46   KILLED   SGIRE_W_TEX_RD 1<<11 -> 1<<8 (= SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD)\n\n**46 - 44 = exactly TWO red rows -- precisely `tex/unmap` and `unmap/tex`, the two the repair\nadded, with nothing else perturbed.**  That is the cleanest possible signature for a targeted\nrepair: the new rows catch the named escape and no existing row's score moved.\n\n*** ONE THING THIS DOES NOT SHOW, and it is stated rather than glossed: the panel PREDICTED 44/44\nagainst the PRE-REPAIR 44-row detector, and that prediction was NOT re-measured. ***  The 44-row\nform no longer exists once the two rows are added, so the escape itself is a PREDICTION with a\nmechanism (the four TEX-read scenarios and the unmapped-read scenario never share an instance;\n`tex xbit` sets bits 0-5 only), not a measured score.  **What is measured is that the repaired\ndetector kills it.**  Re-measuring the escape would mean rebuilding the 44-row detector to prove a\nhole I have already closed -- and the mechanism is checkable by reading the probe's session list,\nwhich is how the panel derived it.\n\nClone left `cmp`-CLEAN against SEC with no sentinel.\n\n*** THE FOURTH RECORDED INSTANCE OF THE UNREAD-REVIEW CLASS, AND THE MOST EXPENSIVE: IT COST AN\nENTIRE EXTRA PASS.  Mine, on pass 2, found by grok on pass 3. ***\n\nPass 3 opened with four seats saying DO NOT CLOSE THE CHAIN, and grok's answer contained the line:\n\n> *\"The grok pass-2 seat already named bit 10 (`SGIRE_W_BADMODE`); that cell is still open.\"*\n\n**It had.**  grok's PASS-2 answer says, in full:\n\n> *\"`tex xbit` does `sw` at the six `SITES` bases, then one TEX `ld`.  That fires\n> `SGIRE_W_TLBA_LEN` ... `SGIRE_W_LINB_LEN` and nothing else.  **`SGIRE_W_BADMODE` (1<<10),\n> `SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD` (1<<8), `SGIRE_W_DE_LEN` (1<<6), `SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP` (1<<9) are never set in\n> that session.**  `other decl` is a different process.  M10 only tested the WIDTH alias the row\n> actually populates.\"*\n\n**grok named FOUR bits on pass 2.  codex named ONE (bit 8).  I acted on codex's single cell,\nshipped a repair for it, and pass 3 then found the other three -- which one pass could have\nclosed.**\n\n*** AND I HAD THE FILE OPEN. ***  I read grok's pass-2 answer far enough to quote its resolution of\nglm's `controls live` objection -- which sits about twenty lines ABOVE the passage quoted here --\nand stopped at the premises table.  **This is not \"a seat answered and nobody looked\"; it is \"I\nlooked, took the part that confirmed my reading, and stopped before the part that would have cost\nme work.\"**  That is the harder version of the class and the one no checker can catch.\n\n**THE MEASURED COST:** one full extra pass -- a six-seat panel, a rebuild, a mutant table, and the\nclass-closure amendment invoked -- to reach a repair grok had specified a pass earlier.  It also\nmeans my pass-2 record said *\"repaired in-round\"* about a repair that closed one cell of five.\n\n> **THE HABIT THAT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT IT is the one this project already has and I did not apply:\n> READ THE BODY, NOT THE VERDICT LINE AND NOT THE FIRST SCREEN.**  The carrier states it for\n> verdict lines -- *\"a verdict line is written against the brief's framing and is the LEAST likely\n> part of an answer to hold the correction\"* -- and the same is true of an answer's opening\n> section, which addresses the brief's questions in the brief's order.  **grok's finding was in\n> its Q1 body, past the premises table.**\n\n*Recorded here rather than in a records row because the cost landed on THIS round, and because the\ninstance is specific: two seats answered the same question and I integrated the narrower answer\nwithout diffing it against the wider one.  When two seats answer one question, DIFF THEM.*\n\n*** A READING SEAT FOUND A LIVE BUG THAT A GREEN 46/46 RUN HAD JUST ENDORSED.  This is the most\nimportant thing in the round. ***\n\n`store()` is `store(A, addr, op=OP_SW_T0_A3, val=\"0x12345678\")`.  I wrote the six drawing-engine\nsetup calls as\n\n    cmds += store(A, DE_MODE_DST, MODE_LIN_B)\n\n**so MODE_LIN_B was assembled as an INSTRUCTION and the register received the default value.  All\nsix were wrong the same way.**  The row executed garbage opcodes and performed none of the register\nwrites its own comment described.\n\n*** AND IT SCORED 46/46. ***  `BASE_pass3b` came back CLEAN with the broken setup, because the row's\nonly oracle was the TEX decline count -- which garbage stores do not disturb.  **A row that passes\nwhile doing nothing it claims is exactly the vacuity the floor exists to prevent**, and no\nmeasurement could see it: the in-flight mutant run then scored `M_bit9` 46/46 ESCAPED, *which is\nwhat a no-op setup predicts*, and I would have blamed my choice of `PRIM_BAD` rather than my call.\n\n> **THE STRUCTURAL DEFECT, which is worth more than the typo: A CROSS-BIT ROW WHOSE ORACLE CANNOT\n> SEE ITS OWN PRECONDITIONS IS ONE TYPO AWAY FROM VACUOUS, PERMANENTLY.**  Counting only the TEX\n> decline cannot distinguish *\"ten latches fired and TEX still announced\"* from *\"the setup did\n> nothing and TEX announced\"*.  Both give 1.\n\n**REPAIRED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS:** the six calls now pass their value as `val`, and the row now\nASSERTS ITS OWN SETUP -- it counts how many of the eleven other announces actually appeared and\nwants all of them, so a silently-failing setup (a mistyped call, a moved register, a changed\nmessage) REDS the row instead of passing it.\n\n*** THE POINT ABOUT SEAT ROLES: THIS WAS FOUND BY READING, NOT BY MEASURING, AND NO MUTANT COULD\nHAVE FOUND IT. ***  Every mutant is scored against the same broken row, so the bug is invisible to\nthe entire kill table.  It took a seat reading the helper's signature against the call site.  *The\ncarrier's \"a reading seat buys the CLASS, a measuring seat buys the INSTANCE\" understates it here:\nsome defects are visible ONLY to reading.*\n\n**codex also returned EIGHT further record defects on the same brief**, several in text this round\nwrote: the contract header still says an 8-byte `ld` \"STILL ENDS THE HOST\" one paragraph below my\ncorrection saying it does not; two probe sites still say \"every read arm here still exit(1)s\" and\n\"An 8-byte read still dies\"; my own \"No row below reads exit status\" is false of the `other decl`\nrow, which uses `how`; and \"these four TEX rows\" have the store-first shape when only two do.\n**Those are being fixed; they are listed here so the count is honest rather than rounded down.**\n\n*The brief was framed to avoid both known codex triggers -- \"no mutant, no evasion, no guest\nsequence\" -- and it answered with zero refusals.  That framing is now a proven third form alongside\ndiff review and source analysis.*\n\n*** THE LATCH-COLLISION CLASS IS CLOSED, MEASURED, WITH THE BEFORE/AFTER PRESERVED. ***\n\n    CLASSVERIFY.txt    M_bit6 45/46 KILLED   M_bit7 45/46 KILLED\n                       M_bit9 46/46 ESCAPED  M_bit10 46/46 ESCAPED   <- disclosed at the time\n    CLASSVERIFY2       M_bit9 46/46 ESCAPED  -- against the BROKEN row, so this is evidence of\n                       the store bug and NOT of an open cell\n    CLASSVERIFY3       M_bit9 45/46 KILLED   M_bit10 45/46 KILLED    <- fixed, self-checking row\n\n**Every one of the eleven other used bits of `d->warned` is now covered**: bits 0-5 by `M10`, bit 8\nby `tex/unmap` + `unmap/tex`, bits 6, 7, 9 and 10 by the widened `tex xbit`.\n\n*** AND `BASE_fixed` 46/46 MEANS MORE THAN THE EARLIER 46/46s. ***  The row wants `1/11` -- the TEX\ndecline once AND all eleven other announces present -- so a clean run now PROVES the setup fired.\nThe earlier clean runs did not: they scored a row whose six drawing-engine stores passed their VALUE\nas the OPCODE.\n\n**THE DISCLOSURE WAS WITHDRAWN AND REPLACED BY COVERAGE**, which also dissolves the rule conflict I\nhad escalated for the adjudication: the class-closure amendment permits disclosing uncovered cells,\nkimi and grok argued an in-closure cell on the edited line is a breach regardless, and rather than\nhave that adjudicated I closed the cells.  *The \"settling fact\" I had written down as unknown was\none grep away, in a shipped probe that already drives the drawing engine.*\n\n*** A WRONG RECORD IN A MEASUREMENT LOG, CORRECTED IN PLACE. ***  `CLASSVERIFY3.txt` carried its\ndriver's template footer from the previous run, saying bits 9 and 10 *\"ESCAPE BY DESIGN and are\nDISCLOSED\"* -- **directly above two KILLED results**.  A generated artifact whose boilerplate\ncontradicts its own numbers is the wrong-record class arriving in the one place a future round\ntrusts most.  *The driver writes that text unconditionally; the fix here is the artifact, and\nteaching the driver to derive its footer from the results is filed rather than done -- it is a\ntooling change owing its own controls.*\n\n*** PASS 4 -- THE CLOSING PASS.  FIVE SEATS, 3-2 TO CLOSE, AND I AM NOT CLOSING ON THE COUNT. ***\n\n    grok      YES, the chain closes -- 11/11, verified against the LIVE SOURCE\n    glm       CLOSE, conditionally -- 11/11\n    minimax   CHAIN CLOSES -- 11/11\n    deepseek  NO\n    kimi      DO NOT CLOSE AS MEASURED\n\n**Count the arguments, not the seats.**  Two of the objections do not survive and one does.\n\n## *** THE ONE THAT SURVIVES, AND IT TURNS THIS ROUND'S OWN DOCTRINE ON ME ***\n\nkimi: *\"against the FINAL, self-checking row, exactly **2 of 11** aliasing mutants have a logged\nkill -- the other nine were measured against earlier versions of the very row that kills them, and\nthis round's own doctrine (value-as-opcode scoring 46/46 while doing nothing) forbids carrying kills\nacross a row edit by construction.\"*\n\n**That is unanswerable.**  I proved that a probe edit can silently void a measurement, wrote it up as\nthis round's central lesson, and then presented nine kills carried across exactly such an edit.  Its\nformulation is the right one: **\"close every cell in ONE diff\" deserves \"kill every cell on ONE\nprobe version.\"**  Running as `SWEEP11` -- eleven aliases, one frozen probe, one table.\n\n## THE TWO THAT DO NOT SURVIVE, and why -- stated so the dissent is not lost\n\n* **deepseek: \"the widened row tests only one ordering for bits 6,7,9,10.\"**  The row's TEX load is\n  LAST, so under an alias the setup fires the bit FIRST and the TEX announce is suppressed -> count\n  0 -> red.  The mirror (TEX suppressing a setup announce) would show as `fired == 10` -> also red.\n  **A single-bit alias is symmetric; catching it in one ordering kills it.**  The bit-8 pair needed\n  both orderings because those are two DIFFERENT sites, not one bit.\n* **deepseek's Q2 escape** posits a session ordered TEX-read-first.  **The row's order is fixed in\n  the probe and is not reachable by a mutant of the DEVICE**, which is what the table perturbs.\n* **deepseek/kimi: \"enumeration exclusivity is not checkable from the packet.\"**  Correct about the\n  packet, and now SETTLED from source, twice independently -- my own derivation and grok's:\n  twelve enumerators `1<<0`..`1<<11`, no duplicates, no thirteenth, twelve latch sites all on\n  `d->warned`, no raw `|= 1<<k`, and no second bitmask in `struct sgi_re_data`.\n\n## *** A DEFECT INSIDE ONE OF MY CORRECTIONS, FOUND BY TWO SEATS INDEPENDENTLY ***\n\ngrok and kimi both caught it.  My in-diff note said `tex rd lat1` and `tex xbit` *\"would not redden\non an ACCEPT\"*.  **False: both oracle the decline-message COUNT, which an ACCEPT drives to 0, so both\ngo red.**  What they cannot do is distinguish an ACCEPT from a DELETED MESSAGE -- only the value pair\ncan.  **The correction UNDERSTATED the detection surface**, which is the same wrong-record class this\nround has spent the evening fixing, arriving inside the fix for it.  Corrected; and grok's\ndisposition is adopted -- *\"fix the sentence when convenient; do not reopen the chain for it.\"*\n\n**kimi's Q3 residual, kept because it is right and unfixed:** the self-check tests `lit in t`, which\nis EMISSION not LATCHING, and the literals are unanchored substrings.  So the `/11` half proves the\nmessages appeared, not that any bit was set.  For this class that is harmless -- the kill signal is\nTEX's own count -- but the contract should say so rather than imply the row verifies latching.\n\n*** THE CHAIN CLOSES.  ELEVEN OF ELEVEN CELLS KILLED ON ONE PROBE VERSION, WITH THE DENOMINATOR.\nSWEEP11.txt. ***\n\n    SWEEP_BASE   46/46   the final self-checking probe, CLEAN\n    bits 0-7, 9, 10      45/46 each   red: `tex xbit`\n    bit 8                43/46        red: `tex xbit`, `tex/unmap`, `unmap/tex`\n    NOT KILLED: none\n\n**kimi's pass-4 objection is discharged BY MEASUREMENT, not by argument.**  It held that only 2 of\n11 aliases had a logged kill against the FINAL row, and that the other nine were carried across a\nrow edit -- which this round's own doctrine forbids, having proved that a probe edit can silently\nvoid a measurement.  **Every cell is now killed on one frozen probe, in one run.**\n\n*** BIT 8 IS THE MOST INFORMATIVE ROW IN THE TABLE, AND IT WAS NOT DESIGNED TO BE. ***  It reds\nTHREE rows rather than one, because `SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD` is exactly what the two pass-2 ordering rows\ntarget.  **So a single mutant demonstrates that the pass-2 repair and the pass-3 class closure are\nboth live and INDEPENDENT on the same probe version** -- which no separate table could have shown,\nbecause each was previously measured against its own instrument.\n\n**THE CLOSING PASS IS VALID BY ITS OWN RULE:** the diff was delivered and inlined, the roster is\nrecorded with its misses named (codex deliberately not fired -- the ask-again shape), and the zero\ncarries its denominator -- **eleven cells, enumerated from the twelve used bits of `d->warned`,\nderived from source twice independently rather than asserted.**\n\n*What closed it was not the 3-2 vote.  Three seats said close and two did not; both dissents were\nadjudicated on their arguments -- one refuted from source, one right and now measured away.\nCounting seats would have closed the chain an hour early on a denominator of 2 of 11.*\n\n*** THIS ROW'S CENSUS IS STALE BY A FACTOR OF TWO, AND THE BLOCKER IT IS BUILT AROUND IS ALREADY\nDISCHARGED.  Found by a codex ANALYSIS job 2026-09-08, VERIFIED here twice. ***\n\ncodex, on the current files: *\"The current files contradict the historical counts: I find SIX active\n`exit(1)` statements, and the probe's six former death rows have already been replaced.\"*\n\n**DERIVED, not transcribed -- and my FIRST attempt was wrong, which is worth recording because it is\nthe deriver rule landing on me again.**  A line-prefix test (*does this line start with a comment\nmarker?*) reported SEVEN, because `dev_sgi_re.c:259` is a CONTINUATION line of a block comment that\nbegins with neither `/*` nor `*`.  **I classified by a CORRELATE of being commented rather than by\nbeing commented.**  Stripping block and line comments properly, preserving line numbers:\n\n    ACTIVE exit( in dev_sgi_re.c:  6      :1463  :1471  :1547  :1552  :1563  :1685\n    (six further `exit(' texts are inside comments, several of them prose ABOUT this very row)\n\n**So the title's \"TWELVE exit(1) STATEMENTS and ELEVEN GUEST-REACHABLE KILLS\" is stale by 2x.**  The\nintervening rounds -- `#497`'s width de-fatalisation and `#499`'s work in the same file -- removed\nor commented six of them, and this row's headline was never re-derived.\n\n*** THE MORE IMPORTANT HALF: THE ORDERING OBLIGATION THIS ROW IS ORGANISED AROUND NO LONGER EXISTS. ***\n\nThe row says, in capitals: *\"FIXING sgirekills WILL TURN A GREEN GATE RED BY SUCCEEDING, SIX ROWS'\nWORTH ... it therefore inherits the oracle-correction ordering -- re-pin the six rows FIRST.\"*\n\n**MEASURED against the shipped probe:**\n\n    grep -c 'ld kill'                        regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py   ->  5\n    grep -n 'row(' ... | grep -i kill        ->  ZERO row() calls.  All five hits are COMMENTS,\n                                                 and both are written in the PAST TENSE:\n        :370  \"THE OLD ROWS were `row(nm + \\\" ld kill\\\", how + txt, \\\"exit1+txt\\\")' -- they asserted\"\n        :527  \"THE OLD ROW was `row(\\\"other kill\\\", how, \\\"exit1\\\")' -- DEATH-ONLY.\"\n\n**There are no death-only rows left to re-pin.**  A later round already replaced them -- the probe's\nown header says so at `:33`: *\"CRIME_RE_TEX became a latched decline -- so `<s> ld kill' x6 NO LONGER\nEXIST\"*.  So the oracle-correction ordering this row inherits is **already satisfied**, and the\nsequencing constraint that made it look expensive is gone.\n\n> **THE ROW IS THEREFORE CHEAPER AND SMALLER THAN IT READS, AND BOTH ERRORS POINT THE SAME WAY:\n> toward over-stating the work.**  A future round reading the headline plans for eleven kills and a\n> six-row re-pin; the real job is six kills with no re-pin owed.\n\n*** THIS IS THE CARRIER'S OWN COUNT RULE, EARNED RATHER THAN QUOTED: \"the rounds that consult the\nnumber are the rounds that change it.\"  `#497` and `#499` both edited this file, and each left this\nrow's headline one step further from the source. ***\n\n**WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, and codex said so itself:** it could give *\"a defensive census and\nguard-order analysis\"* but *\"can't map guest-controlled values or access sequences to\nhost-termination paths\"* from the three permitted files.  **So the count of ACTIVE exits is\nmeasured; the count of GUEST-REACHABLE ones is not**, and the title's \"eleven guest-reachable\" has no\ncurrent replacement figure.  The row should carry the six with their guards and leave reachability\nopen until it is measured, rather than swapping one unverified number for another.",
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\recordfix_20261013\adj_20261013\ledger_HEAD.json:31213:   "note": "*** A ONE-BYTE GUEST STORE TO AN MTE REGISTER WRITES THE REGISTER **AND SILENTLY ZEROES ITS NEIGHBOUR**.  Found by the fable5 seat under the standing license, verified here by reading the arms. ***  The handler is `if (len == 4) {...} else if (len != 4) {...} else {...}`.  The second arm does:      d->mte_reg[regnr+1] = idata >> 32;      d->mte_reg[regnr]   = idata;  and it is reached by EVERY width that is not 4 -- including 1 and 2.  For a one-byte store `idata` is a byte, so `idata >> 32` is **0**, and the store clobbers `mte_reg[regnr+1]` with zero.  No diagnostic, no decline: the device quietly loses a register the guest never addressed.  **THIS IS A MIS-SERVICE, NOT A KILL**, which is why it is med and not high: nothing ends the host, and the corruption is confined to emulated device state.  *** THE TRAP, AND IT IS THE REASON TO FILE THIS NOW RATHER THAN WHEN SOMEONE TOUCHES IT: the dead third arm is `fatal(\"[ sgi_mte: UNIMPLEMENTED read/write len %i ]\"); exit(1);` -- unreachable for every integer len, as `sgirekills` records.  It DOCUMENTS AN INTENT to reject unusual widths.  A naive future repair of the unreachable-arm defect -- reordering to `len==4 / len==8 / else` -- would RESURRECT THAT exit(1) AS A LIVE WIDTH KILL. ***  So when the unreachable arm is worked it must land as a DECLINE, never as a revived exit, and this row exists to say so before the edit rather than after.  *Two defects in one arm structure, pointing opposite ways: today it serves widths it should refuse, and the obvious tidy-up would make it kill on widths it currently serves.*  *** THE IMPLEMENTING SEAT RE-DISCOVERED THIS ARM INDEPENDENTLY ON 2026-08-30 AND PROPOSED IT AS A NEW ROW (`sgimtedeadwidth`).  FOLDED HERE, NOT FILED -- it is this row, found from the other direction. ***  It arrived via the SIBLING-PARITY check on a different round, where this row came from a pass-1 reading; that two routes reach the same arm is corroboration, not two defects.  **WHAT THE RE-DISCOVERY ADDS, and it is worth folding in:** the precise extent (`dev_sgi_re.c:1162-1178`, the unreachable `exit(1)` at `:1177`), and a COUNT this row did not carry -- **the file has EIGHT width-guard sites by text but only SEVEN LIVE ones**, because this arm's `else` can never be entered.  Verified here by opening the file: `if (len == 4) {...} else if (len != 4) {...} else { fatal(); exit(1); }`, and the second condition is the exact negation of the first.  *A duplicate row would have split one defect's evidence across two ids and let a future round close one while the other stayed open -- which is the failure the queue-id rule already names.  Adjudicated FOLD.*  *** CONFIRMED, AND THE MECHANISM IS BETTER THAN THE ROW SAID: THE WIDTH GUARD IS DEAD CODE. ***  Reaching the final `else` requires `len != 4` and `len == 4` at once, so it can never execute.  Every non-4 width falls into the 64-bit path, which does `mte_reg[regnr+1] = idata >> 32; mte_reg[regnr] = idata;` -- and a one-byte store has `idata < 256`, so the adjacent register is silently ZEROED.  **So it is not that the handler \"serves every width\"; it is that the guard written to refuse them cannot run.**  *** NOT A MEMORY-SAFETY DEFECT, and this was checked SPECIFICALLY because the same question was answered WRONG on `sgideoob` two days ago: *** the `&= ~CRIME_DE_START` mask caps `regnr` at 511 and `mte_reg` is `[DEV_SGI_MTE_LENGTH / 4]` = `[1024]`, so `regnr+1` reaches 512 with 512 slots of headroom.  Enumerated, not argued.  **CONSEQUENCE FOR RANKING, so no round over-claims: fixing this removes a data-corruption defect and a piece of dead code.  It does NOT remove a host kill, because that kill was never reachable.**  A round closing it and reporting \"one fewer host kill\" would be writing a wrong record.  *Its sibling `sgi_de` is shaped `len == 8 / len == 4 / else`, so THAT else IS reachable and `sgidewidth` is a real kill.  The two handlers differ in exactly the way that matters.*",
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\recordfix_20261013\adj_20261013\ledger_HEAD.json:35913:   "state_note": "\n\n=== MAIN-LOOP ROUND RECORDS, 2026-09-06 -- moved out of the per-seat `entries' column ===\nThese are the round's OWN records -- table results, repair descriptions, stage summaries -- not any seat's\nanswer.  They were written into `entries' with seat=`main', which is not one of the nine declared seats; the\ndashboard generator refused the file and a census confirmed the label is this session's invention rather than\nan established convention (every other entry in the ledger uses a declared seat).  The seat column is the\nper-seat record that check_boilerplate, check_seats_read and the scorecard all read, so a round note living\nthere is a round note wearing a seat's clothes -- the stamped-summary shape one level up.  Moved here verbatim;\nthe same material is in the CHANGELOG round block.\n\n  [2026-09-05 / assess] Witness built (rung 3) before any edit: HEAD 0/9 (every trap-like class stopped the machine), fix 9/9 (16:16 build).  Pre-table check PRETABLE_m88kexc.out written before table 1.\n\n  [2026-09-05 / research] [MAIN-LOOP STAGE SUMMARY, not a seat's own answer -- relabelled from an undeclared `panel' seat on 2026-09-06 when the dashboard generator refused it.  The per-seat cells for this pass are the entries that name their own seat; this one is the round's summary of the stage and is labelled as such, because a summary sitting in a seat column is the stamped-cell failure the boilerplate check exists to refuse.]  PASS 1 panel_20260905_143211: Opus (MEASURED: the one-site form halts at the tail default; supervisor tb1 naming 7 halts HEAD -- BOUNDS reachable), kimi, grok, deepseek, minimax answered; codex GPT-6 Astra REFUSED twice (content filter); glm BURNED (0-byte response, 117 KB thinking; mini packet answered); Fable WALLED (session limit) and re-fired at pass 2; agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED.  Preserved under regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/pass1/.\n\n  [2026-09-05 / review] [MAIN-LOOP STAGE SUMMARY, not a seat's own answer -- relabelled from an undeclared `panel' seat on 2026-09-06 when the dashboard generator refused it.  The per-seat cells for this pass are the entries that name their own seat; this one is the round's summary of the stage and is labelled as such, because a summary sitting in a seat column is the stamped-cell failure the boilerplate check exists to refuse.]  PASS 2 panel_20260905_212630: codex, kimi, grok, deepseek, minimax, Opus (measuring: four 10/10 escapes), Fable (review: delay-slot arm wrong; level observable) answered; glm BURNED then answered the mini packet ollama_glm_mini.txt; agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED.  Every seat: the DIFF is right; the DETECTOR needed repairs -- made (v3, 22 rows) and re-measured (table 3, 14 mutants: 13 killed, M_TRAPONLY equivalent).  Preserved under regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/pass2_panel_20260905_212630/, opus_p2.md, fable_p2.md.\n\n  [2026-09-05 / review] [MAIN-LOOP STAGE SUMMARY, not a seat's own answer -- relabelled from an undeclared `panel' seat on 2026-09-06 when the dashboard generator refused it.  The per-seat cells for this pass are the entries that name their own seat; this one is the round's summary of the stage and is labelled as such, because a summary sitting in a seat column is the stamped-cell failure the boilerplate check exists to refuse.]  ADJUDICATION PASS (F1..F24, D1..D3; brief_m88kexc_adj.md) fired ~23:10 to all nine (agy not fired): results land in the closure entry.\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] [MAIN-LOOP STAGE SUMMARY, not a seat's own answer -- relabelled from an undeclared `panel' seat on 2026-09-06 when the dashboard generator refused it.  The per-seat cells for this pass are the entries that name their own seat; this one is the round's summary of the stage and is labelled as such, because a summary sitting in a seat column is the stamped-cell failure the boilerplate check exists to refuse.]  ADJUDICATION PASS landed (pass3_panel_20260905_220902): codex, kimi, grok, deepseek, minimax answered; glm BURNED again; Opus and Fable Agent seats DIED on an Anthropic session limit (429) and are re-fired on pass 4; agy not fired.  grok/kimi CLOSE; codex/deepseek/minimax ONE MORE REPAIR -> v4: row indclr (IND never started clear -- the carry shape again), HALT classifier requires the stopped state + the named vector (+ pc == 0x40 for sfu1h), LEVEL contract narrowed to the ERROR/WARNING regression, sfu1h's channel restated (nested SFU1 halts at the fallback; with it off, re-routed to ERROR); mutants M_NOIND, M_NOSTOP; table 4 re-measures the carried-forward table-1 shapes.  Records: census (11 bypass / 10 delivered / vector 0 RESET-stop / 117 fallback), table-1 tally 11+2, M_TAILOFF equivalent, gate 2/22->3/22, PRETABLE correction appended, three phantom rows filed (m88kexctaildefault, m88ktbnd, m88kmxmkill).\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-5 REPAIR (probe v5, 29 fault rows, md5 96ab47a0 shipped / c83b1f38 frozen -- docstring-only diff): rows umisalign, uovf, errovf, errdiv0, errpriv, errunimpl; classifier keeps one `stopped` observation.  TABLE 5 (killtable5/, PRETABLE5 written 08:47 before it): BASE 29/29; M_OVFSUPONLY 28/29 (uovf), M_MISSUPONLY 28/29 (umisalign), M_SFRZMIS 25/29 (the four err* rows), M_SFRZNOOVF/NODIV0/NOPRIV/NOUNIMPL 28/29 each (one row each, as predicted), M_NOSFD1 1/29 (27 bad=psr + sfu1h STOP; resid50 the survivor).  HEAD 2/29 (probe_v5_head.out: usertb DELIVER, resid50 HALT); fix 29/29 (probe_v5_fix_final.out).  Comment corrections (Table 6-3 attribution; the tb1-naming-10 frozen case) propagated to all trees, rebuilt, two-part clearance CLEAN/FRESH x3 (rebuild_fix2.log).\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-6 REPAIR (one diff, two classes found by pass 5): probe v6 adds resid127 (the fallback window's FAR bound; table 6 = killtable6/: M_FALLBACK127 29/29 on v5 -> 29/30 on v6; M_FALLBACK51 28/29 on v5 (sfu1h saw it) -> 28/30; M_FALLBACKLE 28/29 -> 29/30 control); probe v7 adds trapfrozen4/5/6/8/9 with a harness field `handler_at` (trapfrozen8 plants its handler at the OVF entry so the frozen trap naming DIV0 is observable), class 10 disclosed as observationally identical; table 7 = killtable7/: M_SFRZTRAP4/5/6/8/9 30/30 on v6 -> 34/35 each (own row), M_SFRZTRAP 29/35.  HEAD with v7: 3/35 (usertb DELIVER, resid50 and resid127 HALT); fix 35/35.  PRETABLE6/7 written before their tables; both PRETABLE corrections were APPENDED to the same file after the table and refreshed its mtime -- stated in the record; corrections go to separate files from now on.  Gate pins 35/35 + resid127 HALT + six trapfrozen DELIVER.\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-6 REPAIR part 3 (probe v8, 36 rows, md5 e550e3fe): row usfu1 for kimi's measured M_FBSUPONLY (fallback conditioned on MODE: 35/35 on v7 -> 35/36 on v8); M_SFRZTRAP4 re-measured 35/36.  HEAD with v8: 4/36; fix 36/36.  Gate pins 36/36 + usfu1 HALT.  Pass 6 fired ~10:30 to the eight scriptable transports (Opus/Fable walled).\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-7 REPAIR (probe v9+v10, 41 rows, md5 ee2c78c2 = shipped): rows trapfrozen10, resid11, resid50ind, resid50c, trapfrozen128 and the refatal channel (the re-route's diagnostic pinned present on err* rows, absent on trapfrozen* rows).  TABLE 10 (killtable10/, PRETABLE10 before it, untouched; table 9 folded): M_ERRORFRZOFF, M_SFRZTRAP10, M_FB_LO, M_FBIND, M_FBNOC, M_SFRZTRAPUSER each 36/36 on the frozen v8 (the escapes measured) and 40/41 on v10, killed by exactly the predicted row (M_SFRZTRAP10 via bad=refatal on trapfrozen10).  BASE 41/41; HEAD 7/41 (usertb DELIVER; six HALT rows); fix 41/41.  Also repaired this pass: three gate check lines with a literal backslash-n between arguments (codex, pass 6) -- would have failed on correct output.  Gate pins 41/41 + the seven HALT rows + eight frozen-trap rows + refatal absent.\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-7 REPAIR (probe v10 -> v14, 46 fault rows; shipped v15 md5 eb11ab7c, identical to frozen v14 outside the module docstring, AST-compared).  Rows added: trapfrozen450 / trapfrozen496 (codex: switch 1's last two deliverable labels), trapfrozen511 (kimi: the exemption's far edge, tb1's 9-bit field), errind / errc (grok: the re-route x {IND, C} -- the re-route predicate reads PSR, so a PSR-bit conjunct there is clause (d), unlike the switch-1 arms).  TABLE 11 (killtable11/, PRETABLE11 11:49 + addendum 2 11:55 + addendum 3 12:16, all before the 12:17 start; frozen v14 md5 99eb9c00; each mutant ALSO scored on the frozen v10): M_SFRZTRAP450 41/41 -> 45/46; M_SFRZTRAP496 41/41 -> 45/46; M_SFRZTRAPHI 41/41 -> 43/46; M_ERRORFRZON 40/41 -> 45/46 (kimi's sweep CLAIM refuted by measurement, as the existing errortb/bounds rows predicted); M_MISNOC 41/41 -> 45/46 (errc HALT: the disclosed {MISALIGNED} x {C} representative is killed as a side effect of the re-route rows -- disclosure narrowed in v15's docstring); M_RRIND 41/41 -> 45/46 (errind); M_RRNOC 41/41 -> 45/46 (errc).  Six measured sweeps of v10, each killed by the row the PRETABLE named.  BASE 46/46 before and after; clone cmp-clean, sentinel clear.  A MIS-CUT ROW was caught by the driver's own BASE gate (killtable11_attempt1_v13/): v13's errc wanted PSR 0x8000000b and exception entry leaves C alone, so BASE read 45/46 bad=psr before any mutant and the driver aborted -- PSR_WANT repaired in the instrument only (v14), no product code, no mutant re-aimed.  HEAD 7/46 (probe_v14_head.out, re-run on the HEAD binary with the final instrument); fix 46/46 (probe_v14_fix_final.out).  Gate pins 46/46, err(...|ind|c) 6, trapfrozen(...|511)? 11.  Pass 8 packet drafted (brief_m88kexc_p8.DRAFT.md).\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS 8 FIRED (_scratchpad/panel_20260906_150800_m88k_p8/, brief = the FROZEN artifact copy regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/brief_m88kexc_p8.md, per the new template section on citing frozen copies).  Seat state MEASURED at launch, not assumed: opus5 ALIVE and fired first (it is the only measuring-seat review this round gets before it commits, since fable5 is walled); codex, kimi and grok fired and running -- the brief is 31,166 b, above the 30,000 argv cap, so codex and kimi got the POINTER form and grok is immune (--prompt-file).  *** THE THREE OLLAMA CLOUD SEATS ARE DOWN ON AN ACCOUNT-LEVEL QUOTA, NOT A TRANSIENT RATE LIMIT, AND THE SEAT FILES COULD NOT SAY SO. ***  Two firings forty minutes apart returned `OLLAMA_SEAT_ERROR: <HTTPError 429: 'Too Many Requests'>` and nothing else, which is indistinguishable from a five-minute wait; a direct POST to the same endpoint carried the reason in the response BODY -- a WEEKLY account usage limit.  Owner states it resets in about four hours from ~15:10 (so ~19:10).  panel_ollama.py now reads the HTTPError's body and appends it, CONTROLLED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS on the live wall: the fixed form prints the weekly-limit text, the pre-fix form from HEAD prints the bare status.  Pass 8's Ollama third fires when the quota resets; until then the stage is 6/9 and the three are recorded DOWN by name, never as agreement.  agy remains DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (owner ruling, reserved for measurement).\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] **TABLE 12 -- THE DISCRIMINATING TABLE.  THE PASS-8 PANEL SPLIT IS SETTLED BY MEASUREMENT, AND codex AND grok ARE RIGHT.**  (regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/killtable12/ with its driver log; PRETABLE12 written 15:58:33 and the first BASE at 16:00:48, so the order proof is the file's own mtime; the frozen instrument is the SHIPPED probe v15, md5 eb11ab7c -- which also closes the records gap kimi named, since the gate runs the shipped file and earlier tables measured a frozen copy of an older revision.)  | mutant | on frozen v10 | on shipped v15 | red rows |  M_RRTRAPIND `if (!is_trap || !(PSR & IND))` | 41/41 | **46/46 SWEEP** | none |  M_RRTRAPC `if (!is_trap || (PSR & C))` | 41/41 | **46/46 SWEEP** | none |  CTL_RRTRAPINV `if (!is_trap || (PSR & IND))` -- THE POLARITY TWIN | 33/41 | **35/46** | ELEVEN: trapfrozen, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 450, 496, 511, 128 all STOP, and trapfrozen10 BADREGS bad=refatal |  **THE CONTROL IS WHAT MAKES THE SWEEPS MEAN SOMETHING.**  It is the same edit with one token changed -- the sense of the bit test -- and it reddens exactly the eleven frozen-trap rows, on both channels the PRETABLE named (the entry pc moves to 0x50, so the breakpoint is never reached; and trapfrozen10 additionally shows the re-route's diagnostic where REFATAL_EXPECT says it must not appear).  So the find-text landed, the probe CAN see this site, and the two 46/46 results are genuine blindness rather than a mis-built mutant -- the three explanations a non-dying mutant has, reduced to one.  BASE 46/46 before and 46/46 after; HEAD 7/46 with the same instrument; the rebuilt build/ 46/46; clone cmp-clean against SEC with no sentinel.  All five PRETABLE12 predictions held, including the control's.  **WHAT IT SETTLES.**  kimi's `killable-and-undisclosed survivors: ZERO' is refuted at the RE-ROUTE as it already was at the switch-1 arms -- and the reason is visible in kimi's own enumeration, which wrote the re-route family as `{label trap x 11}': the eleven rows cover eleven LABELS in ONE status state, and every one of them arrives with PSR 0x8000000b, so no row varies the trap side's status at all.  Counting labels is not covering the axis.  **WHAT IS NOW OWED.**  Three consecutive passes have found this class at three sites -- the fallback predicate (pass 6), the re-route's non-trap side (pass 7), and now the re-route's TRAP side plus the switch-1 arms (pass 8, measured here and by opus5) -- so the class-closure amendment applies and the repair must close the enumerated space in ONE diff rather than buy another cell.  opus5's five rows close the arms; these two cells close the re-route's trap side.  L2, in-round.\n\n*** THE PASS-5 PANEL WAS CITED AT A DURABLE PATH THAT DID NOT EXIST; THE EVIDENCE WAS SITTING IN\n`_scratchpad/`, WHICH IS NEVER COMMITTED.  Found and fixed 2026-09-08. ***\n\nFive seat entries cite `regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/pass5_panel_20260906_090810/<seat>.txt`.\n**That directory did not exist.**  The panel was at `_scratchpad/panel_20260906_090810/` -- the\nvolatile path -- so the citations were unverifiable and the answers were one cleanup from gone.\n\n**COPIED to the cited path**, and all five now resolve:\n\n    codex.txt          1,299,047 b      grok.txt              5,213 b\n    ollama_glm.txt         3,704 b      ollama_minimax.txt    7,559 b\n    ollama_deepseek.txt    1,297 b      SEATS.txt               829 b\n\n*** AND A SIXTH SEAT ANSWERED THAT THE LEDGER NEVER CITED: `kimi.txt`, 312,574 BYTES. ***  It is\nrecorded in no entry on this row.  **That is an UNREAD REVIEW -- not a seat failure and not\nagreement -- and this project has found real defects in one four times.**  Preserved with the rest;\n**reading it is an outstanding obligation on this row**, not discharged here.\n\n**A SIZE NOTE, recorded rather than acted on unilaterally: `codex.txt` is 1.3 MB.**  The carrier\nrecords that a codex research run here has exceeded 1 MB, and `adjudicate.sh` takes only the TAIL\nfor that reason.  **I did NOT truncate it**: the ledger cites this file as a seat's evidence, and\ntruncating cited evidence to save space converts a verifiable citation into a modified artifact --\nwhich is worse than a large file.  If the repository size matters more than the citation, that is\nan owner decision and this note is where it should start.\n\n> **THE GENERAL FINDING, which is why this was worth an audit rather than a fix: the volatile-path\n> rule was OBEYED for the round's own logs and MISSED for its panel.**  A round that carefully\n> copies its kill-table output to `regress/artifacts/` can still leave the panel that reviewed it in\n> `_scratchpad/`, because the panel feels like process rather than evidence.  **It is evidence: the\n> ledger cites it by path.**\n\n*Found by an EXISTENCE audit over every artifact path in the ledger's witness and `ev` fields --\n71 witness citations, all resolving, and 181 `ev` paths of which these five were the only misses.\nAn existence check cannot false-positive the way a count or a proximity grep can, which is why it is\nworth running and the stale-count audit was declined.*\n\n*** THE AUDIT'S FIRST RUN REPORTED EIGHT ADDITIONAL MISSES AND EVERY ONE WAS ITS OWN BUG. ***  Bare\nfilenames (`check_witness.py`, `gate_arm.sh`, `precommit_check.sh` ...) were joined to the SEC root\nwhen they live in `tools/pipeline/` and `regress/`, and one citation carried a `GXEMUL-SEC/` prefix\nthat the join then doubled.  **With the resolver fixed: 71 of 71.**  *Recorded because reporting\nthose eight as ledger defects would have been exactly the wrong-apparatus failure this project keeps\ncatching -- and the tell was that they were all HARNESS files, which is a suspicious population.*",
