148:COMPARE ignores: see "Beyond the effects".)  (4) Equivalent edits, THIRTEEN, each measured at the full score
149:(table5/TABLE.out, table6/TABLE.out): storing `(uint32_t) idata`, `(int) idata` or `idata & 7` (only 0..7 reach that
150:store, and each compiles to the fix's own binary), reordering the struct's fields, masking the PORT read with 7 (the
151:selector is always 0..7), a PORT read that skips its load at selector 0 (odata starts at 0), a latch set to any
152:nonzero value in place of 1, the bound written `idata > 7`, `(idata & ~7ULL) != 0` or with the literal 8 (each equal
153:to `idata >= DEV_FBCTRL_NPORTS` on every uint64_t), the direction tested as `writeflag != MEM_WRITE` (the arm receives
154:only MEM_READ and MEM_WRITE), the latch tested as `warned_port != 1` (it holds only 0 or 1), and the announce
155:additionally requiring a nonzero idata (a refused value is never 0).  (5) `printf` in place of `fatal()`, or the
156:announce without its trailing newline: indistinguishable in THIS invocation's output.  (6) Narrowing the DATA path's
157:widths, and the device's OTHER two process-ending calls -- fbctrl_command()'s default (any command other than 0-2,
158:including the documented but unimplemented FILL/COPY) and the access function's default (any offset other than
159:0x00/0x10): filed as `fbctrlcmdkill` and `fbctrloffkill`.  (7) Edits to this instrument's own logic are reviewed, not
160:rowed.  (8) The data cells' VALUES are not an axis the rows enumerate.  An edit conditioned on a cell's value
161:(`port[COLOR_B] == 0`, `port[Y1] != 0`) is caught only where a row's refusal or read holds a triggering state, and the
162:states held are: every cell 0 (F, W, X, Z, B), port 5 at 0x2a (P, K, R), X1 at 123 with COMMAND 2 (V's first refusal),
163:ports 1-7 at 0x11*p with COMMAND 2 (V's later refusals and the PORT read right after them), and COMMAND 1 (Y).  The
164:cells-alone effect is claimed only as listed: no cell is read back after an `sh` refusal or after a refusal at
165:previous selector 0, 2, 3, 4 or 6, and after a refusal at previous 5 the only cells read back are port 5's under
166:COMMAND 0 (K) and COMMAND's under COMMAND 1 (Y, its second refusal only).
167:LIMITS OF A ROW.  Session P re-selects PORT=5 before each refusal, so a refused selector cannot carry over into the
168:next row WHEN THE LEGAL SELECTION LANDS; an edit that also refuses legal selections defeats the re-select, and later
169:rows then inherit its value (M_acclatch).  Every read-back is taken after its session ends, so a session that dies
170:part-way blanks the rows it had already passed (M_acc4: session A died at step 5 and every accept row read `-`).  And
171:session A's "accept selector 0 written by sw" cannot see an IGNORED store -- the selector is already 0 at power-on --
172:so the cell "0 by sw is accepted" is held by W's row after PORT=5, not by A's.  Session B reads PORT once per width,
173:after all of that width's refusals: a passing row says no single-bit value of that width got through; a failing row
174:prints the LAST value that got through, which for the compare-narrowing family names the bit for k <= 31 (2^k; INT_MIN
175:prints 0xffffffff80000000) and reads 0 for bits 32-63, where it says only that some value got through.  B's "keeps 5"
176:is verified against the stored-int oracle only: a refusal that WRITES 5 reads as a keep (NOT CLAIMED (3)).  B depends
177:on the emulated `dsll` executing -- the encoding is pinned and the disassembly control checks the word, not the
178:doubling; table 6's width-differentiated reds (bit 9 red at sh/sw/sd and never sb, bit 16 and up never sh, bit 32 and
179:up never sw) are the evidence each 2^k reached the compare.  After a refusal, the DATA accesses made without a fresh
180:selector are K's read, K's write and R's write; R's DATA read re-selects port 5 first.  Every refusal row rests on
181:`fatal()` printing and returning on this path (src/core/debugmsg.c's `fatal()`: no early-out and no exit) -- twelve
182:refusals in P and 108 in B.
183:
184:Every stepped word is compared with the emulator's own step line (the hand-assembled-encoding trap).
185:Output: one row per check, then FBCTRLP_CONTROL=OK|FAILED, FBCTRLP_RESULT=n/t, FBCTRLP_DONE.  The exit status is 0
186:only when the controls are OK and every row is green: a red row is a nonzero exit, not only a crash.
